1 NOTE The Barony of Saint-Martin-le-Gaillard was important, because it consisted of the castle and 14 feudal benefices; the barons had, among other feudal privileges, what is known as the right of "cuissage" or first night (they could s
2 CONC pend the wedding night with any bride in their benefice while the groom waited his turn!)
2 CONT
2 CONT Although various authors list the Lord of Bettencourt and his firstborn son as heirs of the barony and castle of Saint-Martin, Jean de St. Martin left his undivided family estate to his three daughters, Isabeau, Jeanne, and Aude. 
2 CONC Directly afterwards, they partitioned it.
