1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_IV_d%27Avaugour
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henri IV d'Avaugour ''' est né vers 1280 et mort le 1er février 1334, Seigneur d'Avaugour en Goëllo, et baron de Mayenne.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/brittcope.htm#HenriIVAvaugourdied1331
2 CONT
2 CONT a)      '''   HENRI [IV] d'Avaugour ''' (-Avignon [2] Feb 1331, bur Dinan).  Baron d'Avaugour, de Mayenne et de Goello.  The Chronicon Britannicum records the death “eundo de Parisius apud Avenionem circa Purificationem B. M. V.” 
2 CONC 1331 of “Henricus d’Avaugour filius Alani de Dinanno [omits one generation] qui fuit filius Henrici filii Alani fundatoris Belliportus…quintus post comitem Alanum” and his burial “apud Fratres Minores de Dinanno”, adding that he h
2 CONC ad lived about 54 years[628].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (contract 1306) JEANNE d’Harcourt dame de Morgon, daughter of JEAN [II] Seigneur d’Harcourt & his [second] wife Jeanne Vicomtesse de Châtellerault (-after 1346).  Philippe IV King of France, by letters dated 1306, notified
2 CONC  the marriage contract at Pontoise between “Henry d’Avaugour Sgr de Mayenne et de Goëlo” and “damoiselle Jne de Harecourt fille de...chev. Jn. de Harcourt”[629].  “Jne de Harrecourt veuve de Mgr Henry d’Avaugour sire d’Avaugour” r
2 CONC enounced her claims over the patronage of Notre-Dame d’Escouché by charter dated mid-Feb 1339[630].  Letters dated 5 Aug 1400 record a dispute between “Alanum vicecomitem de Rohano dominum de Leonia” and “Ioannem de Britannia comi
2 CONC tem Pentheuriæ et vicecomitem Lemovicensem” relating to the rights of “defunctæ Margaretæ de Alvagaro quondam aviæ suæ” in the succession of “defunctorum Henrici quondam domini de Alvagaro necnon Ioannæ de Haricuria eius uxoris pa
2 CONC tris et matris eiusdem Margaretæ et Blanchæ de Alvagaro ac Ysabellis de Alvagaro”[631]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Henri [IV] & his wife had four children: 
