1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from MEDLANDS 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         ROBERT [V] d’Estouteville (-after 1282 [maybe killed in battle Courtrai 11 Jul 1302]).  “Johannes de Estotevilla miles dominus de Boscheto et Agnes uxor mea” renounced rights over the priory of Lancé in favour of Marmou
2 CONC tier, with the consent of “Robertus primogenitus noster, Radulphus et Astulphus filii mei”, by charter dated 1249[361].  Seigneur d’Estouteville et de Vallemont.  Morandière quotes (in translation) a charter dated Jun 1272 under w
2 CONC hich "Agnes [d’Estouteville] au temps de mon veuvage" donated property to Valmont, with the consent of "domini Roberti, domini Willelmi, et domini Estoldi, et Magistri Johannis de Estoutevilla canonici Rothomagensis, filiorum meor
2 CONC um", but does not cite the corresponding primary source[362].  [The Chronique Artésienne records “mesires d’Estoutevile” among those killed at the battle of Courtrai 11 Jul 1302[363].  It is not certain to whom this entry refers, 
2 CONC possibly Robert [V] given that his son Robert [VI] is named after 1302.]  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ALIX Bertrand, daughter of [ROBERT [V] Bertrand Baron de Briquebecq & his wife Alix de Tancarville.  Père Anselme records her parentage (calling her mother “Alix de Néelle”) and marriage without citing any source on which this i
2 CONC nformation is based[364].  Assuming her family origin is correct, the chronology suggests that she was the daughter of Robert [V] Bertrand.  Robert [V] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          ROBERT [VI] d’Estouteville (-after 1330).  Seigneur d’Estouteville et de Vallemont.  -         see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ii)         ESTOUT d’Estouteville (-after 1316).  Seigneur de Torcy.  Morandière records that he was named in registers dated 1302, 1303 (La Chambre des Comptes) and 1316 (Archbishopric of Rouen)[365].  m ALIX de Meulan, daught
2 CONC er of [AMAURY [V] de Meulan Seigneur de la Queue-en-Brie & his wife ---] (-after 1327).  Père Anselme records her marriage, saying that Alix was the daughter of Amaury [III] de Meulan and his wife Marguerite de Neufbourg[366].  Th
2 CONC at suggestion appears unlikely to be correct, considering the chronology of the Estouteville family.  Maybe Alix was the daughter of Amaury [IV].  Morandière records  that Alix was still living in 1327 (no source cited)[367].  -  
2 CONC        SEIGNEURS de TORCY[368].  '''
