2 CONT Veuf en premières noces de Mahaut de Mehun, dont Guillaume (1226-1280).
2 CONT  Seigneur de Champignelles en Puisaye, de Châteaurenard (en partie), de Charny en Gâtinais, de Conches par donation de Philippe-Auguste, de Nonancourt en Normandie, de Mehun-sur-Yères, de Selles, de Chantecoq, de Cloyes, de Baille
2 CONC t de Vermanton, de Villeneuve-les-Genest en Puisaye, de Bléneau et de Malicorne. Participe à la croisade contre les Albigeois en 1210 et 1224. Combat contre les Anglais en 1217 et commande une flotte de secours pour délivrer Louis
2 CONC  de France, fils de Philippe-Auguste, assiégé dans Londres, mais il est fait prisonnier. Il fait la guerre en 1226, pour le roi Louis VII le jeune durant sa minorité contre Thibault VI, comte de Champagne. En 1237 fonde l'abbaye d
2 CONC e Beauvoir près de Mehun en Berry pour les religieux cisterciens. Chevalier croisé en 1239.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT  - personne: Encyclopédie Généalogique des Maisons Souveraines du Monde
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peter I of Courtenay (September 1126 – 10 April 1183) was the youngest son of Louis VI of France and his second queen consort, Adélaide de Maurienne. He was the father of the Latin Emperor Peter II of Courtenay.  Peter was born in
2 CONC  France and died in Palestine. He married Elizabeth de Courtenay (1127 –14 September 1205), the daughter of Renauld de Courtenay and Hawise du Donjon. He is buried in a tomb in the floor of Exeter Cathedral, next to Elizabeth. The
2 CONC  couple had ten children:
2 CONT  Phillip (1153 – before 1186)
2 CONT  Peter, Latin Emperor of Constantinople (c. 1155 to 1218)
2 CONT  Unnamed daughter (c. 1156 – ?)
2 CONT  Alice (died 12 February 1218), married Count Aymer of Angoulême
2 CONT  Eustachia (1162–1235), married William of Brienne, son of Erard II of Brienne and of Agnès of Montfaucon
2 CONT  Clémence (1164 – ?)
2 CONT  Robert, Seigneur of Champignelles (1166–1239)
2 CONT  William, Seigneur of Tanlay (1168 – before 1248)
2 CONT  Isabella (1169 – after 1194)
2 CONT  Constance (after 1170–1231)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peter I, Prince of France and Lord of Courtenay, was a mounted Crusader who fought and survived the Battle of Belvoir Castle in 1182, but the next year he died in battle in Palestine in the return attack of Saladin's forces at the
2 CONC  Battle of Al-Fule in 1183. There were a total of 1300 mounted Crusaders on horseback and 15,000 foot soldiers who participated. The battle was between the 1st and 2nd Crusades.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In my home family tree Peter is married to Elizabeth de Gatesden 1127-1205 and on my tree I am told they are my 23rd great grandparents.   Elizabeth's parents are Renaud who married Hawise de Courcy of Okehampton, Devonshire my 24
2 CONC th GGPs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter Alice Adelaide de Courteney married Aylmer (Count of Angouleme) De. Valence born 1160 ANGOULEME AQUITAINE FRANCE  my 22nd GGPs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anyone else got these facts?  I have Courteney Descended from the Capet line. Aylmer was a Crusader. Their daughter Isabella MARRIED KING JOHN LACKLAND OF ENGLAND
2 CONT
2 CONT This connection appears correct to me, but perhaps some of you cousins might like to check me out?
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The Capetian lineage of Pierre, de France, Seigneur de Courtenay:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hugh "Magnus" of Paris, count of Paris, duke of the Franks [First Capet]
2 CONT     Hugues Capet, roi des Francs
2 CONT     Robert II Capet, "the Pious" king of the Franks
2 CONT     Henry I, king of France
2 CONT     Philip Capet; Philip I, king of France
2 CONT     Louis VI the Fat, king of France
2 CONT     Pierre, de France, Seigneur de Courtenay
