2 CONT Alice of Courtenay, Countess of Angoulême (1160- 12 February 1218)[1] was a French noblewoman of the House of Courtenay. Her father was Peter of Courtenay and her brother was Peter II of Courtenay, Latin Emperor of Constantinople.
2 CONC  Alice married twice; by her second husband, Aymer Taillefer, Count of Angoulême, she was the mother of Isabella of Angoulême, who was Queen consort of England, as the wife of King John.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is also known as Alix de Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice was born in 1160, the second eldest daughter and one of the ten children of Peter I of Courtenay and Elisabeth of Courtenay. Her family was one of the most illustrious in France; and her paternal grandparents were King Louis
2 CONC  VI of France and Adélaide de Maurienne. Her eldest brother Peter became the Latin Emperor of Constantinople in 1216; and she had an additional three brothers and five sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1178, she married her first husband, Guillaume I, Count of Joigny. The marriage did not produce any children, and they were divorced in 1186. A charter dated 1180 records that Count Guillaume, with Alice's consent, donated prop
2 CONC erty to Pontigny Abbey.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice married her second husband, Aymer Taillefer in 1186, the same year he succeeded his father, William IV as Count of Angoulême. Sometime in 1188, Alice gave birth to her only child:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (1188- 31 May 1246), married firstly 24 August 1200 King John of England, by whom she had five children; in spring 1220, she married secondly, Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, by whom she had another n
2 CONC ine children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice's husband died on 16 June 1202. Their only child, Isabella succeeded him as suo jure Countess of Angoulême. By this time, Isabella was already Queen of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT She herself died on 12 February 1218 at the age of 58. Her daughter would also die at the age of 58 in 1246.
