2 NOTE Sir Gilbert de Hambledon, (ie Hamilton).  Edmunds, in "Names and Places," says that Hamil- ton, a surname, is "From Hambledon, the seat of Sir Gilbert de Hambledon, the founder of the Scotch Ducal family of Hamilton, in the 13th c
3 CONC entury." In " PATRONYMICA Britannica," is mentioned: " Hambledon — Parishes, etc., in Rutland, Lancaster, Buckingham and York." "Hamilton — A corruption of Hambledon, a manor in Buckingham- shire." " William de Hambledon, a younge
3 CONC r son of Robert de Beaumont, third Earl of Leicester, is said to have gone, about the year 12 15, into Scotland, where he was well received by Alexander II. From him sprung all the noble and other Scottish lines of Hamiltons.     
