2 CONT Inherited estates upon death of his older brother, he became Count of Evreux and Seigneur of Montfort. Being a vassal both of the King of France and of the King of England, his position was embarrassing when the two were at war in
2 CONC  1159. He adhered to England and handed over his castles of Rochefort, Montfort, and Epernon to Henry II, which forced Louis to make a truce by cutting his communications between Paris, Orleans, and Etampes. In 1173 Simon joined t
2 CONC he revolt of the 'young king' but was captured when the Count of Flanders took the castle of Aumale. In 1177 he attested the treaty of Ivry and was with Henry II at Verneuil. (Complete Peerage, VII, Appendix D, p. 715)
