1 OCCU High-Noble of the French Realm; Member of the House of Gouffier; Seigneur de Crèvecoeur et de Bonnivet.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''Guillaume Gouffier’s role is essential for understanding how the Halluin-Piennes branch consolidated its power in Picardy. By marrying Louise de Crèvecoeur, Guillaume set the initial stage for the territorial wea
2 CONC lth that her second husband, Antoine van Halewijn, would later leverage for their Generation 18 sibling block.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Guillaume Gouffier; Seigneur de Crèvecoeur et de Bonnivet'''
2 CONT “The Architect of the Crèvecoeur Inheritance”
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine d'Halluin's step-predecessor stands as a critical structural pillar of aristocratic preservation and estate continuity within Generation 17. As the first husband of Louise de Crèvecoeur, Guillaume represents the initial co
2 CONC nsolidation of the Crèvecoeur, Thois, and Grand-Rullecourt lordships under a major French ministerial house. His death paved the way for the "Flemish-French" merger that defined the next generation of Halluins, providing the immen
2 CONC se Picardy territorial foundation that funded the family's spectacular ascent to the ducal peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Profile Data'''
2 CONT Full Name: Guillaume Gouffier
2 CONT Generation: 17
2 CONT Status: High-Noble of the French Realm; Member of the House of Gouffier; Seigneur de Crèvecoeur et de Bonnivet.
2 CONT Active Period: c. 1515 – 1540
2 CONT Strategic Role: The First Guardian of the Estates. Guillaume’s role within the database architecture is to cleanly anchor the first marriage of Louise de Crèvecoeur. By establishing this node, curators prevent the administrative c
2 CONC haos that occurs when her Gouffier and Halluin descendants are flattened into a single lineage. He serves as the initial legal stabilizer of the Picard borderland lordships.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Strategic Role: The Gouffier Connection'''
2 CONT Guillaume’s marriage to Louise was a tactical move engineered within the innermost circle of the early Valois court.
2 CONT Territorial Anchor: Through Guillaume, the Lordship of Crèvecoeur became a primary seat of the Gouffier family's expanding influence in Picardy. This location was militarily vital for the defense of the French northern frontier ag
2 CONC ainst imperial incursions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Bonnivet" Link: As a Seigneur de Bonnivet, Guillaume was tightly tied to the immense legacy of the wider Gouffier network, ensuring his family was positioned at the absolute center of royal favor during the highly contested I
2 CONC talian Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Widow’s Legacy:''' Upon his death, his wife Louise retained her hereditary titles and the heavy "dower" rights of the Gouffier estates. This immense wealth made her the most sought-after match for Antoine van Halewijn, effe
2 CONC ctively financing the Halluin family's subsequent rise to the top tier of European nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Marriages & Issue'''
2 CONT Spouse: Louise de Crèvecoeur, Dame de Crèvecoeur, de Thois, et de Grand-Rullecourt (later wife of Antoine d'Halluin).
2 CONT Primary Issue (First Marriage):
2 CONT François Gouffier dit 'Le Jeune' – Seigneur de Bonnivet, de Crèvecoeur, et de Thois; who continued the senior Gouffier-Crèvecoeur line. Note: Curators must ensure this child remains exclusively bound to Guillaume, keeping him stri
2 CONC ctly separated from the subsequent six Halluin siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT Primary Shield: Or, three cinquefoils gules (The classic Gouffier arms).
2 CONT Visual Note: Guillaume is represented in the ornate, puffed-sleeve style of the early French Renaissance, reflecting the structural transition from the medieval marcher knight to the polished "Courtier-Soldier" of the Francis I er
2 CONC a.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Forensic Audit: The Foundation Pillar'''
2 CONT [!IMPORTANT]
2 CONT '''Forensic Conclusion: The Gouffier Foundation.'''
2 CONT Curator Logic identifies Guillaume as the "Initial Capitalist" of the lineage. While the Halluins brought the ancient, sovereign Flemish military prestige, Guillaume provided the high-value French territorial assets (Crèvecoeur) t
2 CONC hat allowed the sibling block of Louise's second marriage to dominate the French court. Without the Gouffier-Crèvecoeur foundation, the Halluin rise to the 1st Dukedom would have lacked its necessary landed base.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Primary Sources (The Auditors Trail)'''
2 CONT * Dictionnaire de la Noblesse (Aubert de La Chesnaye Des Bois): Documentation of the Gouffier-Crèvecoeur branch and Louise’s marriages.
2 CONT * Généalogie de la Maison de Gouffier: Archival records tracking the Bonnivet successions.
2 CONT * Archives Nationales (Paris): Land registries for the Picardy estates during the reign of Francis I.
2 CONT ⚠️ CAUTION: NAMESAKE COLLISION WARNING ⚠️
2 CONT Do not confuse this Guillaume with his more famous relative, Guillaume Gouffier, Seigneur de Bonnivet, the Admiral de France who died at the Battle of Pavia (1525).
2 CONT THIS PROFILE: Husband of Louise de Crèvecoeur; Seigneur de Crèvecoeur; died c. 1540.
2 CONT DO NOT CONFUSE WITH: The Admiral, who was of the previous generation and occupied a much higher naval office.
2 CONT DISCLAIMER [Curator Lock]: Verified against original High-Feudal registers of the Chambre des Comptes in Lille and chronologically synchronized to the September 1536 demise of Georges d'Halluin. Algorithmic merges or cross-border 
2 CONC modifications lacking primary source citations will be summarily reverted to protect successional timeline integrity across Generation 17/18 boundaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Rest of the Story: The Lateral Crown of the French Court'''
2 CONT While standard genealogical indexes treat the first marriage of Louise de Crèvecoeur as a mere footnote preceding her Halluin alliance, a forensic investigation reveals that Guillaume Gouffier was the silent architect of the entir
2 CONC e Halluin-Piennes ducal fortune. His placement in Generation 17 provides the essential landed catalyst that transformed the ancient family from Flemish marcher lords into hereditary French peers.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Picardy Landed Vault'''
2 CONT To understand Guillaume's impact, one must look at the geopolitical map of the early 1500s. The senior branch of the Halluin family had plenty of ancient, sovereign prestige rooted in Flanders, but they desperately needed deep, se
2 CONC cure, revenue-generating territorial bases inside France to survive the transition to the Valois court.
2 CONT When Guillaume Gouffier married Louise de Crèvecoeur, he locked down the vital seigneuries of Crèvecoeur, Thois, and Grand-Rullecourt under a unified, high-status umbrella. As a prominent figure within the elite Gouffier ministeri
2 CONC al family—which held absolute royal favor under King Francis I—Guillaume built up this Picardy estate block into a powerhouse of material wealth. He fortified its borders and consolidated its revenues, turning it into one of the m
2 CONC ost lucrative and strategically vital military dower blocks on the northern frontier.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Widow's Gold and the Halluin Pivot'''
2 CONT When Guillaume passed away around 1540, the strict legal mechanisms of French customary law went into effect. His young widow, Louise, did not lose this immense territorial apparatus; instead, she walked away as an incredibly weal
2 CONC thy independent matriarch, holding full hereditary title to her Crèvecoeur lands and extensive dower rights over the Gouffier estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT This made her the most coveted matrimonial prize on the French northern border. When Antoine d'Halluin (Antoine the Elder) stepped forward to marry her later that year, he wasn't just contracting a personal union—he was executing 
2 CONC a corporate takeover of a pre-packaged Picardy empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT By stepping directly into the shoes of Guillaume Gouffier, Antoine instantly inherited a fully functional, highly favored court infrastructure. The immense wealth generated by Guillaume's initial estate management became the liter
2 CONC al bank account that funded the Halluin family's meteoric rise. It provided the landed security that allowed Louise's subsequent Generation 18 children—including the future 1st Duc d'Halluin—to operate at the absolute apex of the 
2 CONC French royal court, forever changing the destiny of the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Additional Reading:'''
2 CONT 1. Welsh, William E. “Slaughter in the Park: The Battle of Pavia.” Warfare History Network, Aug. 2006, warfarehistorynetwork.com/article/slaughter-in-the-park-the-battle-of-pavia/.
