1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha,_Duchess_of_Brittany '''Bertha, Duchess of Brittany'''] From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT * Bertha of Brittany (Breton: Berthe Breizh) Bertha of Cornwall (b.c 1114, died 1156), also known as Bertha of Brittany (Breton: Berthe Breizh ), was hereditary Duchess of Brittany between 1148 until her death. Bertha was the elde
2 CONC st daughter of Conan III of Brittany by Maude, the illegitimate daughter of King Henry I of England.She was the last member of the Cornwall family to reign over Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1138 Bertha was married to Alan Count of Penthièvre, who was created created 1st Earl of Richmond by Stephen of England for his support against the dispossessed Empress Matilda during the English Civil War.The marriage between 
2 CONC Bertha and Alan may have been intended to bring Brittany on the side of Stephen in the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha had three children with Alan,
2 CONT
2 CONT Conan IV, Duke of Brittany, b. 1138, their son and heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Brittany, who married Alan III, Vicomte of Rohan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enougen, abbess of St. Sulpice.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1146 her husband Alan had died, and Bertha returned home to Brittay where she married Eudes, Viscount of Porhoet.Two years later her father Duke Conan III died, and on his deathbed he had renounced Hoèl as illegitimate and no s
2 CONC on of his, designating Bertha as his heiress. However, Hoel would remain as count of Nantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Bertha died in 1156 war broke out between Bertha's son Conan IV and her second husband Eudes. With her death the ducal throne passed to her son. Eudas may have made a compact with his brother-in-law, Hoel of Nantes to divide 
2 CONC Brittany between them. However in late 1156 Conan IV was able to defeat Eudas and secure his maternal inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha, Duchess of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha of Cornwall (b.c 1114, died 1156), also known as Bertha of Brittany (Breton: Berthe Breizh ), was hereditary Duchess of Brittany between 1148 until her death.[1] Bertha was the eldest daughter of Conan III of Brittany by Ma
2 CONC ude, the illegitimate daughter of King Henry I of England.[1] She was the last member of the Cornwall family to reign over Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1138 Bertha was married to Alan Count of Penthièvre, who was created created 1st Earl of Richmond by Stephen of England for his support against the dispossessed Empress Matilda during the English Civil War.[1][2] The marriage b
2 CONC etween Bertha and Alan may have been intended to bring Brittany on the side of Stephen in the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha had three children with Alan,
2 CONT
2 CONT Conan IV, Duke of Brittany, b. 1138, their son and heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Brittany, who married Alan III, Vicomte of Rohan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enougen, abbess of St. Sulpice.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1146 her husband Alan had died, and Bertha returned home to Brittay where she married Eudes, Viscount of Porhoet.[1] Two years later her father Duke Conan III died, and on his deathbed he had renounced Hoèl as illegitimate and 
2 CONC no son of his, designating Bertha as his heiress. However, Hoel would remain as count of Nantes.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Bertha died in 1156 war broke out between Bertha's son Conan IV and her second husband Eudes.[1] With her death the ducal throne passed to her son. Eudas may have made a compact with his brother-in-law, Hoel of Nantes to divi
2 CONC de Brittany between them. However in late 1156 Conan IV was able to defeat Eudas and secure his maternal inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
