1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====FMG: Brittany [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/brittnpr.htm#EudesIPorhoetdied1092B Nantes, Porhoet, Rennes]
2 CONT * 3. '''GEOFFROY de Porhoët''' (-1142). Geslin de Bourgogne records that Vicomte Joscelin donated property to the church of Saint-Martin in the castle of Porhoét, assisted by his brothers "Guihenæus, Geoffroy et Alain", by charter
2 CONC  dated 1108[362]. He succeeded his brother before 1114 as '''Vicomte de Porhoët.'''  "Gauffredus vicecomes de Porrehodio castro et fratres eius Alanus et Bernardus" reached agreement with the abbé of Marmoutiers regarding a donati
2 CONC on by "frater suus Jostho vicecomes", by charter dated [1116][363]. "Gaufredus vicecomes filius Eudonis" donated property to the abbey of Saint-Martin, with the consent of "Alanum vicecomitem fratrem suum", by charter dated 1118, 
2 CONC witnessed by "…Bernardus infans frater vicecomitis…"[364]. He was given a hundred in Devon by Henry I King of England, recorded in the 1131 Pipe Roll[365].
2 CONT * '''m HAWISE''', daughter of ---.  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT * '''Geoffroy & his wife had five children:''' 
2 CONT ** a) '''EUDES''' [II] '''de Porhoët''' (-after 1180). "Gaufredus vicecomes, Eudo filius eius…" witnessed a charter dated 15 Jun 1130 under which Donald Bishop of Saint-Malo confirmed a donation to the monks of Saint-Martin de Jos
2 CONC selin[366].  Comte de Porhoët. He succeeded in 1148 as EUDES '''Duke of Brittany''', by right of his first wife. — see below. 
2 CONT ** b) '''JOSCELIN de Porhoët''' (-after 1153). "Eudo comes" founded the abbey of Notre-Dame de Lantenac by charter dated 1149, witnessed by "Josthos et Alanus fratres comitis, Alanus vicecomes de Monteforti…"[367]. "Eudo comes" co
2 CONC nfirmed a donation to Saint-Martin de Josselin, with the consent of "fratrum meorum…Joscii vicecomitis et Alani Ceoche", by charter dated 1153[368]. "Eudo comes" donated property previously donated by "dominus Gaufridus pater meus
2 CONC " to the priory of Josselin on the advice of "fratrum meorum Joscii vicecomitis et Alani Ceoche" by charter dated 1153[369]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ** c) '''ALAIN de Porhoët''' (-1190). "Eudo comes" founded the abbey of Notre-Dame de Lantenac by charter dated 1149, witnessed by "Josthos et Alanus fratres comitis, Alanus vicecomes de Monteforti…"[370]. "Eudo comes" confirmed a
2 CONC  donation to Saint-Martin de Josselin made by “Dominus Gaufridus pater meus”, with the consent of "fratrum meorum…Joscii vicecomitis et Alani Ceoche", by charter dated 1153[371]. He established himself in England in or before 1172
2 CONC  as ALAN [I] la Zouche, the name presumably being based on “Ceoche” as recorded in the previously quoted charter.  — ZOUCHE. 
2 CONT ** d) '''ETIENNE'''. "Eudo Britannie comes" donated property to Saint-Martin de Josselin, with the consent of "fratrum meorum…Joscii vicecomitis et Alani Ceoche", witnessed by "Roaldo Dongie vicecomitis, Henrico de Arundelle, ---d
2 CONC io de Leon, Gaufredo de Monteforti", with the consent of "Alano de Rohan cognato meo", by charter dated 1164, witnessed by "…Stephanus frater meus…Guihomarus alterius filius de Leon…"[372]. 
2 CONT ** e) '''AMICIE de Porhoët''' (-after Aug 1162). "Guillelmus Monfortensis dominus" founded Montfort abbey, with the consent of "uxore mea Amicia...filiis ac fratribus meis", by undated charter, which also records the donation made
2 CONC  by “Amicia uxor mea”[373]. A charter records that "Gaufridus Junior filius Guillelmi domini Monfortensis" laid the first stone for Montfort abbey on 1 May 1152, that "Radulfus...maior fil. eiusdem Guill." laid the second stone, t
2 CONC hat “ipse Guillelmus” laid the third, and “Amicia uxor ipsius Guillelmi” the fourth, adding that on the first Sunday of August (ten years later from the context) “Amicia domina Monfortensis” donated property with the consent of “f
2 CONC iliis suis Radulfo et Gaufrido”[374]. Her parentage is confirmed by a charter dated 1213 under which [her grandson] "Willelmus dominus Montiforten" confirmed donations to Montfort made by "antecessoribus meis...domino Gaufrido de 
2 CONC Monteforti...W. avi mei", quoting a charter dated 1180 under which “Gaufridus dominus Montifortis sequens vestigial patris mei Willelmi” donated property with the consent of “uxore mea et filiis meis Rad. Will et Rolando...Eudone 
2 CONC comite avunculo meo [identified as Eudes Comte de Porhoët, Duke of Brittany, see above]...Oliverio patruo meo”[375].  '''m GUILLAUME''' [I] '''Seigneur de Montfort''', son of --- (-May 1157, bur Montfort abbey).  
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>—————————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT Third son, and second to succeed to the title, of Eudo de Porhoët. Not to be confused with the later Geoffrey de la Zouche. Must have been of age by 1108, as he cosigned (or witnessed) a charter with his brothers Vicomte Josselin,
2 CONC  Wihenoc, and Alain. His wife's given name was Hawise; her ancestry is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I of England gave him a hundred in Devon circa 1131.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffroy & his wife had five children:  sons Eudes (Eudo, Odo), Joscelin, Alain, Etienne, and daughter Amicie. The third son, Alain, would establish himself in England as Alan la Zouche.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NONE OF GEOFFREY'S OTHER CHILDREN EVER USED THE NAME "ZOUCHE"!'''
