1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/normacre.htm#GuillaumeTancarvilledied1129
2 CONT
2 CONT RABEL de Tancarville, son of GUILLAUME [I] de Tancarville & his wife Mathilde d’Arques (-1140).   m secondly ---.  The inheritance by Rabel’s daughter of property held by his known wife Tiphaine (see below), instead of Rabel’s son
2 CONC  Guillaume, suggests that the latter was born from a later otherwise unrecorded marriage of his father.  If that is correct, no indication has been found of the identity of Rabel’s second wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabel & his second wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         GUILLAUME [II] de Tancarville (-[11 Nov 1190/1191]).  His parentage is confirmed by the undated charter under which his son "Raoul, Chamberlain de Tancarville, fils de Guillaume, Chamberlain" confirmed the foundation of
2 CONC  the priory of Sainte-Barbe by "Rabel le Chambellan son aïeul"[832].  The Red Book of the Exchequer records enfeoffments in the duchy of Normandy in [1172], "camerarius de Tanquarville" with 10 knights and 18 knights in his own se
2 CONC rvice[833].  His parentage is confirmed by the undated charter under which his son "Raoul, Chamberlain de Tancarville, fils de Guillaume, Chamberlain" confirmed the foundation of the priory of Sainte-Barbe by "Rabel le Chambellan 
2 CONC son aïeul"[834].  Henry II King of England confirmed the possessions of the priory of Sainte-Barbe-en-Auge, including donations by "Rabelli Camerarii…Willelmus camerarius pater eius…Willelmi Camerarii filii Rabelli", by charter da
2 CONC ted to [1185/89][835].  “...Willielmum Chamberlangum de Tancorvile...” subscribed the charter dated [11 Nov] 1190 under which Richard I King of England agreed an alliance with Tancred King of Sicily at Messina[836].  Guillaume [II
2 CONC ] presumably died soon afterwards as no further references to him have been found.  m ---.  The name of Guillaume’s wife is not known.  Guillaume [II] & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         RAOUL [II] de Tancarville (-before 1204).  "Raoul, Chamberlain de Tancarville, fils de Guillaume, Chamberlain" confirmed the foundation of the priory of Sainte-Barbe by "Rabel le Chambellan son aïeul" by undated charter
2 CONC [837].  “Raoul le chambellan” donated “l’église des Etables” to the abbey of Saint-Georges de Bocherville” by undated charter[838].  Raoul [II] must have died before 1204, the date of a charter of his brother Guillaume [III] (see 
2 CONC below). 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         GUILLAUME [III] de Tancarville (-after 1214).  "Willelmus camerarius de Tancharvilla, filius Rabelli" confirmed the purchase of land at Bretteville from the church of Bayeux, with the consent of "Willelmum filium meum",
2 CONC  by charter dated to [1182/1205][839]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         MARSILIE de Tancarville (-after 1205).  Philippe II King of France confirmed the marriage contract between “Guillelmus cambellanus de Tancarvilla...Isabella filia sua” and “Adæ filio...Galterii junioris camerarii nostri
2 CONC ” by charter dated 1205, which provided for an increase in the dowry if Guillaume de Tancarville had no male heir “ex Alliz uxore sua” and if “Marsilia sororia ipsius” died[840]
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Tancarville, great chamberlain to the king [Henry II] by tenure, a man noble in worth, a very death to the envious, became suspect to our king*, through accusations of many persons. Still, the king often heard of him as
2 CONC  victor in many encounters, heard that he was a father to his knights, and bread to the needy, that he was one who could turn the hearts of all, save only of the envious, to his will, and that he was acceptable and dear to the kin
2 CONC g of the French, and to others of whom the king stood in awe“ (all quotes from De Nugis Curialium or “Courtiers’ Trifles,” by Walter Map, M.R James, ed. & trans.; C.N. L. Brocke & R.A.B. Mynors, rev.; Oxford University Press, 1983
2 CONC , p. 498).
2 CONT
2 CONT *Tancarville was just too powerful, held too many castles and had too many men, and was respected by too many other powerful lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charter de Sainte-Barbe-en-Auge'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His parentage is confirmed by the undated charter under which his son "Raoul, Chamberlain de Tancarville, fils de Guillaume Chamberlain" confirmed the foundation of the priory of Sainte-Barbe by "Rabel le Chambellan son aïeul".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Monastic tending of the pedigree ... careful cultivation of the heir. ... the following charter c.1140-1157, which comes from the highest ranks of the Anglo-Norman aristocracy, the lords of Tancarville, illuminates it with sharp e
2 CONC dged brilliance:
2 CONT
2 CONT "To all faithful in Christ, William the Chamberlain junior, greetings in the Lord. Be it known to all present and future that I William the Chamberlain junior, on the fifth day after I was belted as a knight, came to St-Georges an
2 CONC d there to Abbot Louis and the monks, rejoicing with a great joy, received me in a procession of hounor, and there I offered up my sword on the altar of St-Georges. And then, by the councel and advice of many of the noblemen and c
2 CONC ompanions who had come with me, namely Robert des Ifs, my steward, and Roger de Cailly and John de la Londe and many others, I redeemed my sword by the gift and by the confirmation of many churches, which I conceded to them on tha
2 CONC t day of my gift and confirmed them just as my grandfather, the founder of that monastery, had given them, namely the churches of Abbetot, Epretot with its tithes, St-Romain with two-thirds of its tithes, and Tibermesnil. I also c
2 CONC onfirmed the gifts of my knights and friends which they gave on the same day to the abbey in perpetual alms. Roger de Cailly gave 20s. annually, Robert de Mortemer 10s., Robert des Ifs 10s., John de la Londe, my cousin, 10s., Andr
2 CONC ew de Bosemuncel 10s. or the tithes of one ploughland of land in Abbetot." ... and so on for another eight donors. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''le Château Fort de Tancarville'''
2 CONT
2 CONT A first castle was founded for a lord named Tancredi who gave his name to the castle fort built on a spur overlooking the Seine to Raoul de Tancarville, Chamberlain of William the Conqueror in the 11th century, of which there rema
2 CONC ins the square tower; rebuilt in the 12th century, still for the lords of Tancarville,
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Knightly training'''
2 CONT
2 CONT about 1160 - le Château Fort de Tancarville Early in the reign of Henry II, William Marshall was sent to his cousin William, the Chamberlain of Tancarville, in Normandy, to be trained in knightly accomplishments. Marshal returns t
2 CONC o England in the Autumn of 1167.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Aristocracy of Norman England, by Judith A. Green At all events the distinctive circumstances in England were apparent to contemporaries. On the continent the juvenes went tourneying to establish their reputation and fulfil th
2 CONC e "bachelor's" ideal. England by contrast, as William de Tancarville pointed out to William Marshal, was not a land for tournaments; it was fit only for vavassors and stay-at-homes.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonial England, 1066-1215, by J. C. Holt, p. 236
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Enfeoffments de Normandie'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Red Book of the Exchequer records enfeoffments in the duchy of Normandy in 1172, "camerarius de Tanquarville" with 10 knights and 18 knights in his own service.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Estone Manor - Aston Cantlow, Warwickshire
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1086 it appears among the possessions of Osbern Fitz Richard.[5] It was then rated at 5 hides, and the tenants of the manor included 9 Flemings. By 1169 it had passed to William the Chamberlain of Tankervill,[6] who, four years
2 CONC  later, was farming it of the King at a rent of £13 6s. 8d. By an undated grant he gave to the Abbey of Winchcombe all the land, in wood and plain, between Alne and his manor of Estone on condition that it should remain uncultivat
2 CONC ed and that his men should enjoy the same common rights there as they had in the rest of the wood and plain of Alne.[7] He was still holding the manor in 1177[8] and may have been succeeded by Ralph de Tankervill, who is referred 
2 CONC to fifty years later as having formerly possessed it.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT It ultimately escheated to the Crown[10] and in 1205 John granted it to William de Cantelupe,[11] from whose family the village takes its name.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cantelupes held the manor for four generations in direct descent. The first William obtained a confirmation of it in 1227,[12] and again in 1231,[13] until such time as the King should be pleased to restore it to the right hei
2 CONC rs of Ralph Tankervill.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bruton Hundred'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1212 the hundred comprised the Tancarville fee of Bruton, Honeywick (in Pitcombe), Milton (Clevedon), Pitcombe with Cole, Redlynch, Witham, and Yarlington.[14] In 1225 Discove, Woolston (in North Cadbury), and the vill of West 
2 CONC Bruton were named in addition.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruton hundred was presumably a Crown possession as part of the royal estate of Bruton in 1066 and 1086.[16] Before 1133 it had passed as part of a fee either to William de Tancarville I (d. 1129) or to his son Rabel (d. 1140), he
2 CONC reditary chamberlains of England and Normandy.[17] William de Tancarville II succeeded his father Rable as lord in Bruton and was still alive in 1177.[18] William was followed by his son Ralph,[19] but thereafter the family ceased
2 CONC  to have an interest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1135 and 1154 Alexander de Cauntelo, a principal tenant on the Tancarville fee, granted the hundred, with the market and other land, to the new priory at Bruton in return for a fee farm of 2 marks.[20] The fee farm was tra
2 CONC nsferred to the Crown on the forfeiture of the Cauntelo interest between 1199 and 1208.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hereditary Chamberlains'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the eleventh century, it was a stronghold lordship which depended Raoul de Tancarville, guardian of Duke William the Conqueror, future king of England. The fact that Raoul was chamberlain of Normandy, and this function has rema
2 CONC ined in the family, reinforces a simple corroboration of the hereditary nature of this title. The seal shown here is for Robert de Tancarville le Chamberlain, almost 300 years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marshall is Knighted'''
2 CONT
2 CONT about 1167 in Driencourt (Neufchatel-en-Bray) Marshall returns to his cousin, the Chamberlain of Tancarville, who knights him at Driencourt (Neufchatel-en-Bray), and under whom he won distinction in the half-hearted warfare of the
2 CONC  Norman barons with the Flemings before Neufchatel in July 1167.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources'''
2 CONT
