1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =William de Valence, 1st Earl of Pembroke=
2 CONT From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Valence,_1st_Earl_of_Pembroke Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William de Valence, 1st Earl of Wexford and 1st Earl of Pembroke'''  (1225–1230 – 16 or 18 May 1296), born '''Guillaume de Lusignan or de Valence''', was a French nobleman and Knight, who became important in English politics du
2 CONC e to his relationship to Henry III. He was heavily involved in the Second Barons' War, supporting the King and Prince Edward against the rebels led by Simon de Montfort. He took the name de Valence ("of Valence").
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth son of Isabella of Angoulême, widow of king John of England, and her second husband, Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, and was thus a half-brother to Henry III of England, and uncle to Edward I. William was
2 CONC  born at Valence, near Lusignan, sometime in the mid-to-late 1220s (his elder sister, Alice was born 1224, and two elder brothers followed her).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Move to England==
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of Arms of William de Valence before he became Earl of Pembroke
2 CONT The French conquest of Poitou in 1246 created great difficulties for William's family, and so he and his brothers, Guy de Lusignan and Aymer, accepted Henry III's invitation to come to England in 1247. The king found important pos
2 CONC itions for all of them; William was soon married to a great heiress, Joan de Munchensi or Munchensy (c. 1230 – after 20 September 1307), Lady of Swanscombe and Countess of Pembroke, the only surviving child of Warin de Munchensi, 
2 CONC Lord of Swanscombe and Earl of Pembroke, and wife Joan Marshal, daughter and eventual co-heiress of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Isabel de Clare, 4th Countess of Pembroke suo jure. Her portion of the Marshal estates i
2 CONC ncluded the castle and lordship of Pembroke and the lordship erected earldom of Wexford in Ireland. The custody of Joan's property was entrusted to her husband, along with, apparently, the title of Earl of Pembroke[citation needed
2 CONC ] and Earl of Wexford between 1250 and 1260.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The Second Barons' War==
2 CONT
2 CONT This favouritism to royal relatives was unpopular with many of the English nobility, a discontent which would culminate in the Second Barons' War. It did not take long for William to make enemies in England. From his new lands in 
2 CONC South Wales, he tried to regain the palatine rights which had been attached to the Earldom of Pembroke, but his energies were not confined to this. The King heaped lands and honours upon him, and he was soon thoroughly hated as on
2 CONC e of the most prominent of the rapacious foreigners. Moreover, some trouble in Wales led to a quarrel between him and Simon de Montfort, who was to become the figurehead for the rebels. He refused to comply with the provisions imp
2 CONC osed on the King at Oxford in 1258, and took refuge in Wolvesey Castle at Winchester, where he was besieged and compelled to surrender and leave the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in 1259 William and de Montfort were formally reconciled in Paris, and in 1261 Valence was again in England and once more enjoying the royal favour. He fought for Henry at the disastrous Battle of Lewes, and after the def
2 CONC eat again fled to France, while de Montfort ruled England. However, by 1265 he was back, landing in Pembrokeshire, and taking part in the Siege of Gloucester and the final royalist victory at Evesham. After the battle he was resto
2 CONC red to his estates and accompanied Prince Edward, afterwards Edward I, to Palestine. From his base in Pembrokeshire he was a mainstay of the English campaigns against Llywelyn ap Gruffudd and later Dafydd ap Gruffudd; in the war o
2 CONC f 1282–3 that led to the conquest of Wales he negotiated the surrender of one of Dafydd's last remaining castles, Castell-y-Bere, with its custodian, Cynfrig ap Madog. He also went several times to France on public business and he
2 CONC  was one of Edward's representatives in the famous suit over the succession to the crown of Scotland in 1291 and 1292.
2 CONT William de Valence died at Bayonne on the 13 June 1296; his body is buried at Westminster Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Descendants==
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Joan de Munchensi (described above) had the following children:
2 CONT Isabel de Valence (died 5 October 1305), married before 1280 John Hastings, 1st Baron Hastings (6 May 1262 – 10 February 1313). Their grandson Lawrence later became earl of Pembroke. They had: William Hastings (1282–1311) John Has
2 CONC tings, 2nd Baron Hastings (29 September 1286 – 20 January 1325), married to Juliane de Leybourne (died 1367) Sir Hugh Hastings of Sutton (died 1347) Joan de Valence, married to John Comyn (the "Red Comyn"), Lord of Badenoch (died 
2 CONC 10 February 1306, murdered), and had Elizabeth Comyn (1 November 1299 – 20 November 1372), married to Richard Talbot, Lord Talbot John de Valence (died January 1277) William de Valence (died 16 June 1282, in the Battle of Llandeil
2 CONC o Fawr in Wales), created Seigneur de Montignac and Bellac Aymer de Valence, 2nd Earl of Pembroke and Wexford in 1296 (c. 1270 – 23 June 1324), married firstly to Beatrice de Clermont and married secondly to Marie de Chatillon Mar
2 CONC garet de Valence Agnes de Valence (born c. 1250, date of death unknown) Preceded by New Creation	Earl of Pembroke ? –1296	Succeeded by Aymer de Valence
