1 NAME Hector /de Chartres/ chevalier, seigneur d’Ons- en-Bray, Alonne et Candeville, baro
2 GIVN Hector
2 NSFX chevalier, seigneur d’Ons- en-Bray, Alonne et Candeville, baro
2 _MARNM de Chartres
1 NAME Hector de Chartres, seigneur d’Ons- en-Bray
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1352
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 JUN 1418
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paris
3 STAE Île-de-France
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Grand âître et Enquêteur des Eaux et Forêts de Normandie et de Picardie en 1388, chevalier, seigneur d’Ons- en-Bray, Alonne et Candeville, baron du Chêne-Dollé, Maître d’hôtel du Roi, Grand Maître des Eaux-&-Forêts de Normandie & 
2 CONC de Picardie
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from MEDLANDS 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         HECTOR de Chartres (-Paris 1418).  Père Anselme shows his parentage and cites sources dated 1380, 1388, 1400, and 1406 in which he is named[1113].  Seigneur d’Ons-en-Bray.  It looks likely that Hector acquired Ons-en-Br
2 CONC ay, held by her first husband’s family, from his second wife.  Anselme indicates that Hector was killed at Azincourt in 1415, but this appears to confuse him with his son of the same name[1114].  Régine Pernoud says that Hector de
2 CONC  Chartres died “at Paris during the Cabochian uprising of 1418, a casualty of the Burgundian capture of the city”[1115].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly JEANNE d’Estouteville, daughter of JEAN d’Estouteville Seigneur de Torcy & his wife ---.  Père Anselme records her parentage and marriage, adding that she died childless (no sources cited)[1116].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (after 1373) as her second husband, BLANCHE de Nesle, widow of GUY de Beaumont Seigneur d’Ons-en-Bray, de Clarois, et de Neufville, daughter of [GUY de Nesle Seigneur de Mello, Maréchal de France [Clermont] & his first 
2 CONC wife Jeanne de Bruyères] ([before 1350?]-).  Père Anselme records that “Blanche de Clermont dite de Nesle dame de Brétigny, fille de Jean de Clermont dit de Nesle seigneur de Mello et d’Offémont” married firstly Guy de Beaumont an
2 CONC d secondly Hector de Chartres[1117].  Her likely birth date is incompatible with this parentage, but she could have been Jean [II]’s sister.  A document dated 1373 records that “Madame Blanche de Nesle femme jadis de feu messire G
2 CONC ui de Beaumont chevalier...dame de Ons-en-Bray tant en son nom comme ayant le bail de ses filles” held “son hôtel et lieu de Onz” from “[le] châtel de Clermont” and listed the properties[1118].  Père Anselme confuses matters by re
2 CONC cording, in another passage, that Blanche de Nesle, daughter of Jean [II], married firstly Raoul de Flavy, secondly Guy de Beaumont, and thirdly Hector de Chartres[1119].  This suggestion is impossible chronologically: it is assum
2 CONC ed that Jean’s daughter Blanche was a different person and that she married Raoul de Flavy as her only husband.  It looks likely that Blanche transferred or sold Ons-en-Bray, held by her first husband’s family, to her second husba
2 CONC nd.  Hector & his second wife had three children:  i)          PIERRE de Chartres (-after 1406).  Père Anselme records his parentage, noting that he and his brother Renaud were fined in 1406 for insulting the bailly of the bishop 
2 CONC of Beauvais[1120].  ii)         RENAUD de Chartres ([1375/80?]-Tours 4 Apr 1444, bur Tours Cordeliers).  Père Anselme records his parentage, noting that he and his brother Renaud were fined in 1406 for insulting the bailly of the 
2 CONC bishop of Beauvais[1121].  His descent from the Nesle family is confirmed by his arms including Nesle[1122].  Canon at Beauvais Saint-Pierre.  Deacon of Beauvais Saint-Pierre 1404.  Archbishop of Reims 1414.  Chancellor of France 
2 CONC 28 Mar 1424[1123].  Gallia Christiana records details of his ecclesiastical career, his date of death, and his place of burial[1124].  iii)        HECTOR de Chartres (-killed in battle Azincourt 25 Oct 1415).  Père Anselme records
2 CONC  his parentage, noting that he was “un des quatre chassiers de l’église de Chartres” and swore allegiance to the bishop 12 Apr 1402 for property he held near Chartres[1125].  Père Anselme records his death in 1418 but, as noted ab
2 CONC ove, appears to confuse Hector with his father.  m ANTOINETTE de Hemery, daughter of ---.  Père Anselme records her family origin and marriage (no sources cited)[1126].  Hector & his wife had one child:  (a)       ISABELLE de Char
2 CONC tres .  Père Anselme records her parentage and marriage, noting that her paternal uncle Archbishop Renaud granted “la terre, ville et seigneurie de Vierzon” to her husband 7 Aug 1425[1127].  Dame d’Ons-en-Bray.  m (before 7 Aug 14
2 CONC 25) ANTOINE de Lévis Comte de Villars, son of PHILIPPE [IV] de Lévis Comte de Villars & his wife Antoinette d’Anduze (-after 13 Aug 1454, bur Annonay).  He succeeded his father in 1440 as Vicomte de Lautrec. 
