1 NAME Guigues V /de La Roche-en-Régnier/
2 GIVN Guigues V
2 _MARNM de La Roche-en-Régnier
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1278
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1344
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/AUVERGNE.htm#GuiguesRocheRegnierMDauphineLaTour
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         GUIGUES [IV] de Roche-en-Régnier (-after 1344).  His parentage is confirmed by his Nov 1298 marriage contract.  “Guigo dominus de Ruppe miles...et Guigo de Ruppe eius filius domicellus, sponsus futurus...Delphinæ filiæ 
2 CONC quondam...Bertrandi domini de Turre militis jan defuncti” guaranteed her dower by charter dated 1298[1614].  The 2 Jun 1369 charter quoted below suggests that his father had excluded Guigues [IV] from his succession, although the 
2 CONC latter calls himself “seigneur de Roche” in his 1344 testament quoted below.  Presumably Guigues [IV] continued to claim the seigneurie as he called himself “seigneur de Roche” in his 1344 testament quoted below, although the seig
2 CONC neurie passed to his half-sister Jamague (a succession which was disputed by the descendants of his full sister Alix, as shown below).  No record has been found of the descendants of Guigues [IV]’s daughter Beatrix claiming Roche-
2 CONC en-Régnier.  [Seigneur de Roche-en-Régnier].  Seigneur de Posquières (inherited from his mother).  The testament of “Guigon seigneur de Roche et de Posquières”, dated 1344, provided dowry for “Dalphine de Canilhaco filie...filie n
2 CONC ostre Beatricis uxorisque condam...domini de Canilhaco” to marry “dominum de Ceveyraco”[1615].  This son is referred to, but not named, in the 2 Jun 1369 charter quoted below.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (contract Nov 1298) DAUPHINE de la Tour, daughter of BERTRAND [V] Seigneur de la Tour & his wife Beatrix d’Olliergues (-after 25 Sep 1323).  The testament of "dominus Bertrandus dominus de Turre filius quondam…Bernardi dom
2 CONC ini de Turre militis", dated 1285, makes bequests to "Delfinam filiam suam…Bertrandum filium suum…Guillelmo filio suo…dominam Beatricem uxorem eius…esse pregnantem…filium masculum…postum[um]..."[1616].  The testament of "Yzeu domi
2 CONC na Yseuz domina d’Anjo filia quondam...Agnonis domini d’Oliergues et Magnimontis", dated 12 Mar 1293 (O.S.), bequeathed property to "Delfinæ neptæ meæ filiæ dominæ Beatricis de Turres sororis meæ...pro ipsa maritanda..."[1617].  T
2 CONC he marriage contract of "Guiguonem dominum de Rupe militem…pro Guiguone de Rupe filio suo" and "dominam Beatricem de Olergio dominam de Turre…pro domicella Delphina de Turre filia" is dated Nov 1298[1618].  “Guigo dominus de Ruppe
2 CONC  miles...et Guigo de Ruppe eius filius domicellus, sponsus futurus...Delphinæ filiæ quondam...Bertrandi domini de Turre militis jan defuncti” guaranteed her dower by charter dated 1298[1619].  The testament of “Dauphine dame de Ro
2 CONC che, femme de...Guigue sire de Roche”, dated 25 Sep 1323, named “sa fille Béatrice femme de...Marquesius de Canilhac” as her universal heir[1620].  “Aliénore de Canilhac femme de Briand de Retourtour seigneur de Belcastel” confirm
2 CONC ed her renunciation of the succession of “Béatrix de Roche dame de Canhilac sa mère, que de Dauphine de la Tour jadis femme dudit sire de Roche sa grand-mère” by charter dated 16 Mar 1348[1621].  Guigues & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *i)          BEATRIX de Roche-en-Régnier (after 1298-before 1329).  The testament of “Dauphine dame de Roche, femme de...Guigue sire de Roche”, dated 25 Sep 1323, named “sa fille Béatrice femme de...Marquesius de Canilhac” as her 
2 CONC universal heir[1622].  Felgères notes her parentage and marriage, stating that Marquès de Canilhac was recorded as widower of Béatrix in a charter dated 1329[1623].  “Aliénore de Canilhac femme de Briand de Retourtour seigneur de 
2 CONC Belcastel” confirmed her renunciation of the succession of “Béatrix de Roche dame de Canhilac sa mère, que de Dauphine de la Tour jadis femme dudit sire de Roche sa grand-mère” by charter dated 16 Mar 1348[1624].  '''m''' (after 1
2 CONC 315?) as his second wife, MARQUES Seigneur de Canilhac, son of MARQUES Seigneur de Canilhac & his wife Eléonore d’Apchier (-[1350/59]). 
