1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Reginald-I-de-Courtenay/6000000188589299896
2 CONT * Not the husband of https://www.geni.com/people/Maud-fitzWilliam-Dame-du-Sap/6000000005076814891?through=6000000188589299896
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Renauld de Courtenay==
2 CONT * Born: Unknown
2 CONT * Died: Unknown
2 CONT * Father: Milon de Courtenay 
2 CONT * Mother: Elizabeth de Nevers
2 CONT * Wife: (uncertain) du Donjon
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT # Elizabeth, his heiress, who married Pierre de France, and assumed the Courtenay name 
2 CONT # Adelvia (Adelina), who married Avalon, seigneur de Saillenay
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Updated 3 November 2022
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Renauld de Courtenay''', Seigneur de Courtenay, was the son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Renauld-de-Courtenay-Seigneur-de-Courtenay/6000000002187857591 Miles (Milo) de Courtenay], Seigneur (lord of the manor) of Courtenay, i
2 CONC n the Kingdom of France, today in the Loiret department in north-central France, by his wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Ermengarde-de-Nevers/6000000007329725483?through=6000000002187857591 Ermengard de Nevers].
2 CONT
2 CONT Renaud succeeded his father as Seigneur of Courtenay. He fought in the Second Crusade, with King Louis VII of France. He quarrelled with King Louis VII, who seized Renaud's French possessions and gave them along with Renaud's daug
2 CONC hter Elizabeth to his youngest brother, Pierre (Peter) of France, who thenceforth became known as [https://www.geni.com/people/Pierre-de-France-Seigneur-de-Courtenay/6000000000157075199?through=6000000000157099135 Peter I of Court
2 CONC enay] (died 1183).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find-A-Grave 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 58303847 · 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/58303847/renauld-de_courtenay
2 CONT
2 CONT Renauld de Courtenay was the son of Milo de Courtenay and Ermengarde of Nevers. His mother was the granddaughter of William of Nevers, the grandson of Robert II of France and Constance of Arles, through their daughter Advisa.
2 CONT
2 CONT He set out for Palestine, 1147, with King Louis VII, of France, but he returned before the King. When his older brothers died, he succeeded to the paternal inheritance. He was among those who vindicated Eleanor, wife of Louis VII 
2 CONC (and later Henry II of England), who was accused of infidelity while she was with Louis VII in the Holy Land. King Louis VII seized the lands of Renaud in retaliation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Renaud, while still in France, married Helvis, a sister of Gui du Donjon, who was one of the most famous knights of that age, who descended from the ancient Counts of Corbeil. They are the parents of Renaud de Courtenay, married t
2 CONC o Hawise, and Elizabeth or Isabella, married Peter of France, youngest son of Louis "le Gros," King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gravesite Details
2 CONT
2 CONT Forde Abbey was in Devon until 1842, when it passed to Dorset, and is now within the limits of Chard, Somerset. Find A Grave contributor Todd Whitesides
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Identification 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The other Renaud''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Stewart explained in a post at SGM, 27 January 2003 < [https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/psv2U0Vkd-s/m/wERwaoKEHmMJ GoogleGroups] > “Parents of Elisabeth de Courtenay”
2 CONT
2 CONT >… Despite the legend of a dispute between king and vassal, resulting in Renaud's dispossession and exile to England, there is no documentary evidence that he ever returned from crusade, and since his elder daughter was soon after
2 CONC wards married as his heiress to the king's brother it may be safer to assume that the seigneur de Courtenay didn't survive the expedition. If he did go back to become the lord of Sutton (father of Renaud #3c above), as in the CP v
2 CONC ersion of events, then this Renaud must have been a very old man when he died towards the end of 1190. On the whole this identification appears fairly weak - if the mysterious father of the ''English Renaud'' (#3c) belonged to the
2 CONC  same family he was more probably illegitimate than otherwise, maybe a son or nephew of his French namesake (#2).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT From Peter Stewart, post at SGM, 27 January 2003 < [https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/psv2U0Vkd-s/m/wERwaoKEHmMJ GoogleGroups] > “Parents of Elisabeth de Courtenay”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The following account of the first Courtenay family's last generations was given, largely from earlier secondary sources, in _Histoire généalogique et chronologique de la Maison royale de France..._ by Père Anselme (Pierre de Guib
2 CONC ours) & others, third edition, 9 vols (Paris, 1726-33), vol I pp 474 and 527-8:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1 Miles, seigneur de Courtenay, died after 1127 [NB actually in or after 1138 when he last occurs], married ca 1095 Ermengarde de Nevers
2 CONT **2 (their third son) [https://www.geni.com/people/Renauld-de-Courtenay-Seigneur-de-Courtenay/6000000002187857591?through=6000000000157099135 Renaud], seigneur de Courtenay, allegedly married an [https://www.geni.com/people/Helvid
2 CONC e-du-Donjon-Dame-d-Yerres/6000000006906647110?through=6000000002187857591 unnamed sister] of [https://www.geni.com/people/Guy-de-Donjon/6000000188566987849?through=6000000006906647110 Guy du Donjon] [who is stated wrongly to have 
2 CONC been living in 1148], also described as daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Frédéric-du-Donjon/6000000047046494827?through=6000000188566987849 Frederic],  seigneur du Donjon
2 CONT ***3a [https://www.geni.com/people/Élisabeth-de-Courtenay/6000000000157099135?through=6000000008833011818 Elisabeth], died 14 September in or after 1205, married Pierre de France (ancestors of the second French Courtenay line)
2 CONT ***3b [https://www.geni.com/people/N-N-de-Courtenay/6000000008833011818?through=6000000000157099135 Unnamed daughter], died sp, married [https://www.geni.com/people/Avalon-seigneur-de-Saillenay/6000000188590309835?through=60000000
2 CONC 08833011818 Avalon],  seigneur de Saillenay
2 CONT *** CP vol IV p 317 (table) states that Renaud (#2) lost his lands in France & held Sutton, Berkshire in 1161. He is given a second marriage, to Maud, dame du Sap, said to be childless. ''This version omits #3b above and adds inst
2 CONC ead'':
2 CONT ***3c [https://www.geni.com/people/Renaud-II-de-Courtenay/6000000006906655521?through=6000000002187857591 '''Renaud de Courtenay'''], died 27 September 1194, married [https://www.geni.com/people/Hawise-de-Courcy-Heiress-of-Okehamp
2 CONC ton/6000000010996350245?through=6000000006906655521 Hawise de Curcy], lady of Okehampton (''ancestors of the English Courtenays'') ['''Disputed origins'''] 
2 CONT
2 CONT With minor variations this has become the standard genealogy in English
2 CONT works since.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed children 
2 CONT
2 CONT These children detached from https://www.geni.com/people/Renauld-de-Courtenay-Seigneur-de-Courtenay/6000000002187857591?through=6000000006906655521 and added to https://www.geni.com/people/Reginald-I-de-Courtenay/60000001885892998
2 CONC 96:
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Renaud-II-de-Courtenay/6000000006906655521?through=6000000002187857591
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-de-Courtenay-Lord-of-Sutton/6000000003949713621?through=6000000006906655521
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Guillaume-de-Courtenay/6000000001210366732
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed wife or wives 
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Maud-fitzWilliam-Dame-du-Sap/6000000005076814891?through=6000000002187857591 detached as wife and moved to https://www.geni.com/people/Reginald-I-de-Courtenay-of-Sutton/6000000188589299896
2 CONT * (1) Helene (Hawise) du Donjon, daughter of Frederick du Donjon and Corbeil, sister of Guy du Donjon [alternate / additional wife identifications have been proposed] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From SGM post, Douglas Richardson, 8 November 2019 < [https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/psv2U0Vkd-s/m/wERwaoKEHmMJ GoogleGroups] >
2 CONT
2 CONT Starting at the beginning, Du Bouchet, Histoire généalogique de la Maison royale de Courtenay (1661): 11–12 states that "Renard" de Courtenay [living c.1149] married the sister of Guy du Donion, one of the celebrated knights of hi
2 CONC s time. On pages 377–378, he further identifies Elizabeth de Courtenay, wife of Pierre de France, as the daughter of Renaud, seigneur of Courtenay, by N... du Donjon, daughter of Ferry du Donjon, Seigneur of Yerre [Yerres]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The correct account is that Renaud de Courtenay (living c.1149) had a wife of unknown name and parentage, '''by whom he had two daughters''', the younger being Elizabeth (or Isabelle), wife of Pierre of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Renaud's death, his widow married (2nd) Ferri de Donjon (living 1174), by which marriage she had Baudouin du Donjon, [Saint] Guillaume du Donjon [Archbishop of Bourges], Gui du Donjon, and Pierre du Donjon. …
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Additionally seen:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Renaud de Courtenay 1, son of Miles de Courtenay and his wife Ermengarde de Nevers, is the fifth lord of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Renaud and his older brother William accompanied King Louis VII to Palestine to participate in the Second Crusade . His brother died during the trip, he returned to France and then has appropriate lordships of Courtenay, Châteaure
2 CONC nard, Champignelles, Bléneau, Tanlay and Charny .
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eustachia de Corbeil (sometimes called Elisabeth de Corbeil, or Hélène du Donjon), daughter of Baudoin de Corbeil 3 , of whom he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth , married to Pierre de France , son of King Louis VI . From this marriage comes the Capetian house of Courtenay , Another daughter, married to the lord of Seignelay 5 .
2 CONT
2 CONT http://gw.geneanet.org/frebault?lang=fr&pz=henri&nz=frebault&ocz=0&p=renaud&n=de+courtenay 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marié avec Moenée d'Arthel (Parents : H Hugues III d'Arthel, vicomte de Clamecy ?
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Disputed information 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renaud_de_Courtenay (has disputed information)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Spouse:  (1) '''Maud FitzRobert FitzRoy''' (NOT her half-sister Hawise d'Avranches, as often reported--Hawise married Rainaud/Reginald II. Cawley's Medieval Lands database is incorrect regarding this family). Maud died in 1124 chi
2 CONC ldless. (2) '''Helvis de Donjon''' who was mother to his children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:  Guillaume, Renaud/Reginald II Elizabeth, Robert
2 CONT
