1 OCCU King of France, Roi de France, Roi des Francs (1108-1137), King of the Franks, Kung, King, roi des Francs, Roi des Francs, Koning van Frankrijk (1108-1137), König
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} LOUIS VI OF FRANCE
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_VI_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis VI (1 December 1081 – 1 August 1137), called the Fat (French: le Gros), was King of France from 1108 until his death (1137). Chronicles called him "roi de Saint-Denis".
2 CONT
2 CONT The first member of the House of Capet to make a lasting contribution to the centralizing institutions of royal power, Louis was born in Paris, the son of Philip I and his first wife, Bertha of Holland. Almost all of his twenty-ni
2 CONC ne-year reign was spent fighting either the "robber barons" who plagued Paris or the Norman kings of England for their continental possession of Normandy. Nonetheless, Louis VI managed to reinforce his power considerably and becam
2 CONC e one of the first strong kings of France since the division of the Carolingian Empire. His biography by his constant advisor Abbot Suger of Saint Denis renders him a fully-rounded character to the historian, unlike most of his pr
2 CONC edecessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, Louis fought the duke of Normandy, Robert Curthose, and the lords of the royal demesne, the Île de France. He became close to Suger, who became his adviser. He succeeded his father on Philip's death on July 29, 1108.
2 CONC  Louis's half-brother prevented him from reaching Rheims and so he was crowned on August 3 in the cathedral of Orléans. The archbishop of Reims, Ralph the Green, sent envoys to challenge the validity of the coronation and anointin
2 CONC g, but to no avail.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Palm Sunday 1115, Louis was present in Amiens to support the bishop and inhabitants of the city in their conflict with Enguerrand I of Coucy, one of his vassals, who refused to recognize the granting of a charter of communal pr
2 CONC ivileges. Louis came with an army to help the citizens to besiege Castillon (the fortress dominating the city, from which Enguerrand was making punitive expeditions). At the siege, the king took an arrow to his hauberk, but the ca
2 CONC stle, considered impregnable, fell after two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis VI died on August 1, 1137, at the castle of Béthisy-Saint-Pierre, nearby Senlis and Compiègne, of dysentery caused by his excesses, which had made him obese. He was interred in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded on the t
2 CONC hrone by his son Louis VII, called "the Younger," who had originally wanted to be a monk.
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendants include Winston Churchill, George Washington and Lady Diana.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1104 Lucienne de Rochefort — the marriage was annulled. Their child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Isabelle (c.1105 – before 1175), married (ca 1119) William of Vermandois, seigneur of Chaumont 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1115 Adélaide de Maurienne (1092–1154)
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Philip (1116 – October 13, 1131), King of France (1129–31), not to be confused with his brother of the same name; died from a fall from a horse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Louis VII (1120 – November 18, 1180), King of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Henry (1121–75), archbishop of Reims 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Hugues (born ca 1122)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Robert (ca 1123 – October 11, 1188), count of Dreux 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Constance (ca 1124 – August 16, 1176), married first Eustace IV, count of Boulogne and then Raymond V of Toulouse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Philip (1125–61), bishop of Paris. not to be confused with his elder brother 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Peter of France (ca 1125–83), married Elizabeth, lady of Courtenay
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis VI (1 December 1081 – 1 August 1137), called the Fat (French: le Gros), was King of France from 1108 until his death (1137). Chronicles called him "roi de Saint-Denis". The first member of the House of Capet to make a lastin
2 CONC g contribution to the centralizing institutions of royal power,[1] Louis was born in Paris, the son of Philip I and his first wife, Bertha of Holland. Almost all of his twenty-nine-year reign was spent fighting either the "robber 
2 CONC barons" who plagued Paris or the Norman kings of England for their continental possession of Normandy. Nonetheless, Louis VI managed to reinforce his power considerably and became one of the first strong kings of France since the 
2 CONC division of the Carolingian Empire. His biography by his constant advisor Abbot Suger of Saint Denis renders him a fully-rounded character to the historian, unlike most of his predecessors.
