1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE FAT"; KING OF FRANCE 1108-1137 (ACCEDED 8/3/1108, CONSECRATED AT
2 CONT ORLEANS); DIED OF DYSENTERY
1 NOTE KING
1 NOTE <table width=610><tr align=left valign=top>
2 CONT <td valign=top><table border="1" align="center" width="220"><tr><td bgcolor=ffffff><img src="http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/images/of-france/Louis_06_King_of_France_-_Paris_France_1.jpg"alt="Image of King Louis VI of France" wi
2 CONC dth="200" align="center"></img><br clear="all"></br><center>King Louis VI of France<br><i>person at top of image</i></br><!-- 2871 --></td></tr></table></td>
2 CONT </tr><tr align=left valign=top>
2 CONT <td colspan=3>
2 CONT Louis VI, the Fat  (born 1081, ruled 1108-37), was the first important king of the Capetian line. This linesprang from Hugh Capet, who became king in 987. Louis the Fat was a great fighter, a great hunter, and a great eater.At 46 
2 CONC he became too fat to mount a horse, but he remained the embodiment of warlike energy. His great task was toreduce to order the petty nobles of the royal domain, who could truly be called robber barons. When Louis came to thethrone
2 CONC , every lord of a castle robbed at will and it was not safe for even the king to pass along the road. Twentyyears of hard fighting were necessary to remedy this condition, but in the end, law and order prevailed. So that suchevils
2 CONC  might not recur, every castle that was captured was destroyed or given to faithful followers.
2 CONT </td>
2 CONT <tr><td>For more information see the <a href="http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/index.htm">Our Folk - Hart family</a> Web Site</td></tr></table>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT from &quot;Our Folk&quot; by Albert D Hart, Jr.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Original individual @P2442106616@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2308128985@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE Louis VI, called The Fat (1081-1137), king of France (1108-37), son and 
2 CONT successor of Philip I; he was married to Adelaide of Savoy. Almost his 
2 CONT entire reign was spent in subduing the robber barons, who preyed on the 
2 CONT environs of Paris but were finally forced to yield to royal authority. For 
2 CONT some 20 years during the period from 1109-1135, Louis waged war against 
2 CONT Henry I, the Norman king of England, and against Henry's son-in-law, Holy 
2 CONT Roman Emperor Henry V; he successfully repelled an invasion by Henry V in 
2 CONT 1124. Louis greatly strengthed the royal power in France, granted 
2 CONT benefactions to the church and privileges to towns, and became known as 
2 CONT the protector of the peasants and as a fearless military leader. He was 
2 CONT succeeded on the throne by his son Louis VII.
