1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PLEASE DO NOT MERGE WITH HIS BROTHER, PHILLIPE ARCHDEACON OF PARIS (1125-1161)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_France_%281116%E2%80%931131%29
2 CONT
2 CONT and in French:  http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_de_France_%281116-1131%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip (II) of France (29 August 1116 – 13 October 1131) was the first son of Louis VI of France and his second wife Adélaide de Maurienne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The favourite son of his father whilst a child, Philip was enthroned alongside Louis VI as joint-king in 1129. However, the young king gave his father little joy after that, refusing to pay attention to the old king, or to follow 
2 CONC the high standards that Louis himself followed. He became disobedient, refusing to heed scoldings or warnings; Walter Map said that he "strayed from the paths of conduct travelled by his father and, by his overweening pride and ty
2 CONC rannical arrogance, made himself a burden to all."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip's brief period as King was ended two years after his sacring. Riding with a group of companions along the Seine, in the Parisian market section named the Greve, his running horse was tripped by a black pig which darted out 
2 CONC of a dung heap on the quay. The horse fell forwards, and the young king was catapulted over its head. The fall "so dreadfully fractured his limbs that he died on the day following" without regaining consciousness.[2] He was buried
2 CONC  at St Denis, and succeeded as heir, and co-King, by his meek-mannered brother, Louis the Younger (now known as Louis VII).
2 CONT
2 CONT If Philip had been little other than trouble and a problem to his family and kingdom whilst he had lived, his legacy would prove greater trouble still. Whilst he had lived, he had nurtured a dream of visiting Jerusalem and the tom
2 CONC b of Christ; when he died, his brother, Louis VII, vowed to go in Philip's place. This vow would provide a reason for Louis joining the disastrous Second Crusade, and an excuse to abandon Antioch in favour of Jerusalem – the Crusa
2 CONC de bringing many deaths on both sides, the abandonment of Antioch proving a strategic failure and a cause for the collapse of the marriage between Louis and his wife, Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because he was co-King rather than senior King, he is not generally given an ordinal as King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip (II)
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT Co-reign 	14 April 1129 – 13 October 1131
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation 	14 April 1129
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Louis VI of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Adélaide of Maurienne
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	29 August 1116(1116-08-29)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	13 October 1131 (aged 15)
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Saint Denis Basilica
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother, Louis, took over the throne after Philip died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The book, 'Kings & Queens of Europe'
