2 CONT Burke wrote:
2 CONT This nobleman, Hugh (Keveliok), 3rd Earl of Chester, joined in the rebellion of the Earl of Lancaster and the King of Scots against King Henry II, and in support of that monarch's son, Prince Henry's pretensions to the crown. In w
2 CONC hich proceeding he was taken prisoner with the Earl of Leicester at Alnwick, but obtained his freedom soon afterwards upon the king's reconciliation with the young prince. Again, however, hoisting the standard of revolt both in En
2 CONC gland and Normandy, with as little success, he was again seized and then detained a prisoner for some years. He eventually, however, obtained his liberty and restoration of his lands when public tranquility became completely reest
2 CONC ablished some time about the 23rd year of the king's reign. His lordship m. Bertred, dau. of Simon, Earl of Evereux, in Normandy, and had issue, I. Ranulph, his successor; I. Maud, m. to David, Earl of Huntingdon, brother of Willi
2 CONC am, King of Scotland, and had one son and four daus., viz., 1. John, surnamed le Scot, who s. to the Earldom of Chester, d. s. p. 7 June, 1237; 1. Margaret, m. to Alan de Galloway, and had a dau., Devorguilla, m. to John de Baliol
2 CONC , and was mother of John de Baliol, declared King of Scotland in the reign of Edward I; 2. Isabel, m. to Robert de Brus, and was mother of Robert de Brus, who contended for the crown of Scotland, temp. Edward I; 3. Maud, d. unm.; 
2 CONC Ada, m. to Henry de Hastings, one of the competitors for the Scottish crown, temp. Edward I; II. Mabill, m. to William de Albini, Earl of Arundel; III. Agnes, m. to William de Ferrers, Earl of Derby; IV. Hawise, m. to Robert, son 
2 CONC of Sayer de Quincy, Earl of Winchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl had another dau., whose legitimacy is questionable, namely, Amicia,* m. to Ralph de Mesnilwarin, justice of Chester, "a person," says Dugdale, "of very ancient family," from which union the Mainwarings, of Over Peover, in
2 CONC  the co. Chester, derive. Dugdale considers Amicia to be a dau. of the earl by a former wife. But Sir Peter Leicester, in his Antiquities of Chester, totally denies her legitimacy. "I cannot but mislike," says he, "the boldness an
2 CONC d ignorance of that herald who gave to Mainwaring (late of Peover), the elder, the quartering of the Earl of Chester's arms; for if he ought of right to quarter that coat, then must he be descended from a co-heir to the Earl of Ch
2 CONC ester; but he was not; for the co-heirs of Earl Hugh married four of the greatest peers in the kingdom."
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl d. at Leeke, in Staffordshire, in 1181, and was s. by his only son, Ranulph, surnamed Blundevil (or rather Blandevil) from the place of his birth, the town of Album Monasterium, modern Oswestry, in Powys), as 4th Earl of 
2 CONC Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Upon the question of this lady's legitimacy there was a long paper war between Sir Peter Leicester and Sir Thomas Mainwaring---and eventually the matter was referred to the judges, of whose decision Wood says, "at an assize held
2 CONC  at Chester, 1675, the controversy was decided by the justices itinerant, who, as I have heard, adjudged the right of the matter to Mainwaring." [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883
2 CONC , pp. 365-6, Meschines, Earls of Chester]ALIAS: Hugh de Meschines Earl of Chester
1 NOTE EARL OF CHESTER
1 NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 125, page 113; "Hugh of Kevelioc b 1147, Kevelioc, Co Monmouth d 1181 Leeke, Co Stafford, Earl of Chester, Vicomte d'Avranches in Normandy; m 1169 Bertrade, dau of Simon
2 CONC  de Montfort, Count of Evreux. (CP III 167; V 670 chart IV)." From line 127, page 113; "Hugh of KEVELIOC (125-28) Earl of Chester; m Bertrade de Montfort. (CP II 167)."
