2 NOTE There was an earlier discussion from quite some time ago, pointing out descrepancies in the coats of arms of the Earls of Chester in various locations in the city of Chester.  This is true. So to respond to that discussion I’ve pr
3 CONC epared images of these seven blazon with the proper description, as even that is different than the images discussed above.  
3 CONT List of the Earls of Chester
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT First Creation (1067–1070)
3 CONT
3 CONT •	Gerbod the Fleming, 1st Earl of Chester
3 CONT
3 CONT Second Creation (1071)
3 CONT
3 CONT 1071–1101 Hugh d'Avranches, 1st Earl of Chester (died 1101)[8][9] azure, a wolfs head erased
3 CONT 1101–1120 Richard d'Avranches, 2nd Earl of Chester (1094–1120) gules, 7 cross cross let’s Fitch or, a wolfs head erased
3 CONT 1120–1129 Ranulf le Meschin, 3rd Earl of Chester (died c. 1129) or, a lion rampant gules
3 CONT 1129–1153 Ranulf de Gernon, 4th Earl of Chester (died c. 1153) gules, a lion rampant argent
3 CONT 1153–1181 Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester (1147–1181) azure, 6 garbs or
3 CONT 1181–1232 Ranulf de Blondeville, 6th Earl of Chester (c. 1172–1232) azure, a garb or
3 CONT 1232–1232 Matilda of Chester, Countess of Chester suo jure (1171–1233) (Inherited Oct 1232  inter vivos gift to son Nov 1232)
3 CONT 1232–1237 John of Scotland, 7th Earl of Chester (c. 1207–1237) or, 3 piles gules
3 CONT (dates above are approximate)
3 CONT
3 CONT Third Creation (1254)
3 CONT
3 CONT •	Edward, Lord of Chester, but without the title of earl (1239–1307) (became King Edward I in 1272)
3 CONT
3 CONT Fourth Creation (1264)
3 CONT
3 CONT •	Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester, 1st Earl of Chester (1208–1265) (forfeit 1265)
3 CONT (There is no evidence that Alphonso, elder son of Edward I, was created earl of Chester, although he was styled as such)
3 CONT
3 CONT Fifth Creation (1301)
3 CONT
3 CONT •	Edward of Caernarvon, Earl of Chester (1284–1327) (became King Edward II in 1307)
3 CONT
3 CONT Sixth Creation (1312)
3 CONT
3 CONT •	Edward Plantagenet, Earl of Chester (1312–1377) (became King Edward III in 1327)
3 CONT Thereafter, the Earldom of Chester was created in conjunction with the Principality of Wales. See Prince of Wales for further Earls of Chester.
