2 CONT ''' MATILDA''' ([1171]-[6] Jan 1233).  The Annales Cestrienses record in 1171 the birth of “Matildis filia Hugonis comitis Cestrie”[151].  The accuracy of this date depends on the accuracy of the date of birth of her brother Ranul
2 CONC f, as discussed above.  The Annales Londonienses record that "Ranulphus comes Cestriæ" had four sisters, of whom "primogenita…Matilda" married "comiti David"[152].  Benedict of Peterborough records the marriage in 1190 of "David f
2 CONC rater Willelmi regis Scotiæ" and "sororem Ranulfi comitem Cestriæ"[153].  "Comes David frater regis Scottorum" founded Lindores Abbey, for the souls of "…Matilde comitisse sponse mee et…David filii mei", by undated charter (dated 
2 CONC to before 1203 from the names of the subscribers)[154].  The Testa de Nevill includes a writ of King John dated 1212 which records that "comes Cestrie" gave land "in Forthington et in Ulesbi" in Lincolnshire to "comiti Davidi in m
2 CONC aritagium cum sorore ipsius comitis"[155].  The Annales Cestrienses record the death “circa Epiphaniam Domini” 1233 of “Matildis mater comitis Johannis”[156].  m (26 Aug 1190[157]) [as his second wife,] DAVID of Scotland Earl of H
2 CONC untingdon, son of HENRY of Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon and Northumberland & his wife Ada de Warenne ([1144]-Yardley, Northants 17 Jun 1219, bur Sawtrey Abbey, Hunts). 
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maud_of_Chester '''From Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Maud of Chester,''' Countess of Huntingdon (1171- 6 January 1233)[1], was an Anglo-Norman noblewoman, sometimes known as Matilda de Kevelioc. She was a daughter of Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester, and the wife of David of
2 CONC  Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon. Through her daughter, Isobel, she was an ancestress of Robert the Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Maud was born in 1171, the eldest child of Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester and Bertrade de Montfort of Evreux, a cousin of King Henry II of England. Her paternal grandparents were Ranulf de Gernon and Maud of Gloucester
2 CONC , the granddaughter of King Henry I of England. Her maternal grandparents were Simon III de Montfort and Mahaut. Lady Maud had four siblings including Ranulf de Blondeville, 6th Earl of Chester, Mabel of Chester, Countess of Arund
2 CONC el, and Hawise of Chester, Countess of Lincoln. She also had an illegitimate half-sister, Amice of Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud's father died in 1181 when she was ten years of age. He had served in King Henry's Irish campaigns after his estates had been restored to him in 1177. They had been confiscated by the King as a result of his taking part in th
2 CONC e baronial Revolt of 1173-1174. His son Ranulf succeeded him as Earl of Chester, and Maud became a co-heiress of her brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dervorguilla of Galloway, a granddaughter of Maud of Chester
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 August 1190, she married David of Scotland, 8th Earl of Huntingdon, a Scottish prince, son of Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon, and a younger brother of Malcolm IV of Scotland and William I of Scotland. He was almos
2 CONC t thirty years Maud's senior. The marriage was recorded by Benedict of Peterborough.[2] David and Maud had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John of Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon (1207- 6 June 1237), married Elen ferch Llywelyn. He succeeded his uncle Ranulf as Earl of Chester in 1232, but he died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henry of Huntingdon (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert of Huntingdon (died young)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret of Huntingdon (c.1194- after 1 June 1233), married Alan, Lord of Galloway, by whom she had two daughters, including Dervorguilla of Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isobel of Huntingdon (1199- 1251), married Robert Bruce, 4th Lord of Annandale, by whom she had two sons, including Robert de Brus, 5th Lord of Annandale.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ada of Huntingdon, married Sir Henry de Hastings, by whom she had one son, Henry de Hastings, 1st Baron Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Matilda of Huntingdon
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud died on 6 January 1233 at the age of about sixty-two. Her husband had died in 1219. In 1290, upon the death of Margaret, Maid of Norway, which caused the extinction of the legitimate line of William I, the descendants of Davi
2 CONC d and Maud became the prime competitors for the crown of Scotland. Through their daughter, Isobel, they were the direct ancestors of the renowned Scottish King, Robert the Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband David had four illegitimate children by various mistresses.[2]References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Charles Cawley. "England, earls created 1067-1122". Medieval Lands. http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ a b Charles Cawley. "Kings of Scotland". Medieval Lands. http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT David of Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT David married Maude of Chester, daughter of Hugh de Kevelioc, 3rd Earl of Chester, by whom he had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT John, his successor as Earl
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, died young[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, died young[2];
2 CONT
2 CONT and four daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ada, married Henry de Hastings, father of Henry de Hastings, 1st Baron Hastings[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Isobel of Huntingdon
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Huntingdon.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT As commonly happened with medieval women, her name Maud was used interchangeably with Matilda, Mathilde, and often Mahaut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birth has also been shown to be 1171 and death in 1232. However, most records show the dates I recorded on the tree profile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Chester, Cheshire, England, and died in Abbey Saltre, Huntingtonshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage is reported to be August 26, 1190. 
2 CONT
