1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Hawise of Chester, 1st Countess of Lincoln==
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise of Chester, ''suo jure'' Countess of Lincoln
2 CONT * Spouse(s)	Robert de Quincy
2 CONT * Issue: Margaret de Quincy, 2nd Countess of Lincoln suo jure
2 CONT * Noble family	de Kevelioc
2 CONT * Father	Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester
2 CONT * Mother	Bertrade de Montfort of Evreux
2 CONT * Born	1180, Chester, Cheshire, England
2 CONT * Died	6 June 1241/3 May 1243
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise of Chester, 1st Countess of Lincoln ''suo jure'' (1180- 6 June 1241/3 May 1243[1]), was an Anglo-Norman noblewoman and a wealthy heiress. Her father was Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester. She was the sister and a co-hei
2 CONC ress of Ranulf de Blondeville, 6th Earl of Chester. She was created suo jure 1st Countess of Lincoln in 1232.[2] She was the wife of Robert de Quincy, by whom she had one daughter, Margaret, who became heiress to her title and est
2 CONC ates. She was also known as Hawise of Kevelioc.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise was born in 1180 in Chester, Cheshire, England, the youngest child of Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester and Bertrade de Montfort of Évreux, a cousin of King Henry II of England. Hawise had four siblings, including Maud 
2 CONC of Chester, Countess of Huntingdon, Mabel of Chester, Countess of Arundel, Agnes of Chester, Countess of Derby, and a brother Ranulf de Blondeville, 6th Earl of Chester.[3] She also had an illegitimate half-sister, Amice of Cheste
2 CONC r who married Ralph de Mainwaring, Justice of Chester by whom she had children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Ranulf de Gernon, 4th Earl of Chester, and Maud of Gloucester, the granddaughter of King Henry I of England, and her maternal grandparents were Simon III de Montfort and Mahaut.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1181, when Hawise was a year old, her father died. He had served in Henry II's Irish campaigns after his estates had been restored to him in 1177. They had been confiscated by the King as a result of his having taken part in th
2 CONC e baronial Revolt of 1173–1174. Her only brother Ranulf succeeded him as the 6th Earl of Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT She inherited the castle and manor of Bolingbroke, and other large estates from her brother to whom she was co-heiress after his death on 26 October 1232. Hawise had already became 1st Countess of Lincoln in April 1231, when her b
2 CONC rother Ranulf de Blondeville, 1st Earl of Lincoln resigned the title in her favour.[4] He granted her the title by a formal charter under his seal which was confirmed by King Henry III. She was formally invested as suo jure 1st Co
2 CONC untess of Lincoln by King Henry III on 27 October 1232 the day after her brother's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Less than a month later, in the same manner as her brother Ranulf de Blondeville, 1st Earl of Lincoln, she likewise made an inter vivos gift, after receiving dispensation from the crown, of the Earldom of Lincoln to her daughter M
2 CONC argaret de Quincy who then became 2nd Countess of Lincoln suo jureand her son-in-law John de Lacy, Baron of Pontefract who then became the 2nd Earl of Lincoln by right of his wife. (John de Lacy is mistakenly called the 1st Earl o
2 CONC f Lincoln in many references.) They were formally invested by King Henry III as Countess and Earl of Lincoln on 23 November 1232.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and issue===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before 1206, she married Robert de Quincy, son of Saer de Quincy, 1st Earl of Winchester and Margaret de Beaumont of Leicester. The marriage produced one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret de Quincy, 2nd Countess of Lincoln suo jure (c.1206 – March 1266), married firstly in 1221 John de Lacy, 2nd Earl of Lincoln by whom she had two children, Edmund de Lacy, Baron of Pontefract, and Maud de Lacy; she married
2 CONC  secondly on 6 January 1242 Walter Marshal, 5th Earl of Pembroke.
2 CONT Hawise's husband Robert died in 1217 in London. He had been accidentally poisoned through medicine prepared by a Cistercian monk.[6] Robert and his father had both been excommunicated in December 1215 as a result of the latter hav
2 CONC ing been one of the 25 sureties of the Magna Carta six months before. Hawise died sometime between 6 June 1241 and 3 May 1243. She was more than sixty years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawisse was married a second time to Sir Warren de Bostoke; they had a son, Sir Henry de Bostoke.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Sources: Burke's Landed Gentry (1847), vol. 1, p. 81; G. Ormerod, "History of the County Palatine and City of Chester" (1882), vol. 3, pp. 253, 259; J. P. Rylands, "The Visitation of Cheshire in the Year 1580", Harliean Soc., vol
2 CONC . 18, p. 27.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peerage of England Preceded by Ranulf de Blondeville	Countess of Lincoln suo jure 1231–1232	Succeeded by John de Lacy together with and by right of his wife Margaret de Quincy suo jure
2 CONT
