1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_de_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Henry de Montfort (November 1238 – 1265) was the son of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester, and with his father played an important role in the struggle of the barons against King Henry III. Henry's mother was Princess E
2 CONC leanor of England (daughter of King John), whose marriage to Simon further increased the foreign influence begun by the king, which was to result in great hostility by those very barons who later revolted against the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's father was Simon de Montfort, the leader of the English Barons in the Second Barons' War.  Simon was the younger son of Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester, a French noble who had been barred from his English titles a
2 CONC nd claims due to his allegiance to the French crown. Upon his father's death, the younger Simon had traded his interests in the family's French titles with his older brother for the sole possession of the English claims, and moved
2 CONC  to England in 1229 to assert them. Becoming a favorite of Henry III, the younger Simon de Montfort received permission to marry the king's sister Eleanor of England, a jump in social status that alarmed the English nobility. In g
2 CONC ratitude for the King's permission and support, the first son was named Henry in his honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second Barons' War
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite this relationship, Henry de Montfort sided with his father and the other nobles in revolt against his namesake known as the Second Barons' War, with his father emerging as the leader of the rebellion and eventually the de 
2 CONC facto leader of the nation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: battle of Lewes http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Lewes
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1264, Henry was one of the deputies sent to represent the barons at the Mise of Amiens. When the Mise was set aside he commanded a body of troops despatched to secure the Welsh border. On 28 February, he stormed and sac
2 CONC ked Worcester, and soon afterwards took Gloucester, but on Edward's approach he made a truce with him and retired to Kenilworth. With his brother Guy de Montfort, he led the van at the battle of Lewes, 14 May 1264. After the victo
2 CONC ry, on 28 May, he was made constable of Dover Castle, Warden of the Cinque Ports, and treasurer of Sandwich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Battle of Evesham http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Evesham
2 CONT
2 CONT When the siege of Gloucester began in 1263, Henry de Montfort and Humphrey de Bohun, holding the King and Prince Edward (later Edward I) as prisoners, spent two weeks fortifying the town and castle. Henry then accompanied his fath
2 CONC er to Evesham, where they intended to rendezvous with Henry's younger brother, Simon the younger de Montfort. Instead they met Prince Edward, who had ambushed the younger de Montfort's army and then marched under its stolen Montfo
2 CONC rt banners to lure Henry and the elder Simon into a trap.
2 CONT
2 CONT The final act was played out in a monstrous summer storm, (in) 'such a downpour of rain, such thunder and lightning, ...the darkness was so profound, that though it was dinner-time those who sat down to eat could scarcely see the 
2 CONC food before them'. The first of the nobles to fall in battle was Henry de Montfort, 'first born son and heir, in full view of his father, perished, split by a sword.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath for Henry's family
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus the whole weight of the battle fell upon the earl of Leicester, who was an old and shrewd warrior. He stood the shock like a strong tower; but, surrounded by few followers, and overcome by numbers, he fell, and thus terminate
2 CONC d an hereditary prowess, rendered famous by many glorious deeds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's brother Simon arrived at Evesham in time to see his father's head mounted on a spear. Another brother, Guy de Montfort, was captured during the battle and imprisoned. Guy later escaped and joined the younger Simon in fligh
2 CONC t to Europe. They found fortune in the service of Charles of Anjou, and later avenged the father and brother's deaths by killing Henry of Almain, the nephew of the King, and their own cousin. This act taking place in a church, the
2 CONC  brothers were excommunicated, which retarded sympathy for them in England. Simon died soon thereafter. As Count of Nola, Guy had two daughters, becoming in time an ancestor to several European Royal Families, including the Britis
2 CONC h. Another brother Amaury de Montfort also fled to Italy. A cleric, he worked in the papacy before accompanying his sister Eleanor de Montfort to Wales for her marriage to Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales. Captured by pirates
2 CONC  in the employ of King Edward, the siblings were eventually released, and Eleanor's marriage took place. Eleanor died on 19 June 1282 giving birth to Gwenllian of Wales. Amaury returned to the continent, worked as a cleric, and di
2 CONC ed sometime after 1301.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montfort-203
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Henry_de_Montfort
2 CONT https://www.royalline.uk/people/henry-de-montfort
2 CONT https://great-castles.com/montforthenry.html
