2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nature of request: Letters patent of Edward II stating that William Comyn acknowledged that he had no right at all in the manor of Whitwick in Leicestershire, which John Comyn, Earl of Buchan, his brother, held in chief of the Kin
2 CONC g, and that he surrendered it into the King's hands as the right and inheritance of Alice and Margaret, nieces and heirs of John Comyn; and that the King then gave it in wardship to Alice's husband, Henry de Beaumont, to hold unti
2 CONC l the majority of the heirs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Date derivation: Letters dated 16 July, 4 Edward II (1310)
2 CONT
2 CONT Catalogue reference: SC 8/297/14821 See notes under husband Henry.  Inquisition Post Mortem 415. Alice, late the wife of Henry de Bello Monte Writ, 20 July, 23 Edward III (1349) Lincoln. Inq. taken at Hekyngton, Wednesday after St
2 CONC . James, 23 Edw. III. Hekyngton. The manor and 40l. yearly rent from the manor of Barton held by way of dower of the king in chief by homage and service of one knight's fee... Thoresway, Lyndewode and Steveton. The manors, which E
2 CONC dward II gave to Henry de Bello Monte and the aforesaid Alice his wife and the heirs of the body of Henry, to hold by homage only, with reversion to the king. She was seised of no other lands &c. in the county. She died on Thursda
2 CONC y after the Translation of St. Thomas the Martyr last. The said Henry and Alice had a son John, their firstborn and heir, who died six years ago without heir of his body born within the realm of England or the allegiance of the ki
2 CONC ng of England. Thomas de Bello Monte, son of the said Henry and Alice, aged 24 years and more, is heir to the said Henry, Alice and John. Leicester. Inq. taken at Lughteburgh, 22 Jul., 23 Edw. III. Whitewyk. The manor. Shepeshed. 
2 CONC Two parts of the town.  Both held of her inheritance, of the king in chief by service of a quarter of a knight's fee. Lughteburgh. The manor, held jointly with Henry de Bello Monte, deceased, late her husband, of the king by his g
2 CONC ift and by his charter, of the forfeiture of Hugh le Despencer, service not known, to hold to the said Henry and Alice and the heirs of their bodies, with reversion to the king and his heirs.... She held no other lands &c. in the 
2 CONC county. She died as above. Heir as above, aged 24 years and more at the feast of St. Andrew last. C. Edw. III. File 104. (17.) E. Enrolments &c. of Inq. No. 64  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: Alice married Henry de BEAUMONT, Earl of Buchan, son of Louis de BRIENNE of Acre and Agnes de BEAUMONT, Vicomtesse de Beaumont-au-Maine, before 14 Jul 1310. (Henry de BEAUMONT was born in 1268 in Buchan, Aber
2 CONC deenshire, Scotland and died on 10 Mar 1339/40 in Buchan, Aberdeenshire, Scotland.)  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: UK National Archives; Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem, and other analogous documents, preserved in the Public Record Office, Vol. IX, Edward III, pp. 316-317
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice Comyn, Countess of Buchan 
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice Comyn, Countess of Buchan, Lady Beaumont (1289 '96  3 July 1349) was a Norman-Scottish noblewoman, a member of the powerful Comyn family  which supported the Balliols, claimants to the disputed Scottish throne against their 
2 CONC rivals, the Bruces. She was the niece of John Comyn, Earl of Buchan, to whom she was also heiress, and after his death the Earldom of Buchan was successfully claimed by her husband Henry de Beaumont, Earl of Buchan, by right of hi
2 CONC s wife. His long struggle to claim her Earldom of Buchan was one of the causes of the Second War of Scottish Independence.
2 CONT Alice was the maternal grandmother of Blanche of Lancaster, and thus great-grandmother of King Henry IV of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Alice was born in Aberdeenshire, Scotland, in 1289, the eldest daughter of Alexander Comyn, Sheriff of Aberdeen and his wife Joan le Latimer and the granddaughter of Alexander Comyn, Earl of Buchan. She had a younger sister, Marga
2 CONC ret, who would later marry firstly Sir John Ross, and secondly Sir William Lindsay, Lord of Symertoun.
2 CONT Alice's paternal grandparents were Alexander Comyn, 2nd Earl of Buchan, Justiciar  and Constable of Scotland, and Elizabeth de Quincy; and her maternal grandparents were William le Latimer and Alicia Ledet. Alice's uncle was John 
2 CONC Comyn, Earl of Buchan, one of the most powerful nobles in Scotland. The earl, who died in December 1308, was married to Isabella MacDuff, but the marriage was childless. Alice was John Comyn's heiress to the title of Countess of B
2 CONC uchan, although the earldom had been forfeited to the crown prior to her uncle's death in England to where he had gone as a fugitive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT Shortly before 14 July 1310, Alice married Henry de Beaumont, Lord Beaumont, the son of Louis de Brienne, Viscount de Beaumont and Agnes, Viscountess de Beaumont. Upon her marriage she was styled as Lady Beaumont. Henry was a key 
2 CONC figure in the Anglo-Scottish wars of the 13th and 14th centuries. (See main article: Wars of Scottish Independence) As a consequence of her marriage to Henry, Alice had become, in Scottish eyes, irretrievably English, therefore th
2 CONC e Scots recognised her sister Margaret's right to the Earldom of Buchan rather than her own.
2 CONT The marriage produced ten children:
2 CONT 	 Katherine de Beaumont (died 11 November 1368), married David III Strathbogie, Earl of Atholl, by whom she had issue. 
2 CONT 	Elizabeth de Beaumont (died 27 October 1400), married Nicholas Audley, 3rd Baron Audley. Died without issue. 
2 CONT 	Richard de Beaumont 
2 CONT 	John de Beaumont (died young) 
2 CONT 	Thomas de Beaumont 
2 CONT 	Alice de Beaumont 
2 CONT 	Joan de Beaumont, married Sir Fulk FitzWarin, 3rd Lord FitzWarin 
2 CONT 	Beatrice de Beaumont, married Charles I, Count of Dammartin 
2 CONT 	John de Beaumont, 2nd Lord Beaumont (1318- 14 April 1342), on 6 November 1330 married as her first husband, Eleanor of Lancaster, by whom he had issue. He was killed in a tournament. 
2 CONT 	Isabel de Beaumont (c.1320- 1361), married in 1337 Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster, by whom she had two daughters, Maud, Countess of Leicester  and Blanche of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Buchan
2 CONT In April 1313, Isabella MacDuff, the widow of Alice's uncle John Comyn, was placed into the custody of the Beaumonts, following her release from her harsh imprisonment. She had been confined in a cage for four years in Berwick, En
2 CONC gland by the orders of King Edward I after she crowned Robert the Bruce king of Scotland at Scone in March 1306. In 1310, she was sent to a convent, and three years later was ordered to one of the Beaumont manors where she died on
2 CONC  an unknown date.
2 CONT In 1314, Henry de Beaumont fought at the Battle of Bannockburn  on the side of the English.
2 CONT Sometime between 1317 and 1321, Alice succeeded to the English estates of her younger sister, Margaret.
2 CONT On 22 January 1334, Alice's husband Henry was summoned to Parliament of England  as the Earl of Buchan. He was recognised as earl from that date until 16 November 1339. On 10 February 1334, he sat in the Scottish Parliament bearin
2 CONC g the same title. It was Henry's relentless pursuit of Alice's inherited earldom of Buchan which was one of the factors that lead to the Second War of Scottish Independence  between the Comyns and their ancient rivals, the Bruces.
2 CONT Alice died on 3 July 1349 at the age of sixty. Her husband Henry had died in 1340 in the Low Countries  where he had gone with King Edward III of England. With the death of Alice, the earldom of Buchan forever passed out of the Co
2 CONC myn family.
2 CONT Alice's numerous descendants included, Kings Henry IV of England  and Henry V of England, Philippa of Lancaster, Anne Boleyn, and Humphrey Kynaston, the English highwayman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In fiction
2 CONT Alice Comyn appears as a character in Barbara Erskine's novel Kingdom of Shadows, which is based upon the life of Isabella MacDuff.
2 CONT
