2 CONT *Louise de Montmorency (1496–1547) was a French noblewoman from the ancient House of Montmorency.
2 CONT *She was the younger sister of Anne de Montmorency, Constable of France, and the mother of Gaspard de Coligny, Admiral of France.
2 CONT *Louise married her first husband, Ferri de Mailly, in 1511.[1] This marriage produced a daughter, '''Madeleine de Mailly'''. Ferry died in 1513, and Louise remarried in 1514 to Gaspard I de Coligny. From her second marriage she h
2 CONC ad three sons, all of whom played important roles in the first period of the French Wars of Religion: Odet, Cardinal de Châtillon; Gaspard, the Admiral; and François, Seigneur d'Andelot.
2 CONT *She had considerable patronage power independently of her husband,[2][3] and had an important role in spreading the influence of Calvinism in France in the 16th Century.[4]
2 CONT *Notes
2 CONT *1.^ [1]
2 CONT * 2.^ "The patronage power of early modern French noblewomen" by Sharon Kettering. The Historical Journal, Vol. 32, No. 4 (Dec., 1989), pp. 817-841 JSTOR
2 CONT * 3.^ "The Montmorencys and the Abbey of Sainte Trinité, Caen: Politics, Profit and Reform" by Joan Davies The Journal of Ecclesiastical History (2002), 53 : 665-685 doi:10.1017/S002204690200427X
2 CONT * 4.^ "The Development of Protestantism in 16th Century France" by Graham Noble; History Review, 2002 Questia link
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louise_de_Montmorency _______________________
