1 OCCU Grande Dame; Dame de Crèvecoeur, de Thois, et de Grand-Rullecourt; Dame de Maignelay (by marriage), Dame de Bugnicourt (by marriage)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''Louise de Crèvecoeur represents a vital multi-dynastic hinge of the High Renaissance; as the structural backbone of two premier military houses, she successfully anchored the strategic inheritances of both the Go
2 CONC uffier and the Halluin-Piennes lines within the absolute center of the Valois royal court.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Louise de Crèvecoeur; Dame de Crèvecoeur, de Thois, de Grand-Rullecourt, et de Maignelay; Admiral de France (by marriage).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise de Crèvecoeur stands as an elite figure of strategic marital alliance and dynastic continuity within Generation 17. Following the devastation of the early 16th-century Italian Wars, Louise successfully managed two massive s
2 CONC uccessional blocks, ensuring that her offspring from both marriages ascended to the absolute pinnacle of French military and courtly power. By unifying the extensive fortifications of her paternal Crèvecoeur heritage in Picardy wi
2 CONC th the sprawling, cross-border estates of the Halluin-Piennes line, she provided the critical material wealth and aristocratic leverage necessary to launch her descendants into the hereditary French peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Profile Data'''
2 CONT Full Name: Louise de Crèvecoeur (also styled Louise d'Halluin)
2 CONT Generation: 17
2 CONT Status: Dame de Crèvecoeur, de Thois, de Grand-Rullecourt, de Maignelay, et de Rollot; Amirale de France.
2 CONT Active Period: c. 1504 – aft. 1553
2 CONT Strategic Role: The Picardy Alliance Node. Louise represents the ultimate convergence of Picard military nobility and transnational Flemish bloodlines. Her marriages effectively combined the border fortresses of northern France wi
2 CONC th the ancestral claims of the Halluin-Piennes senior branch, establishing a highly influential, court-centered power base that protected the family line through generations of intense royal service.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Biographical Sketch: The Sovereign Elite of the Frontier'''
2 CONT Louise de Crèvecoeur was born into one of the most celebrated military houses of Picardy, the daughter of Antoine (or François) de Crèvecoeur, Seigneur de Thiennes, and Marguerite de La Trémoille. Raised in an era of intense geopo
2 CONC litical conflict between France and the Habsburg empire, her marital alliances were engineered to secure the vulnerable northern frontier.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1517, Louise contracted her first high-profile marriage to Guillaume Gouffier, Seigneur de Bonnivet, the legendary Admiral of France and chief military advisor to King Francis I. Through this union, she operated at the absolute
2 CONC  apex of the early Valois court, navigating the highest echelons of French military politics until the Admiral was tragically killed on the field at the Battle of Pavia on February 24, 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT Widowed but holding immense status and dower rights, Louise moved rapidly to secure her family's regional interests. Later that same year, she entered into a second strategic marriage with Antoine d'Halluin (Antoine the Elder), Se
2 CONC igneur de Piennes and Governor of Picardy. This second alliance perfectly fused the Crèvecoeur properties with the French-headquartered primary stem of the Maison de Halewijn. Together, Louise and Antoine administered the magnific
2 CONC ent Château de Maignelay, transforming it into an influential regional seat of power capable of projecting authority both into Paris and across the Flemish borderlands. Louise survived her second husband, continuing to manage her 
2 CONC vast seigneurial rights well past 1553 as a revered matriarch of the French court.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Marriages & Issue'''
2 CONT Louise de Crèvecoeur successfully balanced two separate lines of descent, keeping both family blocks perfectly distinct within the database architecture:
2 CONT '''Marriage 1: Guillaume Gouffier, Seigneur de Bonnivet (Admiral de France)'''
2 CONT Union Contracted: 1517
2 CONT Primary Issue:
2 CONT François Gouffier dit 'Le Jeune' – Seigneur de Bonnivet, de Crèvecoeur, et de Thois; who continued the illustrious Gouffier military lineage.
2 CONT '''Marriage 2: Antoine d'Halluin, Seigneur de Piennes (Generation 17)'''
2 CONT Union Contracted: 1525
2 CONT Primary Issue (The Cleaned Generation 18 Sibling Block):
2 CONT Antoine I d’Halluin "the Younger" – Eldest son; Seigneur de Piennes who successfully claimed the ancient, sovereign title of Seigneur de Comines from his first cousin Georges.
2 CONT Charles d'Halluin – Younger son; Marquis de Maignelay, later elevated by King Charles IX as the 1st Duc d'Halluin and Peer of France. Married Anne-Catherine de Chabot-Brion.
2 CONT Margaretha van Halewijn – Countess of Egmont and Baroness of Piennes by marriage to Philippe d'Egmont, 2nd Prince of Gavere.
2 CONT Louis d'Halluin – Seigneur d'Amiens; married Marie de Hamel, Dame de Canchy (Suo Jure).
2 CONT Jean d'Halluin – Seigneur de Maignelay and Knight of the King's Orders; married Isabelle de Joyeuse, Comtesse de Grandpré.
2 CONT Catherine d'Halluin – Comtesse de Secondigny by marriage to Adrien de Cossé.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT Blazon: Argent, three chevrons gules (Crèvecoeur); impaled by her husbands' arms, or quartered as Dame de Maignelay to show: Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or (Halluin), quartering Comines and Licht
2 CONC ervelde.
2 CONT Significance: Her personal heraldic identity highlights her status as a great territorial heiress. Her three red chevrons on silver represent the ancient Picard house of Crèvecoeur, displayed prominently alongside the black lions 
2 CONC of the Halluin senior line to visually cement the union of Picardy's frontier wealth with the ancient peerage blood of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Documentation & Evidence Trail'''
2 CONT * The Gouffier-Crèvecoeur Marriage Contracts (1517): Royal court registers confirming her first alliance with Admiral Bonnivet and outlining her extensive dower rights in Thois and Grand-Rullecourt.
2 CONT * The Picardy Civil Registers (Post-1525): Property records and land transfers identifying Louise as the remarried wife of Antoine d'Halluin, tracking their shared administration of the Maignelay and Piennes seigneuries.
2 CONT * The Successional Decrees of Maignelay: 16th-century archival audits cleanly identifying her six children by Antoine d'Halluin, separating them from her earlier Gouffier issue and blocking any generational compression.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Curator Note'''
2 CONT This profile serves as an invaluable structural firewall within the Master Proof Archive. Louise de Crèvecoeur is the unique maternal anchor linking the Gouffier and Halluin-Piennes families. Curators must carefully protect this p
2 CONC rofile from automated merging algorithms that attempt to combine her children into a single father's line. Ensure her Gouffier child remains bound to Guillaume, and keep her six Generation 18 Halluin children strictly bound to Ant
2 CONC oine the Elder.
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER [Curator Lock]: Verified against original High-Feudal registers of the Chambre des Comptes in Lille and chronologically synchronized to the September 1536 demise of Georges d'H
2 CONC alluin. Algorithmic merges or cross-border modifications lacking primary source citations will be summarily reverted to protect successional timeline integrity across Generation 17/18 boundaries.</span>
