2 DATE ABT 1570
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00457134&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY Tanneguy de Warignies, seigneur de Blainville, was the son of Jacques de Warignies, seigneur de Blainville, la Potterrie et Bléville, and Adrienne Martel, dame de la Potterrie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 January 1596 Tanneguy married Antoinette du Parc, daughter of François du Parc, marquis des Cresnays, and Marie Le Prévost, dame et baronne des Biards. Their three daughters Jeanne, Adrienne and Madeleine would have progeny. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Antoinette's unmarried brother Jacques died, she became dame et baronne des Biards, des Cherits, Monfarville, Rocher Bellefontaine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tanneguy enjoyed a distinguished career at the court of France. He was a member of the king's privy council and council of state, as well as captain of fifty men-at-arms, a gentleman of the king's chamber, the king's lieutenant of
2 CONC  the bailiwick of Caen and governor of Lectoure. However his career was overshadowed by that of his elder brother Jean, seigneur de Blainville. When Jean died without progeny in Issy on 26 February 1628, leaving his widow Catherin
2 CONC e de Voysins as well as a superb collection of paintings in his Parisian mansion, he also left considerable debts. Tanneguy became his heir to whatever assets remained after debts had been repaid.
