1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00024660&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_de_Pisseleu_d%27Heilly
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Jeanne de Pisseleu d'Heilly, Duchess of Étampes (1508 – 1580), was the mistress of Francis I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Adrien de Pisseleu, seigneur d'Heilly, a nobleman of Picardy, who, with the rise of his daughter at court, was made seigneur of Meudon, Master of waters and forests of Île de France, of Champagne and of Brie.
2 CONT
2 CONT King's mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT She came to court before 1522 and was one of the maids-of-honour of Louise of Savoy, Duchess of Angoulême, the mother of Francis I. Francis made Anne his mistress, probably upon his return from his captivity at Madrid (1526), and 
2 CONC soon gave up his long-term mistress, Françoise de Foix, for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was described as being sprightly, pretty, witty and cultured, "the most beautiful among the learned and the most learned among the beautiful"; she succeeded in keeping the favour of the king until his death in 1547. The liais
2 CONC on received some official recognition; when the new Queen of France, Eleanor of Austria, entered Paris in 1530, the King and Anne occupied the same window. In 1533, Francis gave her in marriage to Jean IV de Brosse, whom he create
2 CONC d Duke of Étampes.
2 CONT
2 CONT The influence of the Duchess of Étampes, especially in the last years of the reign, was considerable. She upheld Admiral Philippe de Chabot against the Constable de Montmorency, who was supported by her rival courtesan Diane de Po
2 CONC itiers, the dauphin's mistress. She was a friend to new ideas, tolerant of Protestants, whose beliefs she openly embraced after the King's death. She co-operated with the King's sister, Marguerite de Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT She used her influence to elevate and enrich her family, her uncle, Antoine Sanguin (d. 1559), being made Bishop of Orléans in 1533 and a cardinal in 1539; her three brothers were made bishops and two sisters were abbesses, the ot
2 CONC her sisters making great marriages. The accusations made against her of having allowed herself to be won over by Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, and of playing the traitor in 1544 and in the lead-up to the Treaty of Crépy (Septembe
2 CONC r 1545) rest on no serious proof.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Francis's death, Diane de Poitiers, mistress of Henry II of France, had the Duchess dismissed from the court. Though her creatures at court were humiliated in every way upon her dismissal, she was permitted to die in obs
2 CONC curity much later, probably in the reign of Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Anna Jeanne de Pisseleu d'Heilly, Duchess of Étampes (1508 – 1580), was the mistress of Francis I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Adrien de Pisseleu, seigneur d'Heilly, a nobleman of Picardy, who, with the rise of his daughter at court, was made seigneur of Meudon, Master of waters and forests of Île de France, of Champagne and of Brie.
2 CONT
2 CONT King's mistress[edit] She came to court before 1522 and was one of the maids-of-honour of Louise of Savoy, Duchess of Angoulême, the mother of Francis I.[1] Francis made Anne his mistress, probably upon his return from his captivi
2 CONC ty at Madrid (1526), and soon gave up his long-term mistress, Françoise de Foix, for her.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was described as being sprightly, pretty, witty and cultured, "the most beautiful among the learned and the most learned among the beautiful"; she succeeded in keeping the favour of the king until his death in 1547. The liais
2 CONC on received some official recognition; when the new Queen of France, Eleanor of Austria, entered Paris in 1530, the King and Anne occupied the same window. In 1534, Francis gave her in marriage to Jean IV de Brosse, whom he create
2 CONC d Duke of Étampes.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sketch of Anne by François Clouet. The influence of the Duchess of Étampes, especially in the last years of the reign, was considerable.[3] She upheld Admiral Philippe de Chabot against the Constable de Montmorency, who was suppor
2 CONC ted by her rival Diane de Poitiers, the dauphin's mistress. She was a friend to new ideas, tolerant of Protestants, whose beliefs she openly embraced after the King's death. She cooperated with the King's sister, Marguerite de Nav
2 CONC arre.
2 CONT
2 CONT She used her influence to elevate and enrich her family,[4] her uncle, Antoine Sanguin (d. 1559), being made Bishop of Orléans in 1533 and a cardinal in 1539;[4] her three brothers were made bishops[4] and two sisters were abbesse
2 CONC s, the other sisters making great marriages. The accusations made against her of having allowed herself to be won over by Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, and of playing the traitor in 1544 and in the lead-up to the Treaty of Crépy 
2 CONC (September 1545) rest on no serious proof.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Francis's death, Diane de Poitiers, mistress of Henry II of France, had the Duchess dismissed from the court. Though her court favorites were humiliated in every way upon her dismissal, she was permitted to die in obscur
2 CONC ity much later, probably in the reign of Henry III.
