1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_II,_Baron_of_Vitré
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew II of Vitré (c. 1150 – 9 June 1211[1]) was Baron de Vitré from 1173 to 1210/11.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Andrew II of Vitré was the eldest son of Robert III, Baron of Vitré and his wife Emma of Dinan, daughter of Alan of Dinan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1173. He had already fought with King Henry II Plantagenet in 1168.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1180s, he took part to an armed pilgrimage to the Holy Land and came back to Brittany a few years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1185, he was one of the noblemen who were present at the Assise au Comte Geoffroy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1196, the duchess of Brittany, Constance, was abducted and imprisoned by her husband Ranulf de Blondeville. Andrew rebelled along with several other Breton barons. He agreed to send his daughter Emma, who was his only heiress a
2 CONC t the time, as an hostage to Richard the Lionheart, in exchange for de Constance's release. Emma and the other hostages were taken away but the duchess was not released.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and issue
2 CONT Andrew II of Vitré married four times:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He first married, before 1173, Matilda of Mayenne, daughter of Geoffrey III, Lord of Mayenne, and Constance of Cornouaille (daughter of Duke Conan III, Duke of Brittany). They had no issue and the marriage was annulled in 1189 or 
2 CONC 1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly Enoguen of Léon, daughter of Guihomar IV, Viscount of Léon and his wife Nobilis. They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma, who was hostage in August 1196 and married Alard IV, Lord of Château-Gontier in 1207;
2 CONT Eleanor, who married John of Dol, Lord of Combourg in 1210.
2 CONT Enoguen died at an unknown date and Andrew married Eustacie of Rays, daughter of Harscoët of Rays on 3 March 1199. They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew III, who succeeded his father and married Catherine of Thouars, daughter of Constance, Duchess of Brittany and Guy of Thouars;
2 CONT Constance, who married Alan of Coëtmen, Viscount of Tonquédec, around 1225.
2 CONT Eustacie died before 7 December 1209 and Andrew soon married Luce Paynel, sister of Fulk Paynel. They had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Judith Everard & Michael Jones The Charters of Duchess Constance and her Family, 1171-1221 (1999), The Boydell Press, pp 195/96
2 CONT  Judith Everard & Michael Jones The Charters of Duchess Constance and her Family, 1171-1221 (1999), The Boydell Press, pp 195-196.
