2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Alan de Bretagne, 1st Earl of Richmond)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan of Penthièvre (b.c.1100 – September 15, 1146, ( Breton Alan Penteur ) also known as Alan the Black, was a Breton noble who fought for Stephen of England (before 1100 – September 15, 1146)  Alan was the third son of Stephen, C
2 CONC ount of Tréguier.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a politically arranged marriage Alan married Bertha of Brittany, daughter of Conan III, Duke of Brittany, perhaps in a move to draw Brittany onto Stephen's side in the conflict against the dispossessed Empress Matilda. In part 
2 CONC to promote the alliance with Conan III of Brittany Stephen made Conan's son-in-law Alan as 1st Earl of Richmond in the second creation. The title Earl of Richmond had been held by Alan's uncle Alan 'The Red, who had died heireless
2 CONC . Additionally, Stephen recognized Alan as Earl of Cornwall, in recognition of his other uncle Brian who had formerly possessed Cornwall, but was deprived of it by Ranulph de Gernon, 2nd Earl of Chester after the Battle of Lincoln
2 CONC  (1141).
2 CONT
2 CONT With Bertha of Brittany they had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Conan IV, Duke of Brittany, their son and heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance, who married Alan III, Vicomte of Rohan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enougen, abbess of St. Sulpice.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, Alan had four illegitimate sons[3]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bryan FitzAlan, progenitor of the Lords of Bedale in Richmondshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen FitzAlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reynold FitzAlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert FitzAlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan died in Brittany by 1146 and was buried at Bégard. After his death, Bertha returned to Brittany. There, on his surprise death-bed, Conan III renounced Hoèl as his son and heir and designated Bertha as his heiress. When he die
2 CONC d, Bertha inherited the Duchy of Brittany. As the dowager Countess of Richmond, Bertha continued to represent the alliance between Brittany and Stephan's England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chester's grandson was made Duke of Brittany jure uxoris with Constance, Duchess of Brittany, which made him automatically Earl of Richmond.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Cockayne, G. E., edited by H. A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, & Lord Howard de Walden, The Complete Peerage, London, 1945, vol.x, p.788.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Cockayne et al (1945) vol.x, p.791n, where it states that he was born before his younger brother Henry, who was born in 1100.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Cockayne et al (1945) vol.x, p.790n
2 CONT
