2 CONT 1.  Count of Meulan Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N183 Unknown GEDCOM info: 0D98E650-18C2-49E9-A95A-70EDD7575FDF
1 NOTE
2 CONT Very powerful under Henry I. 1st Earl of Leicester by charter of creation, 1103. Companion of William the Conqueror at Hastings in 1066. Lord of Beaumont, Pont-audemer, Brionne, and Meulan . Robert de Bellomont came into England w
2 CONC ith the Conqueror, and contributed mainly to the Norman triumph at Hastings. This Robert inherited the Earldom of Meullent in Normandy, from his mother Adeliza de Waleran. Of his conduct at Hastings it is said: "A certain Norman y
2 CONC oung soldier, making his first onset in that fight, did what deserved lasting fame, boldly charging and breaking in upon the enemy, with that regiment which he commanded in the right wing of the army."  For these gallant services 
2 CONC he obtained sixty-four lordships in Warwickshire, and many others in Leicester, Wilts, Northampton, Gloucester, in all ninety-one. His lordship did not, however, arrive at the dignity of the English peerage before the reign of Hen
2 CONC ry I, when that monarch created him Earl of Leicester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Bellomont came into England with the Conqueror, and contributed mainly to the Norman triumph at Hastings. This Robert inherited the Earldom of Meullent in Normandy, from his mother Adeliza de Waleran. Of his conduct at H
2 CONC astings it is said: "A certain Norman young soldier, making his first onset in that fight, did what deserved lasting fame, boldly charging and breaking in upon the enemy, with that regiment which he commanded in the right wing of 
2 CONC the army." For these gallant services he obtained sixty-four lordships in Warwickshire, and many others in Leicester, Wilts, Northampton, Gloucester, in all ninety-one. His lordship did not, however, arrive at the dignity of the E
2 CONC nglish peerage before the reign of Henry I, when that monarch created him Earl of Leicester.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources are in a great deal of confusion here; they sometimes have this Robert marrying a second wife Amice de Montfort, whereas sometimes his son Robert marries her; if Amice is this Roberts wife, then her and Elizabeth's chi
2 CONC ldren's birthdates overlap in an impossible fashion. Son Robert is sometimes shown wed to Amicia De La Ware, instead of Amice de Montfort. I have no way at present of verifying what is correct, so I have melded records together as
2 CONC  best I can.
1 NOTE Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester and Count of Meulan (1049 - 5 June 1118) was a powerful English and French nobleman, revered as one of the wisest men of his age. Chroniclers speak highly of his eloquence, his learning, a
2 CONC nd three kings of England valued his counsel.
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied William the Conqueror to England in 1066, where his service earned him more than 91 lordships and manors. When his mother died in 1081, Robert inherited the title of Count of Meulan in Normandy, also the title of Vi
2 CONC scount Ivry and Lord of Norton. He did homage to Philip I of France for these estates and sat as French Peer in the Parliament held at Poissy.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Hastings Robert was appointed leader of the infantry on the right wing of the army.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his brother Henry were members of the Royal hunting party in the New Forest, when William Rufus received his mysterious death wound, 2 August 1100. He then pledged alligience to William Rufus' brother, Henry I of England, w
2 CONC ho created him Earl of Leicester in 1107.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of William Rufus, William, Count of Evreux and Ralph de Conches made an incursion into Robert's Norman estates, on the pretence that they had suffered injury through some advice that Robert had given to the King; thei
2 CONC r raid was very successful for they collected a vast booty.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Henry of Huntingdon, Robert died of shame after "a certain earl carried off the lady he had espoused, either by some intrigue or by force and stratagem." His wife Isabella remarried in 1118 to William de Warenne, 2nd 
2 CONC Earl of Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Roger de Beaumont and Adeline of Meulan, daughter of Waleran III, Count de Meulan, and an older brother of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1096 he married (Isabel) Elizabeth de Vermandois, daughter of Hugh Magnus and a scion of the French royal family. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma de Beaumont (born 1102) 
2 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester (born 1104) 
2 CONT Waleran IV de Beaumont, Count of Meulan (born 1104) 
2 CONT Hugh de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Bedford (born c. 1106) 
2 CONT Adeline de Beaumont, married two times: 
2 CONT Hugh IV of Montfort-sur-Risle; 
2 CONT Richard de Granville of Bideford (d. 1147) 
2 CONT Aubree de Beaumont, married Hugh II of Château-neuf-Thimerais. 
2 CONT Maud de Beaumont, married William Lovel. (b. c. 1102) 
2 CONT Isabel de Beaumont, a mistress of King Henry I of England. Married two times: 
2 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 1st Earl of Pembroke; 
2 CONT Hervé de Montmorency, Constable of Ireland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 50-24, 50-25, 53-24, 53-25, 66-25, 114-29, 140-24, 184-4, 215-24, 215-25 
2 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxford. 
