1 NAME Reinoud II, graaf van Gelre
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1295
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 OCT 1343
2 ADDR
3 CITY Arnhem
3 STAE Gelderland
3 CTRY Netherlands
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Died: 12 October 1343 Buried: Monnikhuizen Arnhem
2 CONT ----
2 CONT On 11 January 1311, Reinald was married to Sophia de Berthout at Roermond; she was the heiress of Malines and niece of the Bishop of Utrecht, and bore him four daughters. Two of them, Matilda and Marie, succeeded as Duchess of Gel
2 CONC derland in their own right, and Elisabeth was Abbess of Gravendaal/Graefenthal. Sophie de Berthout died in 1329.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reinald II officially succeeded his father on 9 October 1326. However, he had declared his father unfit to rule in 1316, and imprisoned him in 1318. In May 1332, now in his forties, he married Edward II’s elder daughter Eleanor of
2 CONC  Woodstock at Nijmegen. Eleanor was not yet fourteen (born June 1318). She had previously been considered as a bride for King Alfonso XI of Castile and the future King Jean II of France, so marriage to Reinald wasn’t a brilliant m
2 CONC atch, especially considering her younger sister Joan was Queen of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Professor Roy Martin Haines, in his ''King Edward II'', recounts the legend that a non-murdered Edward II was a furtive guest at his daughter’s wedding. There was much rejoicing almost exactly a year later when Eleanor, not yet fi
2 CONC fteen, gave birth to a son, also Reinoud (born 13 May 1333). He was followed on 12 March 1336 by a second son, named Eduard after his grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1339, the county of Gelre was ‘upgraded’ to a duchy, in honour of Reinald’s importance in European politics and the Hundred Years War. Emperor Louis of Bavaria granted Reinoud II in 1339 the title of Duke. In the meantime the p
2 CONC ower centre of the County/Duchy had moved northwards and for that reason Reinoud and his wife Eleonora founded the Carthusian monastery of Monnikhuizen, near Arnhem. This also became the burial site of the next few generations of 
2 CONC the Dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reinoud II died in Arnhem on 12 October 1243 and was succeeded by his elder son Reinald III. Eleanor of Woodstock died on 22 April 1355, not yet thirty-seven, and was buried in Deventer Abbey, which she’d founded. As her two sons 
2 CONC died childless, both in 1371, two of her stepdaughters succeeded as Duchess of Gelderland.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reginald_II,_Duke_of_Guelders#Family EN: '''Reginald II, Duke of Guelders''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ======Curriculum VITAE
2 CONT * 1339 : Graaf van GELRE-nld
2 CONT * 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
