1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jeanne de Fougères, Countess of La Marche and of Angoulême 
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Suo jure Dame de Fougères Countess of La Marche and Angoulême Lady of Lusignan, Couhe, and Peyrat		
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse(s) Hugh XII of Lusignan, Count of La Marche and Angoulême, seigneur of Lusignan, Couhe, and Peyrat		
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue  Yolande de Lusigna , Countess of La Marche Hugh XIII of Lusignan, Count of La Marche and Angoulême Guy I of Lusignan  Jeanne of Lusignan  Isabelle de Lusignan  Marie de Lusignan		
2 CONT
2 CONT Noble family de Fougères		
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Raoul III, seigneur of Fougères		
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Isabelle de Craon 		
2 CONT
2 CONT Born Unknown Château de Fougères, Brittany, France		
2 CONT
2 CONT Died After 1273		
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne de Fougères, suo jure  Lady of Fougères, Countess of La Marche and Angoulême (died after 1273), was a Breton  noblewoman and heiress. She was the wife of Hugh XII of Lusignan, Count of La Marche  and Count of Angoulême. Jea
2 CONC nne was responsible for the later additions and fortifications of the Chateau of Fougères  which provided a greater stability for the town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne was born at the Chateau of Fougères, in Brittany on an unknown date, the only daughter and surviving child of Raoul III, seigneur of Fougères and Isabelle de Craon (born 1212). Her paternal grandparents were Geoffrey, seign
2 CONC eur of Fougères and Mathilde de Porhoet, and her maternal grandparents were Amaury I, seigneur of Craon (1175-1226) and Jeanne des Roches  (c. 1195- 28 September 1238), daughter of Guillaume des Roches , seneschal of Anjou , and M
2 CONC arguerite de Sablé .
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne had a brother Jean de Fougères, but he died immediately after his birth on 6 December 1230. As no more sons were born to her mother, Jeanne became the heiress to her father's lordship  of Fougères, which she inherited suo j
2 CONC ure upon the death of her father on 24 February 1256. She thereafter held the title of Dame de Fougères. That same year 1256, she issued orders for the expansion of the chateau of Fougères, adding the Melusine and Gobelin towers a
2 CONC s well as fortifying the ramparts  and gates. Her efforts provided a greater stability for the town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 January 1254, Jeanne was married to Hugh XII de Lusignan, seigneur of Lusignan, Couhe, and Peyrat, Count of La Marche and Angoulême. She became the Countess of La Marche and Angoulême upon her marriage, which was recorded in
2 CONC  the Chronicon Savigniacense on 4 February 1254.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and Jeanne together had six children:
2 CONT 	Yolande de Lusignan  (24 March 1257- 30 September 1314), suo jure Countess of La Marche, Heiress of Fougères, married firstly Helie-Rudel, seigneur of Pons, by whom she had issue; and secondly Robert, seigneur of Mathe. 
2 CONT 	Hugh XIII of Lusignan , Count of La Marche and Angoulême (25 June 1259- 1 November 1303), on 1 April 1276 married Beatrice of Burgundy. Their marriage was childless. 
2 CONT 	Guy I de Lusignan  (died 1308), Count of La Marche and Angoulême, died unmarried and without legitimate issue. 
2 CONT 	Jeanne of Lusignan  (1260- 13 April 1323), married firstly Bernard Ezi III, Lord of Albret , by whom she had two daughters; she married secondly Sir Piers de Geneville of Trim  and Ludlow , by whom she had another three daughters
2 CONC , including the eldest Joan de Geneville, who in her turn married Roger de Mortimer, 1st Earl of March . 
2 CONT 	Isabelle de Lusignan (died 1309), before 1288 in Cognac  married Jean de Vesci. She later became a nun at Fontevrault Abbey  
2 CONT 	Marie de Lusignan (died 1322), in 1288 married Etienne II, Count of Sancerre.
2 CONT  Shortly after 25 August 1270, Jeanne became a widow when her husband Hugh was killed while on Crusade  with King Louis IX of France . Jeanne was granted the wardship of her minor children in Nov. 1271. She died on an unknown date
2 CONC  after 1273. She left a will dated 20 May 1269. She was buried in Savigny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Jeanne's death, her eldest daughter Yolanda became the Heiress of Fougères.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT 	Genealogist n.s. 21 (1905): 78-82, 163-171, 234-243. 
2 CONT 	La Porta, Les Gens de Qualité en Basse-Marche 1(2) (1886): 1-60 (Généalogie de Lusignan). 
2 CONT 	Schwennicke, Europäische Stammtafeln  n.s. 3(4) (1989): 816 (sub Lusignan). 
2 CONT 	Cawley, Charles, Brittany, Dukes and Nobility, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy,  Cawley, Charles, France, Southwest, Angoulême, La Marche, and Périgord, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy. 
2 CONT
