1 NOTE King John
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=2569f6a3-2593-4df3-8525-bad121cac414&tid=10145763&pid=-420525822
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'The World Book Encyclopedia', 1968, p J110. 'Royalty forommoners', Roderick W. Stuart, 1993, p 38.: Reigned 1199-1216. SignedMagna Carta in 1215 at Runnymede.  His reign saw renewal of war withPhillip II Augustus of Franc
2 CONC e to whom he lost several continentalpossesions including Normandy by 1205. He came into conflict with hisBarons and was forced to Sign the Magna Carta. His later repudiation ofthe charter led to the first barons war 1215-17 durin
2 CONC g which John died.Burke says he was born in 1160. John 'Lackland' King Of England was knownas one of England's worst kings; however, modern analysis notes he wasactually much better than his infamous reputation allows.  His barons
2 CONC forced him to grant the famous charter of liberties, Magna Carta, in1215. He was often cruel, but he showed both administrative and militaryability.  John succeeded his brother Richard the Lion-Hearted as king ofEngland and duke o
2 CONC f Normandy in 1199. His rule began badly. By ineptpolitics and the murder of his nephew Arthur, he lost the allegiance ofmany of his French barons. King Philip Augustus of France then declaredwar. In 1205 John was beaten, and lost
2 CONC  all the English holdings in Franceexcept Aquitaine. John persued a policy in England that brought him intoconflict with Pope Innocent III. In 1208 the pope placed England under aninterdict, which banned church services. The follo
2 CONC wing year John wasexcommunicated. The king then showed his capacity for strong rule. Heforced Scotland into a subordinate position, kept the Welsh princes incheck, and held a firm grip on Ireland. But his foreign favorites,profess
2 CONC ional troops, and autocratic financial policy stirred updiscontent among the English barons.  When John failed to reconquer thelost French territories in 1214, most of the barons and many of theclergy revolted. On June 15, 1215, t
2 CONC he king was forced to approve theMagna Carta at Runnymede meadow beside the River Thames.
