1 NOTE King John Signing the Magna Carta
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=d314bae0-ee2f-432d-90c0-89dae1cdd7dc&tid=12140672&pid=-60811326
1 NOTE 07-02-2008 04;31;20PM
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=5a51baba-4f8e-4a1b-b3c7-388990ccb0c4&tid=2456826&pid=78718100
1 NOTE John (of England), called John Lackland (1167-1216), king of England (1199-1216), best known for signing the Magna Carta.
2 CONT John was born in Oxford on December 24, 1167, the youngest son of King Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine. Henry provided for the eventual inheritance of his lands by his older sons before John was born. By 1186, however, only Rich
2 CONC ard I, the Lion-Hearted, and John were left as Henry's heirs. In 1189, as Henry neared death, John joined Richard's rebellion against their father, and when Richard was crowned, he gave John many estates and titles. John tried but
2 CONC  failed to usurp the Crown while Richard was away on the Third Crusade: Upon returning to England, Richard forgave him. When his brother died in 1199, John became king. A revolt ensued by the supporters of Arthur of Brittany, the 
2 CONC son of John's brother, Geoffrey. Arthur was defeated and captured in 1202, and John is believed to have had him murdered. King Philip II of France continued Arthur's war until John had to surrender nearly all his French possession
2 CONC s in 1204. In 1207 John refused to accept the election of Stephen Langton as archbishop of Canterbury. Pope Innocent III then excommunicated him and began negotiating with Philip for an invasion of England. Desperate, John surrend
2 CONC ered England to the pope and in 1213 received it back as a fief. Trying to regain his French possession, he was decisively defeated by Philip in 1214. John's reign had become increasingly tyrannical; to support his wars he had ext
2 CONC orted money, raised taxes, and confiscated properties. His barons finally united to force him to respect their rights and privileges. John had little choice but to sign the Magna Carta presented to him by his barons at Runnymede i
2 CONC n 1215, making him subject, rather than superior, to the law. Shortly afterward John and the barons were at war. He died at Newark in Nottinghamshire on October 19, 1216, while still pursuing the campaign, and was succeeded by his
2 CONC  son, Henry III."John (of England)," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1994 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1994 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation.
2 CONT
2 CONT King John and Magna Carta 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199 Richard I was succeeded by his brother John, the most despicable of English kings. By a series of blunders John lost almost all his French possessions except the southwest corner. The English barons refused to help him reg
2 CONC ain his territory. Angered by his tyrannical rule, they drew up a list of things that even a king might not do. On June 15, 1215, they forced him to set his seal to this Great Charter (in Latin, Magna Carta) of English liberties. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magna Carta is regarded as one of the most notable documents in history. The rights it listed were, in the main, feudal rights of justice and property that had been recognized by previous kings; but now for the first time these ri
2 CONC ghts were insisted upon against the king's will. Thus an important principle was established that the king himself must govern according to law. In later years, whenever a king over-extended his powers, the people could remind him
2 CONC  of Magna Carta. (See also John of England; Magna Carta.) 
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Excerpted from Compton’s Interactive Encyclopedia
2 CONT Copyright © 1993, 1994 Compton’s NewMedia, Inc.
1 NOTE "Lackland" refered to John's status as the youngest son, resulting in nosignificant inherited fiefs from his Father. His titles included King ofIreland 1177, Count of Mortain 1189, Earl of Gloucester. John succeededhis brother Ric
2 CONC hard I as King in 1199. In 1215 he put his seal on theMagna Carta (Great Charter). The Magna Carta is the foundation of EnglishConstitutional law and liberties and placed the King, like the subjectshe ruled, subject to the rule of
2 CONC  law. He is Interred in WorcesterCathedral. "The Encyclopedia of the Middle Ages" Norman F. Cantor,General Editor.
