1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://historytheinterestingbits.com/2015/12/13/the-jilted-princess/?fbclid=IwAR1KmlBj1gKylbQJNJ9ySqKzOApfsWEKJjNfGsqpH7yip1R0L-241B2mibE
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England, Queen Consort of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joan of England, Queen Consort of Scotland" redirects here. For other Joans of England, see Joan of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England, Queen Consort of Scotland (July 22, 1210 – March 4, 1238) was the eldest legitimate daughter and third child of John of England and Isabella of Angouleme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was brought up in the court of Hugh X of Lusignan who was promised to her in marriage from an early age, as compensation for him being jilted by her mother Isabella of Angouleme, however on the death of John of England, Isabe
2 CONC lla decided she should marry him herself and Joan was sent back to England, where negotiations for her hand with Alexander II of Scotland were taking place.
2 CONT
2 CONT She and Alexander married on June 21, 1221, at York Minster[1]. Alexander was 23. Joan was 11. They had no children. Joan died in her brother's arms at Havering-atte-Bower in 1238, and was buried at Tarant Crawford Abbey in Dorset
2 CONC [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing now remains of this church; the last mention of it is before the Reformation.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, Lady of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon, (c. 1188 – February 2, 1237) was the wife of Llywelyn the Great, Prince of Wales and Gwynedd and effective ruler of most of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan (Joanna) was an illegitimate daughter of King John of England and a woman named Clemence Pinel. She should not be confused with her legitimate half-sister Joan, Queen Consort of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about her early life; she was possibly born before her father, King John of England, married his first wife in 1189. Her mother's name is known only from Joan's obituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she is myste
2 CONC riously called "Regina Clementina" (Queen Clemence). Joan seems to have spent her childhood in France, as King John had her brought to the Kingdom of England from Normandy in preparation for her wedding in December 1203 at 15 year
2 CONC s of age or so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great between December 1203 and October 1204. She and Llywelyn had at least two children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elen ferch Llywelyn (Helen or Ellen) (1207-1253), married (1) John the Scot, Earl of Chester and (2) Robert II de Quincy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dafydd ap Llywelyn (c. 1215-1246) married Isabella de Braose, died at Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, (Aber). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of Llywelyn's other recorded children may also have been Joan's:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladus Ddu (1206-1251), married (1) Reginald de Braose and (2) Ralph de Mortimer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Susanna, who was sent to England as a hostage in 1228. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, who married Sir John de Braose, the grandson of William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III, declaring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had not been married to others at the time of her birth, but without giving her a claim to the English thron
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Easter 1230, William de Braose, 10th Baron Abergavenny, who was Llywelyn's nominal prisoner at the time, was discovered together with Joan in Llywelyn's bedchamber. William de Braose was hanged in the marshland at the foot of G
2 CONC arth Celyn, the place known since as Gwern y Grog. Joan herself was placed out of public view, under virtual house arrest, at Garth Celyn, for twelve months after the incident. She was then (apparently) forgiven by Llywelyn, and r
2 CONC estored as wife and princess. She may have given birth to a daughter early in 1231.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but, in Welsh, "Lady of Wales". She died at the royal home, Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, on the north coast of Gwynedd in 1237. Llywelyn's great grief at her death is recorded; he founded
2 CONC  a Franciscan friary on the seashore at Llanfaes, opposite the royal home, in her honour. The friary was consecrated in 1240, shortly before Llywelyn died. It was closed down in 1537 by Henry VIII of England during the Dissolution
2 CONC  of the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan's stone coffin can be seen in Beaumaris parish church, Anglesey. Above the empty coffin is a slate panel inscribed: "This plain sarcophagus, (once dignified as having contained the remains of JOAN, daughter of King JOHN, and 
2 CONC consort of LLEWELYN ap IOWERTH, Prince of North Wales, who died in the year 1237), having been conveyed from the Friary of Llanfaes, and alas, used for many years as a horsewatering trough, was rescued from such an indignity and p
2 CONC laced here for preseravation as well as to excite serious meditation on the transitory nature of all sublunary distinctions. By THOMAS JAMES WARREN BULKELEY, Viscount BULKELEY, Oct 1808"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT PRINCESS JOAN OF WALES of London, daughter of  John I "Lackland", King of England and Clemence, was born circa 1188, died on 1 Jan. 1236 in Aberconwy and was buried in Llan-Faes, Dindaethwy, Isle of Anglesey, Wales. She married in
2 CONC  England, INT after 16 (Aft 16 1205 Apr), (TO-5) LLEWELYN AP IORWERTH, PRINCE OF NORTH WALES, son of (TO-4) Prince Iorwerth "Drwyndwn" Ap and Margred Verch (MADOG), who was born in 1173 in Aberffraw Castle, Caernarvon, and died on
2 CONC  11 April 1240 in Aberconwy. [35, 85, 198, 1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Natural dau. of King John, possibly by Constance, Duchess of Brittany. [2, 86]
2 CONT
2 CONT Roberts says Joan was "illegitimate by Clemence, possibly Clemence Dauntsey, wife of Nicholas de Verdun. " and "For the identification of Clemence, mother of Joan, Princess of Wales, see a forthcoming article in TG by Douglas Rich
2 CONC ardson of Tucson, Arizona". [197]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: See (TO-5) Llewelyn ap IORWERTH, Prince of North Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT 25. KING HENRY III8 OF ENGLAND (John I "Lackland"7, Henry II6, Geoffrey V PLANTAGENET5, Fulk V the Young of ANJOU4, Fulk IV "Rechin"3, Aubri-Geoffrey II de GASTONOIS2, Geoffrey I1), son of (20) John I "Lackland"7, King of England 
2 CONC and (BH-9) Isabella (of ANGOULÊME) (LUSIGNAN), was born on 1 Oct. 1207 in Winchester[209], died on 16 Nov. 1272[209] and was buried on 20 Nov. 1272 in Westminster Abbey, Westminster, Middlesex. He married INT Jan. 1236 (37 ()), (C
2 CONC W-9) ELEANOR OF PROVENCE, QUEEN OF ENGLAND[214], daughter of (CW-7) Count Raymond Berenger V and (AGF-5) Countess Beatrix (of SAVOY), who was born in 1217[209], and died on 25 June 1291 in Amesbury[215]. [214, 6, 71, 137, 148, 196
2 CONC , 58, 109, 90, 1]
2 CONT
2 CONT King of England 1216-1272. [83]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT +	35	i.	EDWARD I "LONGSHANKS"9, KING OF ENGLAND, b. on 17 June 1239 in Westminster, London, England, d. on 7 July 1307 in Burgh-On-The-Sands, Cumbria, England; m. (1) (UC-31) ELEANOR OF CASTILE on 18 Oct. 1254 in Burgos; m. (2) (T
2 CONC -62) PRINCESS MARGUERITE OF FRANCE on 8 Sept. 1299 in Canterbury Cathedral, Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT +	36	ii.	PRINCE EDMUND "CROUCHBACK", b. on 16 Jan. 1244/5 in London, Middlesex, d. on 5 June 1296 in Bayonne, Pyr.-Atlantiques, France; m. (T-57) BLANCHE OF ARTOIS, PRINCESS OF FRANCE between 1 Jan. 1276/7 and in Paris, Seine, Fra
2 CONC nce.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England, Queen Consort of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England, Queen Consort of Scotland (22 July 1210 – 4 March 1238) was the eldest legitimate daughter and third child of John of England and Isabella of Angouleme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was brought up in the court of Hugh X of Lusignan who was promised to her in marriage from an early age, as compensation for him being jilted by her mother Isabella of Angouleme, however on the death of John of England, Isabe
2 CONC lla decided she should marry him herself and Joan was sent back to England, where negotiations for her hand with Alexander II of Scotland were taking place.
2 CONT
2 CONT She and Alexander married on 21 June 1221, at York Minster. Alexander was twenty-three. Joan was ten, almost eleven. They had no children. Joan died in her brother's arms at Havering-atte-Bower in 1238, and was buried at Tarant Cr
2 CONC awford Abbey in Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing now remains of this church; the last mention of it is before the Reformation.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_England,_Queen_Consort_of_Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Joan de Beaufort was born circa 1375 at Château de Beaufort, Meuse-et-Loire, Anjou, France.3 She was the daughter of John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster and Katherine Roët. She married, firstly, Sir Robert de Ferrers, 3rd Lord F
2 CONC errers (of Wem), son of Robert Ferrers, 2nd Lord Ferrers (of Wem) and Elizabeth le Botiler, Baroness le Botiller, in 1391 at Château de Beaufort, Meuse-et-Loire, Anjou, France.3 She married, secondly, Ralph de Neville, 1st Earl of
2 CONC  Westmorland, son of John de Neville, 3rd Lord Neville and Maud de Percy, before 29 November 1396 at Château de Beaufort, Maine-et-Loire, Anjou, France.1,3 She died on 13 November 1440 at Howden, Yorkshire, England.4 She was burie
2 CONC d at Lincoln Cathedral, Lincoln, Lincolnshire, England.4
2 CONT
2 CONT      From before 29 November 1396, her married name became Lady Neville.1 In February 1397 She was born before parent's marriage but legitimated by Parliament. As a result of her marriage, Lady Joan de Beaufort was styled as Count
2 CONC ess of Westmorland on 29 September 1397. She was invested as a Lady Companion, Order of the Garter (L.G.) in 1399.4 Her last will was dated 10 May 1440.5
2 CONT
