1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Isabella of England, Holy Roman Empress; Queen consort of Sicily==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Isabella of England''', also called '''Elizabeth''' (1214 – 1 December 1241) was an English princess and, by marriage, Holy Roman Empress, German Queen, and Queen consort of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the fourth child but second daughter of King John of England and Isabella of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a friendly meeting at Rieti, Pope Gregory IX suggested to Emperor Frederick II that he marry princess Isabella, a sister of Henry III of England. At first Frederick II was concerned to lose his French allies; but when he realis
2 CONC ed that an English marriage might end the ongoing Anglo-French feud and constitute an important step towards the restoration of peace in Western Christendom, thus smoothing the way for a successful crusade, he agreed. The betrotha
2 CONC l was formalized in London in February 1235.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Henry had to levy an unpopular tax of two marks of silver per hide in order to afford the thirty thousand marks Frederick insisted on as Isabella's dowry. Frederick sought this large amount to help fund his wars in nor
2 CONC thern Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The beautiful Isabella was about twenty-one years old when she set out to marry the twice-widowed Emperor Frederick II, who was forty. On her way through Cologne, she delighted the local women when she removed the traditionally wo
2 CONC rn veil so that they could see her face.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage between Isabella and Frederick took place in Worms Cathedral on 15 or 20 July 1235; in the ceremony, she was also crowned Holy Roman Empress, Queen of Germany and Sicily. She was granted the castle of Monte Sant'Angel
2 CONC o by her husband upon her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, as soon as she was married she was added to the Emperor's harem, which included women from Arabia attended by black eunuchs. Their marriage had been a political match, and she was allowed to keep only two of her English w
2 CONC omen-attendants, Margaret Biset, who probably had been her nurse, and her maid Kathrein; the others were sent home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella lived in retirement at Noventa Padovana where her husband regularly visited her. When her brother, Richard, Earl of Cornwall, returned from the crusades, he was allowed to visit her, although Isabella was not allowed to b
2 CONC e present at the official reception. While the imperial court resided at Foggia, Isabella gave birth to her last child and died. She is buried beside Frederick's previous wife, Queen Isabella II of Jerusalem, in Andria Cathedral, 
2 CONC near Bari.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary sources are at variance concerning Isabella's issue, including the number of children she had, their names, and their birth order. What is known for sure is that Isabella had at least four children: a son who died shortly 
2 CONC after his birth in 1236 or 1241, a daughter who - like her older brother - died shortly after her birth in 1237, Margaret, and Heinrich. Margaret is believed by some to have been the first child, and by others to be the child whos
2 CONC e birth caused Isabella's death. The most common belief is that Margaret was the last child. The short-lived son of Isabella has been given the name of Frederick, Jordanus/Jordan, and Carl Otto by various sources. Some historians 
2 CONC believe Isabella actually had five children, two short-lived sons instead of one, and that they were named Jordanus/Carl Otto and Frederick, the two being born in spring 1236 and summer 1240.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Frederick/Jordan/Charles Otto (Spring 1236–1236)
2 CONT * Agnes (born & died 1237) 
2 CONT * Henry (18 January 1238 – May 1254) 
2 CONT * Margaret, landgravine of Thuringia (1 December 1241 – 8 August 1270) 
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
2 CONT
