2 CONT Son of King John (Lackland).  Earl of Cornwall commencing 1225, Count of Poitou 1225 - 1243, elected "King of the Romans" (Germany) in 1257 and reigned as such through his death in 1272.  One of the wealthiest men in Europe during
2 CONC  his lifetime.  Earl Richard was thrice married, fathered 7 children by these marriages, of whom only two survived to adulthood.  Earl Richard was more fortunate with mistress Joan de Valletort (daughter of Sir Walter de Bath), fa
2 CONC thering three sons and two daughters, all of whom survived to adulthood.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT irth: 	Jan. 5, 1209
2 CONT Winchester Hampshire, England Death: 	Apr. 2, 1272 Berkhamsted Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Knight, Earl of Cornwall, Count of Poitou, Lieutenant of Guienne, Privy Councillor, Joint Guardian of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second son of John Lackland, King of England, and Isabel de Taillefer of Angouleme, born at Winchester Castle. Grandson of Henry II the King of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine, the House of Plantagenet. Only Geoffrey de Plantagen
2 CONC et actually used that term as a nsme as he wore a sprig of broom in his hat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Isabel Marshall, daughter of Sir William Marshall and Isabel FitzGilbert, widow of Gilbert de Clare, the Earl of Gloucester. The marriage of his son to a wealthy, powerful family who repeatedly opposed him greatly displ
2 CONC easured King Richard. Richard became step-father to her six children by Gilbert, and they had four children:
2 CONT * John of Cornwall, died as an infant
2 CONT * Isabel of Cornwall buried with her brother, John
2 CONT * Henry of Cornwall, m Contance de Bearn
2 CONT * Nicholas of Cornwall, died as an infant
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, Richard married Sanchia of Provence, daughter of Raymond de Berenger, on 23 Nov 1243 at Westminster Abbey. They had two sons:
2 CONT * Unknown son born and died the same day
2 CONT * Sir Edmund of Cornwall
2 CONT
2 CONT Thirdly, Richard married Beatrice de Falkenberg, the daughter of Deitrich II de Falkenbrg. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard also had several children by an unknown mistress:
2 CONT * Philip of Cornwall, priest
2 CONT * Sir Richard of Cornwall m Joan FitzAlan
2 CONT * Sir Walter of Cornwall
2 CONT * Joan of Cornwall m Richard de Champernoun & Sir Peter de Fissacre
2 CONT
2 CONT 1227 - Made High Sheriff of Berkshire, aged 8 1225 Feb 02 - knighted by his brother, King Henry III 1225 - granted the honour of Launceston, Cornwall, made him one of the wealthiest men in Europe 1229 - granted to honour of Berkha
2 CONC mpstead 1231 - married Isabel Marshall, his main residence became Wallingford Castle in Berkshire 1232-3 - fought in Wales against Llywellyn ap Iowerth 1237 - openly spoke against his brother's greed and mis-administration, Henry 
2 CONC bought his silence with more gifts 1237 - ambassador to Emperor Frederick 1233 - drove Llywelyn back, fortified Radnor Castle 1238 - Richard's sister, Eleamnor, marriedd Simon de Montfort 1240 - Isabel died, buried at Beaulieu Abb
2 CONC ey 1240 - left to crusade for the Holy Lands 1241 - negotiated treaty with the sultan of Krak, releasing many French from captivity 1243 - married Sanchia of Provence, the sister of his brother's wife 1243 - demounced all his righ
2 CONC ts in Ireland and Gascony, abandoning the title of Count of Ponthieu 1244 - granted the honour of Bradninch, Devon 1250 - Joint Ambassador to France and to Pope Innocent IV 1257 - elected King of the Romans, styled King of Almain,
2 CONC  Jan 13rd, crowned at Aachen Jan 1259 1259 - failed to establish authority in Germany and returned to England 1261 - wife Sanchia died 1263 - achieved a temporary truce between the barons and his brother 1264 - taken prisoner at t
2 CONC he Battle of Lewes May 14 1265 - released and lands restored after the Battle of Evesham Aug 04 1629 - married Beatrice de Falkenburg 1270 - purchased the honour of Tremarton, Cornwall 1272 - April 2 or 3 - Richard died testate, b
2 CONC uried with Sanchia 1277 - Oct 17 - Beatrice died, buried at Friars Minors, Oxford
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard would raise his voice three times in protest against his brother, Henry the king's policies or his choices, and each time, Richard would receive generous gifts in order to placate and silence him. When his first wife, Isab
2 CONC el was on her deathbed, she asked to be buried next to her first husband at Tewkesbury, Gilbert de Clare. Richard had her interred at Beaulieu Abbey but sent her heart to Tewkesbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard fought in no battles but managed to negotiate for the release of prisoners and the burials of Crusaders killed at a battle in Gaza in 1239, and fortified Ascalon, which had been demolished by Saladin. On his return from th
2 CONC e Holy Land, Richard visited his sister Isabella, the empress of Frederick II and met Sanchia (Cynthia) who he made his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 May 1257 the archbishop of Cologne himself crowned Richard "King of the Romans" in Aachen, but only after large sums of money were paid to those who opposed him. and his title never meant much.
2 CONT
2 CONT He founded Hailes Abbey by 1246, Burnham Abbey in Buckinghamshire in 1263, and the Grashaus, Aachen in 1266, fought with Henry against Simon de Montfort in the Second Barons' War (1264–67). After the defeat at the Battle of Lewes,
2 CONC  Richard hid in a windmill, but was found and imprisoned until September of 1265.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard's third wife, Beatrice, was sixteen and one of the most beautiful woman of the era. They had no children, and she died at 24 in 1277 buried before the high altar at the Church of the Grey Friars in Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1271, he had a stroke when his right side became paralyzed and he lost the ability to speak. On 2 April 1272, Richard died at Berkhamsted Castle in Hertfordshire, buried next to Sanchia of Provence and Henry of Almain,
2 CONC  his son by his first wife, at Hailes Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was succeeded by Edmund, son of his second wife Sanchia, and Rudolph I as the King of the Romans. 
2 CONT
