2 CONT Made High Sheriff of Berkshire, aged 8, in 1227.  Knighted by his brother King Henry III on Feb. 2, 1225.  In 1225, granted the honour of Launceston, Cornwall; this made him one of Europe's richest men. In 1231, married Isabel Mar
2 CONC shall and made his main residence Wallingford Castle in Berkshire; she died in 1240. Also in 1240, he left to crusade for the Holy Lands. Elected the "King of the Romans" in 1257, styled King of Almain, Jan. 13; crowned at Aachen;
2 CONC  failed to establish authority in Germany and returned to England. Taken prisoner at Battle of Lewes, May 14, 1264; released in 1265; lands restore after Battle of Evesham Aug. 4 Purchased honour of Tremarton, Cornwall, in 1270. F
2 CONC ounded Hailes Abbey by 1246, Burnham Abbey in Buckinghamshire in 1263; and the Grashaus, Aachen in 1266. Fought with Henry in Second Barons' War (1264-67). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had his son Sir Walter of Cornwall with an unknown mistress; also by same mother, Philip of Cornwall (priest); Sir Richard of Cornwall (married Joan FitzAlan); Joan of Cornwall. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Shropshire Walcot Family - Chart VII: The Cornwall Family; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id101.html. (Steven Ferry, May 1, 2020.)www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Cornwall
2 CONT BIRTH 5 Jan 1209
2 CONT Winchester, City of Winchester, Hampshire, England
2 CONT DEATH 2 Apr 1272 (aged 63)
2 CONT Berkhamsted, Dacorum Borough, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Hailes Abbey
2 CONT Hailes, Tewkesbury Borough, Gloucestershire, England 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 5691700
