2 CONC ère", by charter dated 1231 (no source citation)[1050].  “Johannes de Estotevilla miles dominus de Boscheto et Agnes uxor mea” renounced rights over the priory of Lancé in favour of Marmoutier, with the consent of “Robertus primog
2 CONC enitus noster, Radulphus et Astulphus filii mei”, by charter dated Mar 1249 (O.S.?)[1051].  “Domina de Estoutevilla soror quondam Gaufridi vicecomitis Castriduni“ claimed “portionem suam iure hereditario contingentem de vicecomita
2 CONC tu Castriduni” from [the husband of her niece Clémence de Châteaudun] “domino Roberto de Drocis...cum filia sua“ by charter dated 1260[1052].  Père Anselme, saying that this 1260 document names “Isabeau de Châteaudun” as the “domi
2 CONC na de Estoutevilla...” (not the case in the extract consulted), indicates that Isabelle (see above) was Jean’s second wife[1053].  The suggestion is unsustainable considering that Agnes is named in the later sources cited below.  
2 CONC Morandière quotes (in translation) a charter dated Jun 1272 under which "Agnes [d’Estouteville] au temps de mon veuvage" donated property to Valmont, with the consent of "domini Roberti, domini Willelmi, et domini Estoldi, et Magi
