1 NOTE After she fled from France in 1345, Jeanne of Flanders, Countess de Montfort, in her last days of madness was kept at Tickhill Castle for 30 years, near York.  She died about 1376.  Her young son was then taken as ward of King Edw
2 CONC ard III.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John Comte de Monfort,  the husband of Jeanne, Countess of Montfort was imprisoned in 1341 over the dukedom of Brittany, the wife of John Comte de Montfort took up his cause, and fought "like a man with the courage of a man a
2 CONC nd the heart of a lion" - this remarkeable wife rode from town to town in Brittany, fallied the allegiance of dispirited partisans to her three-year-old son, John.  She promised she had "riches enough to maintain the cause.  She p
2 CONC rovisioned and fortified garrisons against Charles of Blois and the French, organized reisistance, and paid largely and gave freely; presided over councils, conducted diplomacy, and expressed herself in eloquent and graceful lette
2 CONC rs."
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband, Comte John IV de Montfort following his escape from the Louvre in Paris, the Countess Jeanne de Montfort, gallant defender of Hennebont, fled to England to the protection of King Edward III.  The bl
2 CONC ows, intrigues, privations and broken hopes of her life proved too much for the countess, who went mad, and was confined in the castle of Tickhill for the next 30 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 100 years war between England and France, the private war between Charles of Blois and the Countess Jeanne de Montfort came to a head during the siege of Hennenbont (on the Blavet, in northwest France) in 1342.  The lad
2 CONC y led a heroic defense in full armor astride a war-horse in the streets, exhorting the soldiers under a hail of arrows; ordering the women of the town to cut short their skirts and carry stones and pots of boiling pitch to the wal
2 CONC ls to cast down upon the enemy.  during a lull in the battle, she led a party of knnights out of a secret gate, and galloped by a roundabout way to take the enemy camp in the rear, destroying half their force. And the siege was de
2 CONC feated.
1 NOTE SHE BECAME FAMOUS FOR HER GALLANT DEFENSE OF HENNEBONT DUIRNG HER HUSBAND'S
2 CONT CAPTIVITY (1341, PARIS, TOWER OF THE LOUVRE, RELEASED 9/1343); PROBABLY BECAME
2 CONT INSANE ca1343 AND REMAINED SO UNTIL HER DEATH
