1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Isabelle de Beauvau or Isabeau de Beauvau (around 1436–1475) was a French noblewoman, of the Beauvau family, lady of Champigny and de la Roche-sur-Yon, countess of Vendôme by her marriage'''.
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT '''Isabelle was the only child''' of the marriage between Count Louis de Beauvau (1409-1492) and his first wife, Marguerite de Chambley,[1] a woman of noble birth from Lorraine. '''Isabelle's lineage made her valuable to René of A
2 CONC njou, who was dealing with a succession crisis over the duchy of Lorraine. He was trying to strengthen ties with Lorraine's nobility which is why her name appears with those of her mother and Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine, in a han
2 CONC dwritten collection of poems by Alain Chartier offered to Marie de Clèves.[2] Other than that not much is known about Isabelle's childhood; her marriage negotiations started before she was eighteen'''.
2 CONT Marriage and becoming Countess
2 CONT '''Isabelle married John VIII, Count of Vendôme, on 9 November 1454 at Angers,[3] thus becoming the Countess of Vendôme. Isabelle and John had''':
2 CONT *Jeanne (1460-1487); in 1478 she married Louis de Joyeuse (around 1450–1498);
2 CONT *Catherine (1461-after 1525), married in 1484 to Gilbert de Chabannes, seneschal of Limousin;
2 CONT *Jeanne de Bourbon (1465-1511), known as Jeanne la Jeune; she married 1) in 1487 Jean II of Bourbon, 2) in 1495 Jean I??, Count of Auvergne (through this marriage, she became the grandmother of Catherine de Médicis); 3) in 1503 Fr
2 CONC ançois de La Pause, baron of La Garde;
2 CONT *Renée (1468-1534), abbess of the Trinity of Caen from 1491 to 1505, then from Fontevrault from 1505 to her death;
2 CONT *François de Bourbon-Vendôme (1470-1495)[4]
2 CONT *Louis, Prince of La Roche-sur-Yon (1473-1520)[4]
2 CONT *Charlotte (1474-1520), married to Engilbert de Clèves, count of Nevers (1462-1506);
2 CONT *Isabelle (1475-1531), abbess of the Trinity of Caen from 1505 to 1531
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT '''Isabelle died giving birth to her last daughter. She is buried in the Saint-George collegiate church in Vendôme, the Bourbon Vendôme necropolis, which has since disappeared. Through her third daughter she was great-grandmother 
2 CONC to the French nobility. She is most remembered for the poem by Alain Chartier to Marie of Cleves'''.
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabelle_de_Beauvau
2 CONT ________
2 CONT '''Isabel de Beauveau'''1 
2 CONT F, #113705, '''b. circa 1436, d. circa 1474'''
2 CONT Last Edited=2 Jun 2013
2 CONT '''     Isabel de Beauveau was born circa 1436. She was the daughter''' of Louis de Beauveau, Seigneur de Champigny and Margaret de Chambley.1 '''She married Jean II de Bourbon, Comte de Vendôme''', son of Louis de Bourbon, Comte 
2 CONC de Vendôme and Jeanne de Laval, '''on 9 November 1454. She died circa 1474'''.
2 CONT '''Children of Isabel de Beauveau and Jean II de Bourbon, Comte de Vendôme'''
2 CONT 1. Louis de Bourbon, Prince de Roche-sur-Yon+1 d. 1520
2 CONT 2.Jeanne de Bourbon+ b. 1465, d. 22 Jan 1511
2 CONT 3. François de Bourbon, Duc de Vendôme+1 b. 1470, d. 1495
2 CONT 4. Charlotte de Bourbon-Vendôme+2 b. 1474, d. 14 Dec 1520
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 68. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT 2.[S3268] Hans Harmsen, "re: Chester Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 21 August 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Chester Family."
2 CONT https://thepeerage.com/p11371.htm#i113705
