0 @N6000000002187738982@ NOTE Robert de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Leicester
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Leicester (died circa 21 October 1204) was an English nobleman, the last of the Beaumont earls of Leicester. He is sometimes known as Robert FitzPernel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was the son of Robert de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Leicester and Petronilla, who was either a granddaughter or great-granddaughter of Hugh de Grandmesnil. As a young man, he accompanied King Richard I on the Third Crusade, and 
1 CONC it was while the crusading forces rested at Messina, Sicily that Robert was invested with the Earldom of Leicester in early 1191.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert's newly-gained estates included a large part of central Normandy. He held castles at Pacy, Pont-Saint-Pierre and Grandmesnil. Earl Robert also was lord of the vast honor of Breteuil, but the family castle there had been dis
1 CONC mantled after the 1173-1174 War. On his return from the crusade, he turned his attentions to the defense of Normandy from the French. After defending Rouen from the advances of Philip II of France, he attempted to retake his castl
1 CONC e of Pacy. He was captured by forces of the French king and remained imprisoned for 3 years. Later, King John would bestow the new fortress and lordship of Radepont (the land of Radepont was traded to King John by the seigneur du 
1 CONC Neubourg for lands and revenues in the pays de Caux) to the earl.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sometime after his release in 1196 he married Loretta, daughter of William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber. They had no children, and Robert's death in 1204 brought the end of the Beaumont male line.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year of his death Normandy was lost to the French, Earl Robert attempted to come to an independent arrangement with King Philip of France, in which he would hold his land in Normandy as a liege-vassal of the Kings of France
1 CONC , and his lands in England as a liege-vassal of the Kings of England. In any event, Robert died that year, but his great English estates were divided between the heirs of his two sisters. The eldest sister, Amicia, had married the
1 CONC  French baron Simon de Montfort, and their son, also named Simon de Montfort, inherited half the estate as well as the title of Earl of Leicester. The younger sister, Margaret, had married Saer de Quincy, and they inherited the ot
1 CONC her half. Three years later Saer was created Earl of Winchester.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont Lord High Steward
1 CONT 1190–1204 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Simon de Montfort 
1 CONT Earl of Leicester
1 CONT 1190–1204 
