0 @N6000000003300525555@ NOTE
1 CONT Alexander III and Margaret were first cousins.
1 CONT Alexander III, King of Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT 1241-86, king of Scotland (1249-86), son and successor of Alexander II. H e married a daughter of Henry III of England and quarreled with Henry, an d later Henry?s son Edward I, over the old English claims to overlordshi p in Scot
1 CONC land. The great achievement of Alexander was his final acquisiti on for Scotland of the Hebrides and of the Isle of Man, which his fathe r had already claimed from Norway. King Haakon IV of Norway attempted t o drive the Scots fro
1 CONC m the islands, but a storm battered his ships, and h e was defeated in the battle of Largs in the Clyde river. In 1266, Alexan der signed a treaty with Magnus VI, assigning the islands to Scotland. Al exander survived his children
1 CONC , and when he died his only near relative wa s his little granddaughter Margaret Maid of Norway.
1 CONT Alexander was crowned by David de Bernham, Bishop of St. Andrews.
1 CONT ALEXANDER III (r. 1249-86)
1 CONT Born in 1241, the only son of Alexander II and his second wife, Marie d e Coucy, Alexander became king at the age of seven, on the death of his f ather. On Christmas Day 1251, when he was ten, he was knighted at York b y Henry III
1 CONC , and the following day he was married to the English monarch' s eldest daughter, Princess Margaret.
1 CONT Alexander proved a strong-willed king who, despite the English influenc e in his youth, refused to swear homage for his kingdom. He continued hi s father's efforts to establish Scottish mastery of the Isles. In 1263 hi s army infl
1 CONC icted a notable defeat on Hakon, King of Norway, at the Battl e of Largs in Ayrshire. The Norwegians were subsequently forced to conced e to him the Western Isles and the Isle of Man, under the treaty of Pert h in 1266.
1 CONT Alexander established good relations with his brother-in-law Edward I. O n 19 August 1274, Alexander and Margaret attended the coronation of her b rother in Westminster Abbey. Margaret died six months later, leaving thre e childre
1 CONC n.
1 CONT Within a few years, Alexander suffered a further series of family tragedi es. His younger son David died in 1281 at the age of eight; his daughte r Margaret, who had married King Eric of Norway, died in childbirth in 12 83; and hi
1 CONC s elder son Alexander died childless in 1284 after a long illne ss. A week after the prince's death, the Scottish Parliament recognised M argaret, the little daughter of Eric and Margaret, as the heir presumptiv e to the Scottish 
1 CONC throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander was only 44, and so he decided that the best way to avoid a con stitutional crisis was to remarry and have more sons. On 14 October 128 5 he therefore took as his wife Yolande, Comtesse de Montfort, daughter o f Robert I
1 CONC V, Comte de Dreux.
1 CONT Five months later, on 19 March 1286, Alexander's horse stumbled and he fe ll from a cliff near Kinghorn, Fife. The king was killed, leaving the Sco ts to mourn an energetic, effective monarch who had brought them peace an d prospe
1 CONC rity.
1 CONT MARGARET (r. 1286-90)
1 CONT When her grandfather died, Princess Margaret, 'the Maid of Norway', was o nly three years old. The Scottish Parliament appointed six Guardians to r ule on her behalf, and on 18 July 1290 the Scots agreed in the Treaty o f Birgham 
1 CONC (Berwickshire) that she should marry Edward I of England's elde st son, Prince Edward.
1 CONT At the end of September, the eight-year-old Queen set sail for Scotland , escorted by Bishop Narve of Bergen. She was taken ill on the voyage an d her ship put in at Orkney, but she died there, in the arms of the Bisho p. Her body
1 CONC  was taken back to Bergen, where she was buried beside her mot her.
