0 @N6000000003658774141@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT MAHAUT DE CHÂTILLON, married by marriage settlement dated 1350 GUY DE LUXEMBOURG, Count of Ligny (in Namur), seigneur of Roussy, Beaurevoir (in Arrouaise), and Richebourg, Castelain of Lille, Governor of arras and Picardy, and, in
1 CONC  right of his wife, Count of Saint-Pol, son and heir of Jean I de Luxembourg, sire of Ligny, Beaurevoir, and Roussy, Castelain of Lille, by his wife, Alix .... They had five sons ... and three daughters .... He also had one illegi
1 CONC timate son, Jean.  Mahaut was heiress in 1360 to her brother, Guy de Châtillon, Count of Saint-Pol.  In 1363 Pope Urban V wrote to King Edward III of England, requesting that the king give license to Count Guy, then held as hostag
1 CONC e in England, to go to France on his own affairs and that of his late father and brother.  The same year Count Guy and his wife, Countess Marguerite [sic], received an indult for a portable altar.  GUY DE LUXEMBOURG was slain at t
1 CONC he Battle of Basweiler 28 August 1371, commanding the van of the army of Wenzel of Luxembourg, Duke of Luxembourg.  His widow, Mahaut, was living 27 August 1372, but died before 1378.
