0 @N6000000000337163797@ NOTE (Research):Louis VI Encyclopædia Britannica Article    born 1081 died Aug. 1, 1137   byname Louis The Fat,  French Louis Le Gros king of France from 1108 to 1137; he brought power and dignity to the French crown by his recovery of
1 CONC  royal authority over the feudal nobles in his domains of the Ile-de-France and the Orléanais. Louis was designated by his father, Philip I, as his successor in 1098 and was already effectively the ruler well before Philip's death
1 CONC  in 1108. He quickly recognized that his priority must be to bring the unruly barons of the royal lands under firm control, and he spent much of his reign in conflict with such men as Hugh de Puiset. His success won him the respec
1 CONC t of his greater vassals and was crucial to later Capetian expansion. From his pacification program Louis developed several important concepts for future kings: for example, that the king was a vassal of no man.   Louis had a good
1 CONC  relationship with the church and clergy. He has been presented by some historians as the father of communes or towns, but in fact he recognized towns only out of circumstance rather than from principle.   Louis's major wars were 
1 CONC against King Henry I of England during the periods 1104-13 and 1116-20. When Charles the Good, count of Flanders, was assassinated in 1127, Louis supported William Clito, who became the successor; even though William was eventuall
1 CONC y toppled, Louis's actions demonstrated the new strength of the monarchy. In 1124 he was able to muster forces from many parts of France to counter a threatened invasion by the Holy Roman emperor Henry V. Louis's last major achiev
1 CONC ement was to arrange a marriage between his son Louis VII and Eleanor, heiress of William X, duke of Aquitaine. Abbot Suger of Saint-Denis, a most trusted adviser, is the primary historian for Louis's reign.
0 @N6000000000337408690@ NOTE Weis, p. 97:  King of France 1108-1137;  Crusader.  Father of Louis VII (the Young) - first husband of Eleanor of Aquitaine.
