1 CONT New Creation Earl of Worcester
1 CONT 1138–1166 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Extinct 
0 @N6000000002187742114@ NOTE Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester (1104 – 5 April 1168), also known as Robert "Le Bossu" (meaning "Robert the Uneven" in French), was an English nobleman of French ancestry. He was the son of Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl o
1 CONC f Leicester and Elizabeth de Vermandois, the twin brother of Waleran de Beaumont.
1 CONT
1 CONT The two brothers, Robert and Waleran, were adopted into the royal household shortly after their father's death (upon which instance Robert inherited all of his father's hereditary titles, chiefly Earl of Leicester). They accompani
1 CONC ed King Henry I among various missions, firstly to Normandy, then to meet with Pope Callixtus II in 1119. In 1135, the two twins were present at King Henry's deathbed; the monarch's death led to The Anarchy (under the weak rule of
1 CONC  King Stephen), and Robert eventually captured his rival, Roger de Tosny. In June of 1139, the inseparable brothers led the actions against Roger of Salisbury (the Bishop of Salisbury) and Alexander (the Bishop of Lincoln); the fo
1 CONC rmer was killed in December of that year, while the latter survived for eight more years.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Stephen had taken the two brothers as his personal advisors; the two brothers remained in his confidence for several decades. However, after Stephen's compromise with his cousin, Matilda (wherein Henry, Matilda's son, would s
1 CONC ucceed Stephen as king), the twins provided Henry, soon to be crowned Henry II of England, with "means for his struggle." Thereafter, the brothers were in the new monarch's confidence, as evidenced by Robert's appointment as chief
1 CONC  justiciar and as a hereditary steward; eventually, he bought out Hugh Bigod, 1st Earl of Norfolk (another noble who enjoyed the confidence of the crown) and is considered the first Lord High Steward of England. Robert enjoyed a h
1 CONC igh status in Henry's court, even acting as head of the kingdom (in a vice-regal capacity) for a time. His name appeared at the top of the Constitutions of Clarendon, and he was present at the Council of Northampton.
1 CONT
1 CONT He founded, in addition to St. Mary de Pré, the abbey of Garendon, the monastery of Nuneaton, the priory of Lusfield, and the hospital of Brackley, and was a liberal benefactor to many other houses.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married Amice de Montfort, daughter of Raoul, senior of Gael et of Montfort. They had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Hawise, who married William Fitz Robert, 2nd Earl of Gloucester; 
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Leicester; 
1 CONT Isabel, who married with: 
1 CONT Simon II of St Liz, 4th Earl of Huntingdon and Northampton; 
1 CONT Gervase Paynel of Dudley. 
1 CONT Margaret, who married Ralph V de Tosny 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT thePeerage.com 
1 CONT "Beaumont, Robert de, Earl of Leicester", The Dictionary of National Biography, Rpt. in thePeerage.com 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT — Lord High Steward
1 CONT 1154–1168 Succeeded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Leicester 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont Earl of Leicester Succeeded by:
1 CONT Robert Blanchemains 
