0 @N6000000002187742024@ NOTE Hugh de Grandmesnil
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh de Grandmesnil (1032 – February 22, 1098), also known as Hugh de Grentmesnil or Grentemesnil, was a commander at the Battle of Hastings. Following the conquest William I of England gave De Grandmesnil a 100 manors for his ser
1 CONC vices, sixty-five of them in Leicestershire. He was appointed sheriff of the county of Leicester and Governor of Hampshire.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married the beautiful Adeliza, daughter of Ivo, Count of Beaumont-sur-l'Oise, with whom he inherited Brokesbourne in Herefordshire, and three lordships in Warwickshire. Together they had five sons and as many daughters—namely, 
1 CONC Robert, William, Hugh, Ivo, and Aubrey; Adeline, Hawise, Rohais, Matilda, and Agnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baron Grandmesnil was at the heart of Anglo-French politics. In 1067 Hugh joined with William Fitz Osbern and Bishop Odo in the government of England, during the King's absence in Normandy. He also was one of the Norman nobles who
1 CONC  interceded with the Conqueror in favour of Williams' son Robert Court-heuse, and effected a temporary reconciliation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelize wife of Hugh de Grandmesnil died at Rouen in 1087, and was buried in the Chapter House of St. Evroult.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1094, Hugh de Grentmesnil was again in England, and, worn out with age and infirmity, finding his end approaching, assumed, in accordance with the common practice of the period, the habit of a monk, and expired six days after h
1 CONC e had taken to his bed, February 22, in the city of Leicester. His body, preserved in salt and sewn up in the hide of an ox, was conveyed to Normandy by two monks of St. Evroult, and buried by the Abbot Roger on the south side of 
1 CONC the Chapter House, near the tomb of Abbot Mainer. His eldest son, Robert, inherited his Normandy lands in the Ouch valley, while his son Ivo inherited the Honour of Leicester.
