0 @N6000000003051193597@ NOTE 1215: Surety for the Magna Charta
1 CONT 1219: Crusader
1 CONT Justiciar of England
1 CONT
1 CONT He was one of the barons present at Lincoln, when William "the Lion" of Scotland did homage to the English king in Oct., 1200. He subsequently obtained large grants and immunities from King John, and was created Earl of Winchester
1 CONC , on Mar. 20, 1207, having been, in 1203, governor of the castle of Ruil, in Normandy.
1 CONT He was one of the Barons to contend for the Charter of Liberty, and is credited with having re-written it from the Charter of Henry I, and the Saxon Code, and, opposing the king's concession to the Papal legate, had the bitter hat
1 CONC red of King John, and was very active in the conferences between the barons and the king. But, though the king made him, in 1215, governor of Mountsorell Castle, he was one of the barons to whom the city and Tower of London were r
1 CONC esigned, and elected one of the 25 barons who were to enforce the Magna Charta and govern the kingdom, being excommunicated with the other barons in the following year.
1 CONT He was sent, with Robert fitz Walter, the Surety, by the other barons to invite the Dauphin of France to assume the crown of England, and, even after the death of King John, he kept a strong garrison in Mountsorell Castle, on beha
1 CONC lf of Prince Louis. The fortress being beseiged and nearly captured by a division of the troops of Henry III, he and Prince Louis gathered a large force in London, and having raised the seige, marched to Lincoln, then also surroun
1 CONC ded by the king's army. In the general battle that followed, the barons were defeated, being greatly outnumbered, and Saire, with many others, was made prisoner, and his estates forfeited. In the following Oct., his immense estate
1 CONC s were restored upon his submission.
1 CONT In 1218, he went with the Earls of Chester and Arundel to the Holy Land, and assisted at the seige of Damietta, in 1219, and died in the progress toward Jerusalem. He was buried at Acre, in the Holy Land.
