0 @N6000000002188684042@ NOTE Simon VI de Montfort
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Simon de Montfort "the younger" or Simon VI de Monfort (April 1240-1271) was the second son of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Eleanor of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father and his elder brother Henry were killed at the Battle of Evesham in August 1265: the younger Simon had been slow to bring his forces from London, and had seen them and their banners captured by Prince Edward, who then u
1 CONC sed the banners to trick the older Simon. The younger Simon tried to raise a rebellion in Lincolnshire, but this had petered out by Christmas.
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his younger brother Guy escaped to France and Italy: in 1271 they discovered and murdered their cousin Henry of Almain at the church in Viterbo, for which they were excommunicated. Simon died later that year at Siena, "curs
1 CONC ed by God, a wanderer and a fugitive".
0 @N6000000003658776351@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT SIMON DE MONTFORT, Knt., 2nd son, Constable of Portchester, born near Brindisi in summer 1240.  At the opening of the Barons' War in 1264, he defended Northampton against the king.  He was captured after a gallant fight 5 April 12
1 CONC 64, and imprisoned at Windsor.  Following his father's victory at the Battle of Lewes 14 May 1464 [[sic]], he was released from prison.  At Christmas 1265 he was forced to submit himself to the judgement [[sic]] of the king and co
1 CONC uncil at Northampton.  They pardoned him on condition that he would surrended [[sic]] Kenilworth Castle and quit England for life, with a yearly pension of 400/.  In Feb. 1266 he escaped to Winchester and went over sea to France. 
1 CONC  He was present in France 26 March 1268.  In 1271 he and his brother, Guy, murdered their cousin, Henry of Almain ... in a church at Viterbo, Italy.  SIR SIMON DE MONTFORT died at a castle near Siena in 1271.
