0 @N6000000002188684052@ NOTE Amaury de Montfort, Canon of York
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Amaury de Montfort (1242/1243-1301) was the fourth son to parliamentary pioneer Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester, and Eleanor of England, daughter of King John.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amaury entered the priesthood as a young man, and held the positions of Treasurer of York Cathedral, canon of Rouen, Evreux, London and Lincoln. He served as a papal chaplain as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the deaths of his father and older brother Henry de Montfort at the Battle of Evesham in 1265 (by men under the command of then Prince Edward, later Edward I, aka Edward Longshanks), Amaury fled to France with his mother, yo
1 CONC unger sister, and surviving brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amaury de Montfort soon began studying medicine and theology at the University of Padua. His older brothers, Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola and Simon de Montfort (the younger) were seeking their fortunes in Italy. A tragic turn of
1 CONC  events lead to the bloody 1271 confrontation between Guy and Simon and their cousin Henry of Almain (also Edward's cousin, by virtue of his father, Henry III being elder brother to both Eleanor of England and Henry of Almain's fa
1 CONC ther, Richard, Earl of Cornwall).
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, whom the de Montfort sons considered a traitor to their father's ideals, was attacked during mass at Viterbo, and murdered on the altar steps, resulting in the excommunication of both de Montfort sons. While Amaury was not 
1 CONC in Viterbo, and was not involved in the murder, Edward swore vengeance upon all of Simon de Montfort's sons, Amaury included. Simon the younger died that year, reportedly of a tertian fever, while Guy managed to appeal to the pope
1 CONC  (with the aid of his father-in-law), resulting in his return to the church.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1275, after the death of his mother at Montargis Abbey, Amaury, by then a Papal Chaplain, accompanied his younger sister Eleanor de Montfort on a winter sea voyage to Wales and her new husband, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd (the grandso
1 CONC n of Llywelyn Fawr). Intercepted at sea by mercenaries in the employ of now King Edward I, both Amaury and Eleanor were taken captive.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Eleanor's captivity was gentle and relatively short-lived (she was returned to her husband after three years), Amaury was held in the infamous prison of Corfe Castle for over three years (and then spent another four years in
1 CONC  other, less stark prisons), despite the strenuous objections of the French Monarchy, the Welsh Principality, and the Pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his release in 1282, Amaury returned to France in exile, never again to see his sister, who died in childbirth that very year, mere months before the death of her husband Llewylyn and the subjugation of Wales to the English c
1 CONC rown. Amaury advanced in the church, and died in Italy after 1301, a prominent clergyman.
0 @N6000000003658776251@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT AMAURY DE MONTFORT, clerk, Treasurer of York, Canon of Rouen, Évreux, London, and Lincoln, papal chaplain, 3rd son, born about 1242 (aged 34 in 1276).  In 1264 his appointment as Treasurer of York was revoked, and in 1266 Pope Cle
1 CONC ment IV ordered the dean and chapter not to recognize him.  In 1268 he went to Italy, and studied for three years at the university of Padua.  In 1275/6, while escorting his sister, Eleanor, to Wales, he was captured at Bristol.  
1 CONC He was liberated 21 April 1282, on condition of abjuring the realm.  In 1284 he began a suit in the court of Rome against Edmund of Lancaster, the king's brother, for restitution of his inheritance.  He was in Paris in 1286, actin
1 CONC g as executor of his morhter's will, and at Montargis in 1289, where he made his own will.  AMAURY DE MONTFORT was living Feb. 1300/1.  He left a will dated 1289.
