0 @N6000000000337384219@ NOTE Born: 25 MAR 1133, Le Mans, Anjou
1 CONT Acceded: 19 DEC 1154, Westminster Abbey, London, England
1 CONT Died: 6 JUL 1189, Chinon Castle, France
1 CONT Interred: Fontevraud Abbey, France
1 CONT Notes:
1 CONT Source:  University of Hull, Directory of Royal Genealogical Data
1 CONT http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/public/genealogy/royal/
1 CONT
1 CONT Reigned 1154-1189. He ruled an empire that stretched from the Tweed to the
1 CONT Pyrenees. In spite of frequent hostitilties with the French King his own
1 CONT family and rebellious Barons (culminating in the great revolt of 1173-74) and
1 CONT his quarrel with Thomas Becket, Henry maintained control over his possessions
1 CONT until shortly before his death. His judicial and administrative reforms which
1 CONT increased Royal control and influence at the expense of the Barons were of
1 CONT great constitutional importance. Introduced trial by Jury. Duke of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Plantagenet, Geoffrey V the Fair, Count of Anjou and Maine, b. 24 AUG 1113
1 CONT [AD note:  there were rumors than King Stephen was actually Henry's father - see Thomas Costain's book "The Conquering Family"]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mother: , Matilda the Empress, Queen of England, b. ABT 1103/04
1 CONT
1 CONT Married 18 MAY 1152, Bordeaux Cathedral, Bordeaux, France to , Eleanor of Aquitaine, Duchess of Aquitaine - children of this marriage:
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 1: , William, Count of Poitiers, b. 17 AUG 1152
1 CONT Child 2: , Henry the Young King, King of England, b. 28 FEB 1155
1 CONT Child 3: , Matilda (Maud), b. JUN 1156
1 CONT Child 4: , Richard I Coeur de Lion, King of England, b. 8 SEP 1157
1 CONT Child 5: Plantagenet, Geoffrey II of Bretagne, Duke of Brittany, b. 23 SEP 1158
1 CONT Child 6: Plantagenet, Eleanor, b. 13 OCT 1162
1 CONT Child 7: Plantagenet, Joan, b. OCT 1165
1 CONT Child 8: , John I Lackland, King of England, b. 24 DEC 1167
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Gary Boyd Roberts, "The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the American Colonies or the United States, Who Were Themselves Notable or Left Descendants Notable in American History" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing 
1 CONC Co, Inc., 2001) pp. 350-351
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 3
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #4:  Douglas Richardson, "Plantagenet Ancestry:  A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families" (Baltimore, Maryland:  Genealogical Publishing Company, Inc., 2004), pp. 108-109
0 @N6000000000437071489@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> II, Of England Ruled 1154-1189 Thefirst of the ANGEVIN Kings. By marrying Eleanor, Duchess of Aquitaine in 1152, he acquired vast lands in France. His policy of establishing royal authori
1 CONC tyin England led to Thomas A. Becket's murder.
1 CONT
1 CONT first monarch of the house ofAnjou, or Plantagenet, an important administrative reformer, who was one of the most powerful European rulers of his time. --------------- Henry II., Kingof England, first of the Plantagenet line, was 
1 CONC the eldest son of Geoffrey, Earlof Anjou, and his wife, the ex-Empress Maud <emperor.html>, daughter of HenryI., and was born at Mans, in March, 1133. He received his education in England,under the care of his uncle Robert, Earl o
1 CONC f Gloucester. On the death of his father, in 1151, he succeeded to the earldom of Anjou, Touraine, and Maine, and in the following year, by his marriage with Eleanor of Aquitaine, the divorced wife of Louis VII. of France, he beca
1 CONC me possessor of the duchy of Aquitaine or Guienne. The same year he invaded England, but a treaty was concluded, in 1153,by which it was agreed that he should succeed to the throne of England on the death of Stephen <kingiops.html
1 CONC >. This event took place in October, 1154, and Henry was crowned without opposition at Westminster, in December. His first measures were directed to the redress of the disorders and anarchy which had prevailed in the reign of Step
1 CONC hen. He seized and destroyed most of the baronial castles; dismissed the foreign troops; renewed the charter granted by Henry I. ; andresumed most of the lands which had been alienated from the crown by Stephen. On the death of hi
1 CONC s brother Geoffrey he claimed and got possession of Nantes,and was thus master of the whole western coast of France. His attempt on Toulouse, in 1159, involved him in a war with the King of France, which was only terminated two ye
1 CONC ars later. In 1162 Thomas a Becket  was elected Archbishop of Canterbury, and the great struggle between the civil and ecclesiastical powers began, which resulted in the Constitutions of Clarendon, the exile and murder of Becket, 
1 CONC war with France, the king's penance at Becket's tomb, and the repeal of the Constitutions. In 1171 Henry invaded Ireland, and, under the authority of abull of Pope Adrian IV., which had been published in 1156, effected a conquesto
1 CONC f that island. The remaining years of his reign were embittered by the numerous revolts of his sons, instigated by their mother. Eleanor, whose jealousy was excited by the king's affection for Fair Rosamond, attempted to follow he
1 CONC r sons to the court of France, but was seized and imprisoned during Henry's life. The King of Scotland, who supported the rebellion of the young princes, was taken prisoner at Alnwick, in 1174, but was released after a few months,
1 CONC  on doing homage to Henry. A formal reconciliation with the princes took place, but was followed by a fresh revolt and civil war. Prince Henry, who, as heir-apparent, had been crowned in 1170, died in France, in 1183. Geoffrey was
1 CONC  killed at a tournament, two years later; and John joined his brother Richard in a new rebellionagainst their father, in which they were aided by Philip Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The oldking was prostrated by sickness, and the revolt of his youngest son John was the last and fatal blow from which he could not recover. He died at Chinon, July6, 1189, and was buried at Foutevraud. Notwithstanding the conflic
1 CONC ting estimates of the character and measures of Henry II., viewed as the champion of state supremacy, it is evident that he was a man of powerful intellect superior education, great energy, activity, and decisiveness, and also of 
1 CONC impetuous passions.Ruling almost despotically, he greatly diminished the power of the nobles, andthus relieved the people of their intolerable tyranny. Good order and just administration of the laws were established and the practi
1 CONC ce of holding the assizeswas introduced. He revived the trial by jury in
