0 @N6000000001503906424@ NOTE Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester and Count of Meulan (1049 – June 5, 1118) was a powerful English and French nobleman, revered as one of the wisest men of his age. Chroniclers speak highly of his eloquence, his learning, 
1 CONC and three kings of England valued his counsel.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Roger de Beaumont and Adeline of Meulan, daughter of Waleran III, Count de Meulan, and an older brother of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick. He accompanied William the Conqueror to England in 1066, w
1 CONC here his service earned him more than 91 lordships and manors. When his mother died in 1081, Robert inherited the title of Count of Meulan in Normandy, also the title of Viscount Ivry and Lord of Norton. He did homage to Philip I 
1 CONC of France for these estates and sat as French Peer in the Parliament held at Poissy.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the Battle of Hastings Robert was appointed leader of the infantry on the right wing of the army.
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his brother Henry were members of the Royal hunting party in the New Forest, when William Rufus received his mysterious death wound, 2 August 1100. He then pledged alligience to William Rufus' brother, Henry I of England, w
1 CONC ho created him Earl of Leicester in 1107.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of William Rufus, William, Count of Evreux and Ralph de Conches made an incursion into Robert's Norman estates, on the pretence that they had suffered injury through some advice that Robert had given to the King; thei
1 CONC r raid was very successful for they collected a vast booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Henry of Huntingdon, Robert died of shame after "a certain earl carried off the lady he had espoused, either by some intrigue or by force and stratagem." His wife Isabella remarried in 1118 to William de Warenne, 2nd 
1 CONC Earl of Surrey.
0 @N6000000002187742175@ NOTE Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester and Count of Meulan (1050 – June 5, 1118) was a powerful English and French nobleman, revered as one of the wisest men of his age. Chroniclers speak highly of his eloquence, his learning, 
1 CONC and three kings of England valued his counsel.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Roger de Beaumont and Adeline of Meulan, daughter of Waleran III, Count de Meulan, and an older brother of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick. He accompanied William the Conqueror to England in 1066, w
1 CONC here his service earned him more than 91 lordships and manors. When his mother died in 1081, Robert inherited the title of Count of Meulan in Normandy, also the title of Viscount Ivry and Lord of Norton. He did homage to Philip I 
1 CONC of France for these estates and sat as French Peer in the Parliament held at Poissy.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the Battle of Hastings Robert was appointed leader of the infantry on the right wing of the army.
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his brother Henry were members of the Royal hunting party in the New Forest, when William Rufus received his mysterious death wound, 2 August 1100. He then pledged alligience to William Rufus' brother, Henry I of England, w
1 CONC ho created him Earl of Leicester in 1107.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of William Rufus, William, Count of Evreux and Ralph de Conches made an incursion into Robert's Norman estates, on the pretence that they had suffered injury through some advice that Robert had given to the King; thei
1 CONC r raid was aery successful for they collected a vxst booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Henry of Huntingdon, Robert died of shame after "a certain earl carried off the lady he had espoused, either by some intrigue or by force and stratagem." His wife Isabella remarried in 1118 to William de Warenne, 2nd 
1 CONC Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT In 1096 he married Elizabeth de Vermandois, daughter of Hugh Magnus and a scion of the French royal family. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Emma de Beaumont (born 1102) 
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester (born 1104) 
1 CONT Waleran IV de Beaumont, Count of Meulan (born 1104) 
1 CONT Hugh de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Bedford (born c. 1106) 
1 CONT Adeline de Beaumont, married two times: 
1 CONT Hugh IV of Montfort-sur-Risle; 
1 CONT Richard de Granville of Bideford (d. 1147) 
1 CONT Aubree de Beaumont, married Hugh II of Château-neuf-Thimerais. 
1 CONT Matilda de Beaumont, married William Lovel. 
1 CONT Isabel de Beaumont, a mistress of King Henry I of England. Married two times: 
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 1st Earl of Pembroke; 
1 CONT Herve de Montmorency, Constable of Ireland 
1 CONT Agnes de Beaumont, married Guillaume, Sire de Say. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxfor
0 @N6000000001783291916@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Robert became the 1st Earl of Leicester, he was never styled thus. He was already the Count of Meulan in the French Vexin; and not expected that a person who was a great Count would change his style for a new dignity. He 
1 CONC was also Seigneur of Beaumont, Pont-Audemer, Brionne and Vatteville in Normandy. See Complete Peerage,
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was very young, probably about 20, when he accompanied Duke William to England where he was most certainly present at the Battle of Hastings. In 1081, he inherited from his mother's family the comté of Meulan and was therea
1 CONC fter referred to as "Count of Meulan". Robert was one of the chief lay ministers of William (II) Rufus, King of England from 1087 to 1100, with whom he sided against Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy. On the death of William Rufus
1 CONC  he became one of the chief advisors of King Henry (I) Beauclerc.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1110 Robert was beseiged at Meulan by King Louis (VI), aka "Louis the Fat", King of France, who took the castle by storm. In the following year, he retaliated by plundering Paris. He destroyed the bridges over the Seine, thus p
1 CONC reventing Louis the Fat from coming to the relief of the city while enabling himself to make a safe retreat with his booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont married in 1096 to Isabel de Crépi of Vermandois, daughter of Hugh "the Great" de Crépi, Count of Vermandois and his wife Adelaide de Vermandois. Robert and Isabel had the following children:
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont (1104 - 1166, twin brother of Robert de Beaumont), Count of Meulan and Earl of Worcester who married circa 1141 to Agnes de Montfort, a daughter of Amauri de Montfort, Count of Évreux.  They had a son:
1 CONT Sir Robert de Beaumont (d 1207), Count of Meulan.
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont
1 CONT Hugh de Beaumont, said to have been created Earl of Bedford.
1 CONT Adeline de Beaumont, who married Hugh IV, Seigneur of Montfort-sur-Risle.
1 CONT Aubrey de Beaumont, who married Hugh II, Seigneur of Chateauneuf-en-Thimerais.
1 CONT Maud de Beaumont, who married William Louvel, Seigneur of Ivri and Brevel.
1 CONT Isabel (or Elizabeth) de Beaumont who first "had an affair" with King Henry I of England and later married Gilbert FitzGilbert de Clare.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/beaumont.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Additional Source Information
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also Seigneur of Beaumont, Pont-Audemer, Brionne, and Vatteville, in Normandy upon succession to this father. He accompanied Duke William to England, and distinguished himself at the battle of Hastings, whereupon he receive
1 CONC d large grants of lands in Warwickshire, with smaller holdings in Leicester, Northants, and Wiltshire. In 1080, he witnessed a foundation charter as Robert de Bellomonte, and the following year he inherited from his mother's famil
1 CONC y, the Comte of Meulan. After the Conqueror's death, Robert was an adherent of William Rufus, in whose court he was in high favor. With his holdings in Normandy and vast possessions in Warwick and Leicester, he was one of the most
1 CONC  powerful vassals of the Crown. When William Rufus invaded the French Vexin in 1097, he received his troops in his fortresses from the Count of Meulan. After Rufus' death, Robert became of the chief advisors of Henry I, and upon t
1 CONC he death, on Crusade, of Ives de Grandmesnil, Robert retained his very extensive estates, which Ives had mortgaged to him around 1102. He was present with the King's army at Tinchebrai 28 Sep 1106, and in 1110 he was besieged at M
1 CONC eulan by Louis VI, who took the castle by storm, whereupon the following year Robert retaliated by plundering Paris. He was buried at the Abbey of Preux with his ancestors. While he is said to have been Earl of Leicester, there is
1 CONC  disagreement that he did, in fact, acquire the earldom.
1 CONT
1 CONT First Earl of Leicester, documented companion of William the Conqueror at Hastings, 1066.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Meulan, 1st Earl of Leicester was born circa 1046. He was the son of Roger de Beaumont, Seigneur de Portaudemer and Adeline de Meulan. He married Elizabeth de Vermandois, daughter of Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et
1 CONC  de Valois and Aelis de Vermandois, Comtesse de Vermandois, in 1096.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Meulan, 1st Earl of Leicester gained the title of Comte de Meulan, in France. He gained the title of 1st Earl of Leicester.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Robert de Meulan, 1st Earl of Leicester
1 CONT Isabella de Beaumont
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Robert de Meulan, 1st Earl of Leicester and Elizabeth de Vermandois
1 CONT Hugh de Meulan, 1st and last Earl of Bedford
1 CONT Isabella of Meulan+ b. bt 1102 - 1107, d. a 1172
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont, 1st and last Earl of Worcester+ b. 1104, d. bt 9 Apr 1166 - 10 Apr 1166
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester+ b. 1104, d. 5 Apr 1168
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on 5 June 1118.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://thepeerage.com/p10774.htm#i107737>
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester and Count of Meulan (1049 - June 5, 1118) was a powerful English and French nobleman, revered as one of the wisest men of his age. Chroniclers speak highly of his eloquence, his learning, 
1 CONC and three kings of England valued his counsel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Invasion of England and Hastings
1 CONT
1 CONT He accompanied William the Conqueror to England in 1066, where his service earned him more than 91 lordships and manors. When his mother died in 1081, Robert inherited the title of Count of Meulan in Normandy, also the title of Vi
1 CONC scount Ivry and Lord of Norton. He did homage to Philip I of France for these estates and sat as French Peer in the Parliament held at Poissy.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the Battle of Hastings Robert was appointed leader of the infantry on the right wing of the army.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Rufus
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his brother Henry were members of the Royal hunting party in the New Forest, when William Rufus received his mysterious death wound, 2 August 1100. He then pledged alligience to William Rufus' brother, Henry I of England, w
1 CONC ho created him Earl of Leicester in 1107.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of William Rufus, William, Count of Evreux and Ralph de Conches made an incursion into Robert's Norman estates, on the pretence that they had suffered injury through some advice that Robert had given to the King; thei
1 CONC r raid was very successful for they collected a vast booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Henry of Huntingdon, Robert died of shame after "a certain earl carried off the lady he had espoused, either by some intrigue or by force and stratagem." His wife Isabella remarried in 1118 to William de Warenne, 2nd 
1 CONC Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_de_Beaumont,_1st_Earl_of_Leicester>
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
