0 @N6000000002319310824@ NOTE Reigned 1199-1216. Signed Magna Carta in 1215 at Runnymede.
1 CONT      His reign saw renewal of war with Phillip II Augustus of France to
1 CONT whom he has
1 CONT      lost several continental possesions including Normandy by 1205. He
1 CONT came into
1 CONT      conflict with his Barons and was forced to Sign the Magna Carta.
1 CONT His later
1 CONT      repudiation of the charter led to the first barons war 1215-17
1 CONT during which
1 CONT      John died. Burke says he was born in 1160.
1 CONT      King of Ireland 1177, Count of Mortain 1189, Earl of Gloucester.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT JOHN OF ENGLAND  (1167-1216). Vicious, shameless, and ungrateful, King
1 CONT John has been called the worst king ever to rule England. Yet the very
1 CONT excesses of his reign proved positive in that they provoked such a
1 CONT violent reaction that his subjects revolted and forced him to put his
1 CONT seal on the Magna Carta. This document became the safeguard of English
1 CONT liberty. John's nickname was Lackland because at first he owned no
1 CONT land. Later his father, King Henry II, gave him castles, lands, and
1 CONT revenues in both England and France. John plotted against his father,
1 CONT however, and the discovery of this conspiracy was a factor in the
1 CONT king's death. John's brother, Richard the Lion-Hearted, became king and
1 CONT added to John's possessions (see Richard, Kings of England). While
1 CONT Richard was absent from England on the Third Crusade, John conspired
1 CONT against him also.
1 CONT    When Richard died in 1199, the barons selected John to be their
1 CONT king. This denied the royal claim of Arthur, son of another brother,
1 CONT Geoffrey. Two French provinces fought for young Arthur, but the boy
1 CONT fell into the hands of John and died soon after. During the war John
1 CONT lost all his French possessions except Aquitaine. John then quarreled
1 CONT with Pope Innocent III about the appointment of Stephen Langton as
1 CONT archbishop of Canterbury. John was excommunicated, and England was
1 CONT forbidden all religious services except baptism and extreme unction.
1 CONT The growing discontent of his subjects finally forced John to recognize
1 CONT the new archbishop.
1 CONT    When John went to France seeking to regain his lands in Normandy,
1 CONT the barons marched against the king and demanded a charter of
1 CONT liberties. All but a handful of followers deserted John. He was forced
1 CONT to meet the barons at Runnymede on June 15, 1215, and to sign the Great
1 CONT Charter (see Magna Carta).
1 CONT    John had no intention of supporting the charter, however. He
1 CONT recruited a new army and destroyed the estates of the barons. The
1 CONT barons then offered the English crown to Louis, a French prince. In the
1 CONT midst of a war for the throne, John died of a fever. The task of
1 CONT restoring the torn kingdom fell to his nine-year-old son, Henry III
1 CONT (see Henry, Kings of England).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Acceded: 27 MAY 1199, Westminster Abbey, London, England
