0 @N6000000002244407016@ NOTE Best known for signing the Magna Carta, at the insistence of the English
1 CONT Barons; the youngest son of Henry II, along with Richard I, the Lion Hearted.
1 CONT John was from the house of Anjou, or Plantagenet.
0 @N6000000002248112161@ NOTE Reigned 1199-1216. Signed Magna Carta in 1215 at Runnymede.
1 CONT His reign saw renewal of war with Phillip II Augustus of France to whom he has
1 CONT lost several continental possesions including Normandy by 1205. He came into
1 CONT conflict with his Barons and was forced to Sign the Magna Carta. His later
1 CONT repudiation of the charter led to the first barons war 1215-17 during which
1 CONT John died. Burke says he was born in 1160.
1 CONT King of Ireland 1177, Count of Mortain 1189, Earl of Gloucester.---------------
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========================
1 CONT
1 CONT  JOHN (r. 1199-1216)   John was an able administrator interested in law and government but he neither trusted others nor was trusted by them. Heavy taxation, disputes with the Church (John was excommunicated by the Pope in 1209) a
1 CONC nd unsuccessful attempts to recover his French possessions made him unpopular. Many of his barons rebelled and in June 1215 they forced the King to sign a peace treaty accepting their reforms.  This treaty, later known as Magna Ca
1 CONC rta, limited royal powers, defined feudal obligations between the King and the barons, and guaranteed a number of rights. The most influential clauses concerned the freedom of the Church; the redress of grievances of owners and te
1 CONC nants of land; the need to consult the Great Council of the Realm so as to prevent unjust taxation; mercantile and trading relationships; regulation of the machinery of justice so that justice be denied to no one; and the requirem
1 CONC ent to control the behaviour of royal officials. The most important clauses established the basis of habeas corpus ('you have the body'), i.e. that no one shall be imprisoned except by due process of law, and that 'to no one will 
1 CONC we sell, to no one will we refuse or delay right or justice'.  The Charter also established a council of barons who were to ensure that the Sovereign observed the Charter, with the right to wage war on him if he did not. Magna Car
1 CONC ta was the first formal document insisting that the Sovereign was as much under the rule of law as his people, and that the rights of individuals were to be upheld even against the wishes of the sovereign. As a source of fundament
1 CONC al constitutional principles, Magna Carta came to be seen as an important definition of aspects of English law, and in later centuries as the basis of the liberties of the English people.  As a peace treaty Magna Carta was a failu
1 CONC re and the rebels invited Louis of France to become their king. When John died in 1216 England was in the grip of civil war.	
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: http://www.royal.gov.uk/output/Page64.asp
0 @N6000000003122792422@ NOTE Ruled England from 1199-1216
0 @N6000000003051248150@ NOTE John was the youngest son of Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitane. His parents drifted apart after his birth, and his youth was divided between his eldest brother's house where he learned the art of knighthood, andthe house of his fat
1 CONC her's justiciar, Ranulf Glanvil, where he learned the business of government.
1 CONT As the fourth child, inherited lands were not available to him, giving rise to his nickname, Lackland.
1 CONT His first marriage, to Isabel of Gloucester, lasted but ten years and was fruitless; Isabella of Angouleme, his second wife, bore him two sons. He also had an illegitimate daughter, also named Joan, who married Lywelyn the Great, 
1 CONC Ruler of All Wales, from which the Tudor line of monarchs was descended.
1 CONT The Angevin family feuds left quite a mark on John - he proved his betrayal to both his father and his brother Richard . He and Richard clashed in 1184 when the elder refused to turn Aquitane over to theyounger brother, as dictate
1 CONC d by Henry II. The following year Henry sent
1 CONT John to rule Ireland, but John alienated the native Irish and the transplanted Anglo-Normans who emigrated to carve out new lordships for themselves; the experiment was a total failure, and John returned home within six months.
1 CONT Richard, after acceding to the throne in 1189, gave John vast estates to appease his younger brother, but to no avail. He tried to overthrow Richard's administrators during the German captivity, but failed. He conspired with Phili
1 CONC p II in another attempt, which again failed. Upon Richard's release in 1194, John was forced to sue for pardon, and spent the next five years in his brother's shadow, staying out of trouble long enough to be named heir to the crow
1 CONC n.
1 CONT John's reign was full of trouble. A quarrel with the Church resulted in England being placed under an interdict in 1207, with John excommunicated two years later. The dispute, centered around John's refusal to install the papal ca
1 CONC ndidate, Stephen Langdon, as Archbishop of Canterbury, and was not resolved until John surrendered to the wishes of Innocent III, one of the greatest medieval popes.
1 CONT A succession dispute with his nephew, Arthur of Brittany, ultimately resulted in the loss of French territories, as the king's French vassals preferred Arthur. By Spring 1205, John had crossed the Channel back into England as the 
1 CONC last of his French possessions fell out of his hands. From 1206 to the end of his reign, John was preoccupied with regaining these territories, levying a number of new taxes upon the landed barons to pay for his campaigns. This wo
1 CONC uld have been satisfactory had John been winning battles, but he was continually trounced by the French.
1 CONT The discontented rebel barons revolted, capturing London in May 1215. In June, at Runnymeade, John met with the barons and signed the Magna Carta, a feudal rights document stressing three points: 1) the Church was free to make its
1 CONC  own appointments, 2) no more than the normal amounts of money could be collected to run the government, unless the king's feudal tenants gave their consent, and 3) no freeman was to be punished except in concert with the common l
1 CONC aw. This document proved to be the forerunner of modern constitutions. John signed the document as a means of buying time, and failed to keep his word. The nobility called for French assistance, and John died in the midst of an in
1 CONC vasion.
1 CONT John was remembered in elegant fashion by Sir Richard Baker in A Chronicle of the Kings of England : "...his works of piety were very many... as far his actions, he neither came to the crown by justice, nor held it with any honour
1 CONC , nor left it peace." John's treacherous nature was the cause of the greatest loss of English continental territory until Hundred Years' War (1337-1453).
1 CONT
1 CONT [From "The British Monarchy", www.royal.gov.uk/history.angevin.htm]
1 CONT
1 CONT John and Magna Carta
1 CONT
1 CONT John (reigned 1199-1216) was an able administrator interested in law and government but he neither trusted others nor was trusted by them. Heavy taxation, disputes with the Church (John was excommunicated by the Pope in 1209) and 
1 CONC unsuccessful attempts to recover his French possessions made him unpopular. Many of his barons rebelled and in June 1215 they forced the King to sign a peace treaty accepting their reforms.
1 CONT This treaty, later known as Magna Carta, limited royal powers, defined feudal obligations between the King and the barons, and guaranteed a number of rights. The most influential clauses concerned the freedom of the Church; the re
1 CONC dress of grievances of owners and tenants of land; the need to consult the Great Council of the Realm so as to prevent unjust taxation; mercantile and trading relationships; regulation of the machinery of justice so that justice b
1 CONC e denied to no one; and the requirement to control the behaviour of royal officials. The most important clauses established the basis of habeas corpus ('you have the body'), i.e. that no one shall be imprisoned except by due proce
1 CONC ss of law, and that 'to no one will we sell, to no one will we refuse or delay right or justice'.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Charter also established a council of barons who were to ensure that the Sovereign observed the Charter, with the right to wage war on him if he did not. Magna Carta was the first formal document insisting that the Sovereign w
1 CONC as as much under the rule of law as his people; and that the rights of individuals were to be upheld even against the wishes of the sovereign. As a source of fundamental constitutional principles, Magna Carta came to be seen as an
1 CONC  important definition of aspects of English law, and in later centuries as the basis of the liberties of the English people.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a peace treaty Magna Carta was a failure and the rebels invited Louis of France to become their king. When John died in 1216 England was in the grip of civil war.
