1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John 
1 CONT By the Grace of God, King of England, Lord of Ireland, Duke of Normandy and Aquitaine and Count of Anjou 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign April 6, 1199 – October 18/19, 1216 
1 CONT Coronation May 27, 1199 
1 CONT Born December 24, 1167 
1 CONT  Beaumont Palace, Oxford 
1 CONT Died October 18 / 19, 1216 
1 CONT  Newark 
1 CONT Buried Worcester Cathedral 
1 CONT Predecessor Richard I 
1 CONT Successor Henry III 
1 CONT Consort Isabella of Angouleme
1 CONT (c. 1187–1246) 
1 CONT Issue Henry III (1207–1272)
1 CONT Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall
1 CONT (1209–1272)
1 CONT Joan of England (1210–1238)
1 CONT Isabella of England (1214–1241)
1 CONT Eleanor of England (1215–1275) 
1 CONT Royal House Plantagenet 
1 CONT Father Henry II (1133–1189) 
1 CONT Mother Eleanor of Aquitaine (1124–1204) 
1 CONT
1 CONT John (French: Jean) (December 24, c. 1166 – October 18/19, 1216) reigned as King of England from April 6, 1199, until his death. He succeeded to the throne as the younger brother of King Richard I (known in later times as "Richard
1 CONC  the Lionheart"). John acquired the nicknames of "Lackland" (Sans Terre in French) for his lack of an inheritance as the youngest son and for his loss of territory to France, and of "Soft-sword" for his alleged military ineptitude
1 CONC . He was a Plantagenet or Angevin King.
1 CONT
