0 @N6000000002187747938@ NOTE Joan, Lady of Wales
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan, Lady of Wales, or Joan of England (1191 - Feb. 2nd 1237) was the wife of Llywelyn the Great, Prince of Gwynedd and effective ruler of most of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was an illegitimate daughter of King John of England and a woman named Clemence. She should not be confused with her legitimate half-sister Joan, Queen Consort of Scotland. Little is known about her early life; she was eviden
1 CONC tly born before her father, King John of England, married his first wife in 1189. Her mother's name is known only from Joan's obituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she is mysteriously called "Regina Clementina" (Queen Clemence)
1 CONC . Joan seems to have spent her childhood in France, as King John had her brought to England from Normandy for her wedding in December 1203.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great between December 1203 and October 1204. She and Llywelyn had at least three children together:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwladus Ddu (1206-1251), married (1) Reginald de Braose and (2) Ralph de Mortimer 
1 CONT Elen ferch Llywelyn (1207-1253), married (1) John the Scot, Earl of Chester and (2) Robert de Quincy 
1 CONT Dafydd ap Llywelyn (1208-1246) married Isabella de Broase 
1 CONT Some of Llywelyn's other recorded children may also have been Joan's:
1 CONT
1 CONT Susanna, who was sent to England as a hostage in 1228 
1 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III, declaring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had been unmarried at the time of her birth, but without giving her a claim to the English throne. In 1230, 
1 CONC Joan was alleged to have committed adultery with William de Braose, Lord of Abergavenny, a relation by marriage who was Llywelyn's nominal prisoner at the time. Some sources say that the couple were discovered together in Llywelyn
1 CONC 's bedchamber. William de Braose was hanged, and Joan herself was imprisoned for some time before Llywelyn accepted her back as his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but she was sometimes known as "Lady of Wales". Llywelyn's great grief at her death is recorded. She was buried at the priory of Llanfaes near Beaumaris, and her stone coffin can be seen in
1 CONC  Beaumaris parish church.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fiction
1 CONT This princess Joan and her affair with William de Braose was the subject of Saunders Lewis's Welsh verse play Siwan.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan appears as a main character in Sharon Kay Penman's historical novel Here Be Dragons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Rotuli Litterarum Clausarum in Turri Londinensi I, p. 12. 
1 CONT Luard, Henry. Annales Monastici 1, 1864 
1 CONT Tewkesbury Annals 
1 CONT Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur 
0 @N6000000001744991942@ NOTE Tracy Mallon <CarinaGray@AOL.COM> wrote in June 1997: "Here is the information that I have on Joan, natural daughter of John, King of England.
1 CONT Joan was born about 1191, daughter of John, King of England and an unknown
1 CONT mother.  From what I have found, there is some debate about who Joan's mother
1 CONT was.  Sharon Kay Penman, in the notes to "Here Be Dragons," says that all she
1 CONT could find out about Joan's mother in her research was her Christian name,
1 CONT Clemence.  The journal "The Plantagenet Connection" has published an
1 CONT ahnentafel of Elizabeth Plantagenet, wife of Henry VII, which gives Joan's
1 CONT mother's name as Constance (Clementia) of Brittany.  This is the same
1 CONT Constance who was married to Geoffrey Plantagenet, John's brother.
1 CONT Considering the well-known animosity between Constance and John and her
1 CONT attempts to press her own son Arthur's interests as far as the throne of
1 CONT England were concerned (and we all know how disastrously that turned out!), I
1 CONT really find the proposed relationship a little hard to swallow.  The
1 CONT Constance that history portrays does not strike me as one who John could
1 CONT easily seduce or coerce into bed.  Another source, "Magna Charta Barons" by
1 CONT Charles H. Browning, copyright 1969 (I'm unsure of the overall reliability of
1 CONT this source), gives Joan's mother as Agatha de Ferrers, daughter of Robert de
1 CONT Ferrers, fourth Earl of Derby {PW: This is the lineage that Ed Mann supports}.  This one seems kind of out in left field to me.  While I do believe the Clemence/Clementia name to be correct, I doubt that we will ever truly know ex
1 CONC actly who Joan's mother was (much like her uncle, William Longespee).
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan married Llywelyn in 1206, when she was about 15 years old.  Llywelyn,
1 CONT born in 1173 to Iorwerth Drwyndwn and Marared of Powys, was 18 years older
1 CONT that Joan and had previously been married.  I don't know anything about his
1 CONT first marriage except that previously mentioned ahnentafel of Elizabeth
1 CONT Plantagenet gives her name as Gwenliann of Brynffenigi.  Llywelyn also had a
1 CONT concubine, Tangwystl of Rhos, daughter of Llywarcg Goch ap Llywarch of Rhos
1 CONT and Tangwysl of Cwmmwd (also according to the ahnentafel).  Joan and
1 CONT Llywelyn, according to all except one of the sources that I have read,
1 CONT remained married until Joan died in 1237.  NOTE--The ahnentafel of Elizabeth
1 CONT Plantagenet states that Joan married as her second husband Alexander II, King
1 CONT of Scotland in 1221.  John also had a legitimate daughter, Joan, who was
1 CONT married to Alexander (yes, she was about 10 when the marriage took
1 CONT place--Henry III liked to marry his sisters off young--Eleanor married her
1 CONT first husband when she was nine).  I think the author of the ahnentafel (not
1 CONT to take anything away from the hard work that this person put into this
1 CONT enormous undertaking) got confused by the fact that both sisters named Joan
1 CONT died in the same year, 1237.  Joan (John's legitimate daughter by Isabella
1 CONT d'Angouleme) was born in 1211, I believe, when her older sister was twenty.
1 CONT
1 CONT One interesting story from Joan and Llywelyn's marriage:  about 1230/1231,
1 CONT Llywelyn returned home unexpectedly to find Joan alone in their bedroom with
1 CONT William de Braose.  This story is recounted in "Here Be Dragons" and one
1 CONT other "purely historical" source which I cannot recall the name of right now.
1 CONT  William was later hanged on Llywelyn's order.  NOTE--If this story took
1 CONT place in about 1230/1231, it would have been impossible for Joan to have
1 CONT divorced Llywelyn to marry Alexander II in 1221 (unless she subsequently
1 CONT divorced Alexander to remarry Llywelyn, which doesn't really make sense).
1 CONT
1 CONT Their children:
1 CONT If you thought there wasn't enough conflicting information in this story,
1 CONT here's some more.  Llywelyn is attributed with the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn (1196-1244)--he was the father of Llywelyn Fawr (the
1 CONT Last Prince of Wales) who married Eleanor de Montfort and Dafydd ap Gruffydd,
1 CONT traditionally held to be the first prisoner executed by drawing and
1 CONT quartering (later used on William Wallace)
1 CONT
1 CONT Gladys "the Black" (because of her dark coloring) (1194/8-1260)--she was
1 CONT married to Reginald de Braose first and then Roger Mortimer and was
1 CONT great-grandmother to the Roger Mortimer who was Queen Isabella's lover (wife
1 CONT of Edward II)
1 CONT
1 CONT Helen (1207-1253)--married first to John the Scot, Earl of Huntingdon, and
1 CONT then to Robert de Quincey "the younger" (there were two brothers named Robert
1 CONT in the family), son of Saire de Quincey, Magna Charta surety, and was the
1 CONT great-great grandmother of Joan, the Fair Maid of Kent, wife of Edward, the
1 CONT Black Prince
1 CONT
1 CONT Dafydd ap Llywelyn (1208-1246)--succeeded his father as Prince of Wales and
1 CONT was married to Isabella de Braose (daughter of the above-mentioned William!)
1 CONT
1 CONT Angarad
1 CONT
1 CONT Angharad (notice the similar spellings--the above is a female, this one is a
1 CONT male)
1 CONT
1 CONT Tegwared--he was mentioned briefly in the author's notes to "Here Be
1 CONT Dragons".  I mention him because Sharon Kay Penman mentions specifically that
1 CONT she found him in "Welsh Genealogies" by Peter C. Bartrum
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret (Marared in Welsh)--I cannot find any information about her in any
1 CONT of my genealogical sources, but her husband is given as Walter Clifford in Sharon Kay Penman's book "Falls the Shadow" (the sequel to "Here Be Dragons")
1 CONT
1 CONT Now here is the tricky part--attributing each child to a mother. Obviously,
1 CONT by the dates given above, Gruffydd and Gladys cannot be Joan's children. The
1 CONT Elizabeth Plantagenet ahnentafel attributes both of these children to
1 CONT Tangwystl (as does Sharon Kay Penman).  The ahnentafel attributes the
1 CONT following to Joan:  Helen, Margaret, Angharad, and Dafydd (two boys, two
1 CONT girls).  In "Here Be Dragons", Sharon Kay Penman attributes Helen and Dafydd
1 CONT to Joan; Gruffydd, Gladys, Margaret and Angarad (called Gwenllian in the
1 CONT book) to Tangwystl and Angharad and Tegwared to an unknown mother.  It would
1 CONT be easiest if we had birthdates on all the children, then we could at least
1 CONT figure out which children did not belong to Joan.  I've got a file I downloaded from AOL listing the descendents of William the Conqueror, which attributes Dafydd, Angharad and Gladys to Joan (no mention is made of the other child
1 CONC ren).  Obviously, Gladys being Joan's daughter impossible given Gladys' birth date (whether it is in 1194 or 1198).  So who knows?  The only children that I feel completely comfortable assigning to a particular mother are Gruffydd
1 CONC , Gladys, Helen and Dafydd.
1 CONT
1 CONT SOURCES:
1 CONT
1 CONT Penman, Sharon Kay.  "Here Be Dragons".  New York: Ballatine Books, June
1 CONT 1993.
1 CONT Penman, Sharon Kay.  "Falls The Shaow".  New York:  Ballatine Books, May
1 CONT 1989.
1 CONT Lewis, Marlyn (compiler for "The Plantagenet Connection").  "Ahnentafel for
1 CONT Elizabeth Plantagenet (Wife of Henry Tudor):  Generations 1-15".  Arvada, CO:
1 CONT  Heliotrope Communications, 1996.
1 CONT Wurts, John C.  "Magna Charta".  Copyright 1945.
1 CONT Browning, Charles H.  "Magna Charta Barons".  Copyright 1969.
1 CONT Adams, Arthur and Frederick Weis.  "Magna Charta Sureties".  Copyright 1955.
1 CONT
1 CONT While I make no claims as to the overall validity of the above works, most of
1 CONT the information I give above is found in more than one source, which makes me
1 CONT a little more likely to believe the information."
