0 @N6000000001578884781@ NOTE Earl of Cornwall (from shortly after 30 May 1227) and Court of Poitou (from before 18 Aug 1225, renounced about Dec 1243).
1 CONT Knighted 2 Feb 1225 by his brother Henry III;
1 CONT Commander in Chief of the Crusaders, 1240-1241;
1 CONT Joint Guardian of England with Queen Eleanor during the absence of Henry III in Gascony, Aug 1253-May 1254 and sole Guardian May-Dec 1254;
1 CONT . Fighting against the rebellious Barons, he was taken prisoner with Henry III at the battle of Lewes, 14 May 1264.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT 1209–72, second son of King John of England and brother of Henry III . In 1227, following an expedition to Gascony and Poitou, Richard forced Henry to grant him the land and wealth he regarded as his right, as well as the title of
1 CONC  earl of Cornwall. He improved his position further by his marriage (1231) to Isabella, daughter of William Marshall, 1st earl of Pembroke. He went on a crusade in 1240 and concluded (1241) a truce with the sultan of Egypt. On Hen
1 CONC ry’s expedition to Poitou in 1242, Richard was barely able to save his brother from complete military disaster. In the 1230s, Richard had often associated himself with the baronial opposition to Henry. However, after his marriage 
1 CONC (Isabella having died) to the queen’s sister in 1243, he became a faithful supporter of the king and his most sensible adviser. He financed the reform of the coinage in 1247, adding greatly to his already considerable wealth, and 
1 CONC acted as regent when Henry was out of the country. Richard refused (1252) Pope Innocent IV’s offer of the Sicilian crown (which Henry later accepted for his son Edmund), but in 1257 he had himself elected king of the Romans (i.e.,
1 CONC  emperor-elect of the Holy Roman Empire). Richard was crowned at Aachen and made three visits to Germany, but was never more than nominal ruler there. When the Barons’ War </65/ba/BaronsWa.html> broke out in earnest, Richard was o
1 CONC ne of Henry’s chief supporters. He was captured at the battle of Lewes (1264) and held prisoner until after the battle of Evesham (1265). In the settlement after the war he advised moderation against the rebels.  The Columbia Ency
1 CONC clopedia, Sixth Edition. 2001
1 CONT
1 CONT Barons’ War	
1 CONT
1 CONT in English history, war of 1263–67 between King Henry III  and his barons. In 1261, Henry III renounced the Provisions of Oxford  (1258) and the Provisions of Westminster (1259), which had vested considerable power in a council of
1 CONC  barons, and reasserted his right to appoint councilors. The barons led by Simon de Montfort , earl of Leicester, finally resorted to arms in 1263 and forced the king to reaffirm his adherence to the Provisions. In 1264 a decision
1 CONC  in favor of the crown by Louis IX of France as arbitrator led to a renewal of war, but Montfort defeated Henry’s forces in the battle of Lewes, and the king once again submitted to government by council. Early in 1265, Montfort s
1 CONC ummoned his famous representative Parliament  to strengthen his position, which was threatened by the possibility of an invasion by Henry’s adherents abroad. The invasion did not take place, but an uprising against Montfort of the
1 CONC  Welsh “Marchers” (Englishmen along the Welsh border) led to his defeat by the king’s son (later Edward I)  at Evesham. Montfort was killed in the battle, but some baronial resistance continued until 1267. The barons had failed to
1 CONC  establish their own control over the crown, but they had helped prepare the way for the constitutional developments of the reign of Edward I.	
1 CONT See R. F. Treharne, The Baronial Plan of Reform (1932, repr. 1972); F. M. Powicke, King Henry III and the Lord Edward (1947).	
1 CONT
0 @N6000000001876711148@ NOTE http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard,_1st_Earl_of_Cornwall
0 @N6000000002187747927@ NOTE Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT English Royalty 
1 CONT House of Plantagenet 
1 CONT
1 CONT John 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Henry III 
1 CONT    Richard, Earl of Cornwall 
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Scots 
1 CONT    Isabella, Holy Roman Empress 
1 CONT    Eleanor, Countess of Leicester 
1 CONT For the philosopher, see Richard Rufus of Cornwall 
1 CONT Richard of Cornwall (5 January 1209 – 2 April 1272) was Count of Poitou (bef. 1225), Earl of Cornwall (from 1227) and King of the (German) Holy Roman Empire (formally "King of the Romans") (from 1257).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born at Winchester Castle, the second son of King John of England and Isabella of Angouleme, and thus, the younger brother of King Henry III. Richard's revenues from Cornwall provided him with great wealth, and he became on
1 CONC e of the wealthiest men in Europe. Though he campaigned on King Henry's behalf in Poitou and Brittany, and served as Regent three times, relations were often strained between the brothers in the early years of Henry's reign. Richa
1 CONC rd rebelled against him three times, and had to be bought off with lavish gifts.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1231 he married Isabel Marshal, the widow of the Earl of Gloucester, much to the displeasure of his brother King Henry, who had been arranging a more advantageous match for Richard. In that same year he acquired Wallingford Cas
1 CONC tle, and spent much money on developing it. Isabel and Richard had four children, of whom only their son, Henry of Almain, survived to adulthood. When Isabel was on her deathbed in 1240, she asked to be buried next to her first hu
1 CONC sband at Tewkesbury, but Richard had her interred at Beaulieu Abbey instead. As a pious gesture, however, he sent her heart to Tewkesbury. Later that year Richard joined the Sixth Crusade and departed for the Holy Land. He fought 
1 CONC in no battles but managed to negotiate for the release of prisoners and the burials of Crusaders killed at a battle in Gaza in 1239. He also refortified Ascalon, which had been demolished by Saladin. On his return from the Holy La
1 CONC nd, Richard visited his sister Isabella, the empress of Frederick II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard opposed Simon de Montfort, and rose in rebellion in 1238 to protest against the marriage of his sister, Eleanor, to Simon. Once again he was placated with rich gifts, but in 1240 when he and Montfort joined the Crusade at 
1 CONC the same time, they made a point of not traveling together. On his return, Richard married Sanchia of Provence, the sister of his brother Henry's queen, Eleanor. This marriage tied him even more closely to the royal party.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's claims to Gascony and Poitou were never more than nominal, and in 1241 King Louis IX of France invested his own brother Alphonse with Poitou. Moreover, Richard and Henry's mother, Isabella of Angouleme, claimed to have b
1 CONC een insulted by the French king. They were encouraged to recover Poitou by their stepfather, Hugh X of Lusignan, but the expedition turned into a military fiasco after Lusignan betrayed them. The pope offered Richard the crown of 
1 CONC Sicily, but according to Matthew Paris he responded to the extortionate price by saying, "You might as well say, 'I will sell or give you the moon; go up and take it'." Instead, his brother King Henry purchased the kingdom for his
1 CONC  own son Edmund.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1257, Richard was elected by three German Electoral Princes known as the "English party" (Cologne, Mainz and Palatinate) as King of Germany. He had bought the electors' votes for the vast sum of 28,000 marks. He spent the next 
1 CONC few years attempting to persuade the pope to crown him; at last, in May 1257, Pope Alexander IV crowned Richard "King of the Romans" at Aachen. However, like his lordships in Gascony and Poitou, his title never held more than hono
1 CONC rary significance, and he made only four brief visits to Germany between 1257 and 1269.
1 CONT
1 CONT He joined King Henry in fighting against Simon de Montfort's rebels in the Second Barons' War (1264–1267). After the shattering royalist defeat at the Battle of Lewes, Richard took refuge in a windmill, was discovered, and impriso
1 CONC ned until September 1265.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 2, 1272, Richard died at Berkhamsted Castle in Hertfordshire. He was buried at Hayles Abbey, which he had founded.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Marriages 
1 CONT 2 Issue 
1 CONT 3 Media 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages
1 CONT He married three times:
1 CONT
1 CONT On 30 March 1231 to Isabel Marshal, widow of Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, and daughter of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke. She died in childbed 17 January 1240. 
1 CONT On 23 November 1243 to Sanchia of Provence, daughter of Raymond Berenger IV, count of Provence. She died 9 November 1261. 
1 CONT On 16 June 1269 to Beatrice of Falkenburg, daughter of Dietrich I, Count of Falconburg. There were no children. She was aged about sixteen to Richard's sixty, and was said to be one of the most beautiful women of her time. Beatric
1 CONC e died October 17, 1277 and was buried at Friars Minor in Oxford. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Issue
1 CONT Isabel bore him four children, all of whom died in the cradle, except Henry of Almain (1235–1271), Richard's heir apparent. Henry was the victim of the famous murder at Viterbo, when he was cut down while praying in a church by hi
1 CONC s cousins, Simon the younger de Montfort and Guy de Montfort, Count of Nola. Richard had two sons by Sanchia, Edmund, 2nd Earl of Cornwall (1249–1300) but he died childless, and Richard Cornwall (1252-1296) who married Joan Saint 
1 CONC Owen (1260-?) and had issue. He, however, died at the seige of Berwick-upon-Tweed in 1296.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard had the reputation of being a womanizer, and indeed his only descendants are found among his illegitimate children. His mistress, Joan de Valletort, was certainly the mother of one of his children. Their daughter Joan de C
1 CONC ornwall married Richard Champernowne. An illegitimate son, Philip de Cornwall, was a cleric in 1248. Another illegitimate son, Walter de Cornwall, was granted lands by his half-brother Edmund, and died in 1313.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Media
1 CONT Richard and his first wife, Isabel Marshall, appear as characters in Virginia Henley's historical novels, The Marriage Prize and The Dragon and the Jewel.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Denholm-Young, Noel. Richard of Cornwall, 1947 
1 CONT Tyerman, Christopher. England and the Crusades, 1095-1588, 19 
1 CONT Lewis, Frank. Beatrice of Falkenburg, the Third Wife of Richard of Cornwall, 1937 
