1 CONC  earl of Cornwall. He improved his position further by his marriage (1231) to Isabella, daughter of William Marshall, 1st earl of Pembroke. He went on a crusade in 1240 and concluded (1241) a truce with the sultan of Egypt. On Hen
1 CONC ry’s expedition to Poitou in 1242, Richard was barely able to save his brother from complete military disaster. In the 1230s, Richard had often associated himself with the baronial opposition to Henry. However, after his marriage 
1 CONC (Isabella having died) to the queen’s sister in 1243, he became a faithful supporter of the king and his most sensible adviser. He financed the reform of the coinage in 1247, adding greatly to his already considerable wealth, and 
1 CONC acted as regent when Henry was out of the country. Richard refused (1252) Pope Innocent IV’s offer of the Sicilian crown (which Henry later accepted for his son Edmund), but in 1257 he had himself elected king of the Romans (i.e.,
1 CONC  emperor-elect of the Holy Roman Empire). Richard was crowned at Aachen and made three visits to Germany, but was never more than nominal ruler there. When the Barons’ War </65/ba/BaronsWa.html> broke out in earnest, Richard was o
1 CONC ne of Henry’s chief supporters. He was captured at the battle of Lewes (1264) and held prisoner until after the battle of Evesham (1265). In the settlement after the war he advised moderation against the rebels.  The Columbia Ency
1 CONC clopedia, Sixth Edition. 2001
1 CONT
1 CONT Barons’ War	
1 CONT
1 CONT in English history, war of 1263–67 between King Henry III  and his barons. In 1261, Henry III renounced the Provisions of Oxford  (1258) and the Provisions of Westminster (1259), which had vested considerable power in a council of
1 CONC  barons, and reasserted his right to appoint councilors. The barons led by Simon de Montfort , earl of Leicester, finally resorted to arms in 1263 and forced the king to reaffirm his adherence to the Provisions. In 1264 a decision
1 CONC  in favor of the crown by Louis IX of France as arbitrator led to a renewal of war, but Montfort defeated Henry’s forces in the battle of Lewes, and the king once again submitted to government by council. Early in 1265, Montfort s
1 CONC ummoned his famous representative Parliament  to strengthen his position, which was threatened by the possibility of an invasion by Henry’s adherents abroad. The invasion did not take place, but an uprising against Montfort of the
1 CONC  Welsh “Marchers” (Englishmen along the Welsh border) led to his defeat by the king’s son (later Edward I)  at Evesham. Montfort was killed in the battle, but some baronial resistance continued until 1267. The barons had failed to
1 CONC  establish their own control over the crown, but they had helped prepare the way for the constitutional developments of the reign of Edward I.	
1 CONT See R. F. Treharne, The Baronial Plan of Reform (1932, repr. 1972); F. M. Powicke, King Henry III and the Lord Edward (1947).	
1 CONT
