0 @N6000000003658772943@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT BLANCHE DE BRIENNE, daughter and heiress.  She married by settlement dated Feb. 1266/7 WILLIAM (or GUILLAUME) DE FIENNES (or FENES), Knt., seigneur of Fiennes and Tingry (Pas-de-Calais), and, in England, of Wendover, Buckinghamshi
1 CONC re, Lambourne, Essex, Chokes and Gayton, Northamptonshire, Martock, Somerset, Carshalton and Clapham, Surrey, custodian of the county of Ponthieu, son and heir of Enguerrand (or Ingram) de Fiennes, seigneur of Fiennes (Pas de Cala
1 CONC is), Baron of Tingry and Ruminghen, and, in England, of Wendover, Chokes, Gayton, etc., by his 1st wife, _____, daughter of Jacques de Condé, seigneur de Condé, Bailleul, and Moriammez in Hainault.  They had two sons ... and four 
1 CONC daughters. ... Blanche was co-heiress to her mother, by which she inherited Loupelande in Maine.  he and his brother, Giles, joined the crusade to the Holy Land in 1270-1272 under Lord Edward.  In 1275 William pledged part of his 
1 CONC estates, including Martock, Somerset, to his kinswoman, Eleanor of Castile, wife of King Edward I, when, at his request, she agreed to pay £1000 to Humphrey de Bohun on the latter's marriage to his sister, Maud. ... He was summone
1 CONC d to serve against the Welsh in 1277 and 1282.  He held the custody of the county of Ponthieu in 1279.  In 1285 Blanche had the gift of 12 leafless oak stumps from Selwood Forest from the king for her fuel.  William spent many yea
1 CONC rs beyond the seas, yet was restored to his English lands during the truce of 1299, for which he acted as a hostage in the negotiations between England and France.  He was summoned to serve against the Scots in 1300.  Due to consi
1 CONC derable debts, he conveyed the manors of Clapham, Surrey to Thomas Romayn, citizen of London, Carshalton, Surrey to William de Ambesas, Knt., and Lambourne, Essex to Robert Burnell, Bishop of Bath and Wells.  SIR WILLIAM DE FIENNE
1 CONC S was slain with divers other magnates at the Battle of Courtrai 11 July 1302.
